"use strict";(self.webpackChunktma2_site=self.webpackChunktma2_site||[]).push([[4860],{6096(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-15-time-late-synthesis-slides.md","title":"Slides: the time-late track as a whole","description":"The illustrated companion to","date":"2026-11-15T20:30:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-019","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-019"}],"readingTime":1.54,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: the time-late track as a whole","date":"2026-11-15T20:30","tags":["slides","spec-019"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"The time-late track as a whole: what six studies established, and what they did not","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to\\n[the account of the time-late track](/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis): the standing\\nsystem, what it gained over the old one, what remains, the attempts to beat it, the last\\ndiagnostic, and three periods as tracks, good and bad.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-15-time-late-synthesis-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Gains**: the old system (C\u2033 read as a track) \xf7 the standing control, by kind of target\\n  and bearing error, on the test seeds of specs 017 and 018.\\n- **Remaining**: the standing control \xf7 a fit told the true manoeuvre history, on three\\n  test seeds. A reference, not a bound.\\n- **Challengers**: the standing control \xf7 each attempt to beat it, on Annex B and busier\\n  targets: the learned model\'s read-out and its own track (spec 016), the search told the\\n  true statistics (017, 018), the prior adapted to the period, declared and hedged (018).\\n- **Ladder**: development work on 130 used periods: the standing control \xf7 the search\\n  under the broadened prior taking on the true statistics a part at a time, and each part\\n  alone; orange ticks are the same ratio without its ten best periods.\\n- **Three periods as tracks**, chosen by a rule in the script: of the 20 stored shifted\\n  periods of spec 017\'s test, those where the standing control is best, median and worst\\n  against the old system.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\n```\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl_synthesis.py --out experiments/20261115-tl-synthesis/tables.md --fig-dir site/static/img/2026-11-15-time-late-synthesis-slides\\n```\\n\\nThe script reads only committed records (`experiments/20261114-tl4/`, `20261114-tl5/`,\\n`20261115-tl6/`, `20261115-tl7-dev/`); each figure\'s `.json` sidecar names the inputs\' digests and the commit.\\nThe data are test seeds 341, 344 and 347, 5-second bearings, generated in their CI runs."},{"id":"/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-15-time-late-synthesis.md","title":"The time-late track as a whole: what six studies established, and what they did not","description":"Between 11 and 15 November the project ran six studies on the time-late consumer: the","date":"2026-11-15T20:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-019","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-019"}],"readingTime":12.38,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"The time-late track as a whole: what six studies established, and what they did not","date":"2026-11-15T20:00","tags":["write-up","spec-019"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: the time-late track as a whole","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: one last diagnostic, and the time-late track written up as a whole","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-kickoff"}},"content":"Between 11 and 15 November the project ran six studies on the time-late consumer: the\\nanalyst who wants the target\'s whole track over a period after the fact, not the best\\nestimate now. Each has its own write-up and its own panel review. This is the account of\\nall six together: what is standing, what was shown, what was withdrawn, and what was never\\ntested. Figures are from the committed test records and their panel reviews; the table of\\nrecord is generated by a script. The illustrated companion is\\n[the slides post](/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-slides).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The question\\n\\nGiven every bearing of a period of 20 to 120 minutes, at 5-second intervals, say where the\\ntarget was, its course and its speed at every moment, and when it manoeuvred. Nothing was\\ntrained except in one study, which says so. Every result is on simulated data from one\\ngenerator.\\n\\n## What is standing\\n\\n**The classical control** (study L\'s `ctl`, declared):\\n\\n1. wild bearings are edited out;\\n2. the bearing sd is enlarged when the bearings\' second differences show the errors are\\n   correlated in time;\\n3. a search over manoeuvre histories, each fitted with an arc model (a turn at a constant\\n   rate, a speed change at a constant rate) and scored by its evidence under Annex B\'s\\n   manoeuvre statistics and under a broadened set, pooled;\\n4. a manoeuvre is declared only when it is worth an evidence margin, with a stated 1%\\n   chance that the target never manoeuvres.\\n\\nIt replaced \\"C\u2033 read as a track\\": the causal segmenter\'s answer at the end of the period,\\nread backwards.\\n\\n**Its known faults**, which a reader should have before trusting it:\\n\\n- it calls a one-manoeuvre target quiet more often than the search it grew from: 0.29\\n  against 0.05 with correlated errors, 0.17 against 0.04 with 5% wild bearings;\\n- on busier targets it misses about half the manoeuvres;\\n- it does nothing under a slowly varying bias;\\n- its editing and its enlarged sd know the simulator\'s error family (wild bearings at 10\\n  sd are edited at 4), and its correlation estimate fires in 8% of clean periods;\\n- the evidence it ranks by has an unrepaired fault at the edges of the prior\'s support;\\n- the prior it uses is the simulator\'s own for Annex B targets.\\n\\n## What was shown\\n\\nAll ratios are paired per period, in period median range error, on fresh test seeds,\\nunless a line says otherwise.\\n\\n**The search with the arc model and Annex B\'s statistics roughly halves the old system\'s\\nerror.** C\u2033 \xf7 search is 1.7 and 2.1 on Annex B targets in the gate and its follow-on\\n(specs 013 and 014), and 2.05 and 2.24 for the repaired search on two later seeds; on\\nbusier targets 1.5 to 1.65; 3.1 with 2% of bearings wild. The panels narrowed what that\\nmeans. It is mostly smoothing with the right motion model and prior, not finding the right\\nhistory: the search wins where its history is wrong too, it is worse than C\u2033 in about 30%\\nof periods, and refitting C\u2033\'s own manoeuvres with the arc model and prior obtains between\\na third and a half of the gain (specs 014 and 015). A search under a flat prior obtains\\nabout four fifths of it: Annex B\'s statistics themselves are worth about a fifth.\\n\\n**Editing and an honest bearing sd remove most of the damage from bad bearings.** Search \xf7\\ncontrol: 1.68 with 2% wild bearings, 2.44 with 5%, 1.33 with correlated errors, 1.70 on\\ntargets that hold course (false manoeuvres in 8% of periods against 49%). The outlier and\\ncorrelated-error gains replicate over a tuning, a validation and a test seed. On clean\\nAnnex B targets the control is level with the search or slightly ahead (0.96 on\\nvalidation; 1.05 [0.99, 1.12] and 1.09 [1.02, 1.18] on two test seeds; the median period\\nis level), and under a slowly varying bias it does nothing (1.00).\\n\\n**Against the old system the standing control is 2.1 to 2.4 times better on Annex B\\ntargets, 1.6 to 1.7 on busier targets, about 5 with wild bearings, 2.9 with correlated\\nerrors, and level on targets that hold course** (seeds 344 and 347).\\n\\n**What remains, against a fit told the true manoeuvre history, depends on how that fit is\\nmade.** The undeclared control against a reference whose times are free unless it drifts:\\n1.3 on Annex B targets, 1.2 on targets with larger, rarer manoeuvres, 1.8 to 2.1 on busier\\ntargets (three seeds). The declared control against a reference with its times held: 1.6,\\n1.5 and 2.3 to 2.7 (two seeds). These references are told true times and are not bounds:\\nthe control beats them in a quarter of periods on busier targets and in up to nearly a\\nhalf elsewhere.\\n\\n**On busier targets part of the remainder cannot be closed, and how much depends on where\\nthe line is drawn.** Over a third of their manoeuvres add less than 20 to the fit\'s \u03c7\xb2 when\\nleft out singly (16% on Annex B targets); that single-omission measure is not additive and\\noverstates what is hidden. A reference told only the manoeuvres above the line leaves the\\ncontrol 0.65, 0.44 or 0.14 of its gap to close at lines of 10, 20 and 40; more in course\\nand speed than in range. The closable part sits in the under-a-third of periods where the\\ncontrol misses a manoeuvre the bearings show (spec 017).\\n\\n**Beyond range.** The first candidate history is the true one in about 0.64 of Annex B\\nperiods. The candidates\' stated probabilities are honest on average on Annex B targets\\nand carry only a rank on busier ones. In course and speed the control\'s gain over\\nthe old system is of the same size as in range; for the challengers below, course and\\nspeed are given where they differ.\\n\\n## What was tried against the control\\n\\n| Challenger | Annex B targets | Busier targets | Study |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Learned whole-period model, its declared read-out (three seeds, 12.5 million periods each) | 0.81 to 0.82 | 0.80 to 0.82 | 016 |\\n| The same model\'s own track | 0.71 | 1.01 in range; 0.79 course, 0.77 speed | 016 |\\n| Search told the targets\' true statistics | 0.96, 0.98 | 1.17, 1.10; 1.15 [1.07, 1.23] pooled over a tuning and two test seeds | 017, 018 |\\n| A prior chosen from 30 by the period\'s evidence | 0.93; 0.91 [0.84, 0.98] pooled over three seeds | 1.00 [0.92, 1.08] | 018 |\\n| Hedged mean of candidate tracks | 0.92 | 1.00 in range, about 0.9 in course | 018 |\\n\\nControl \xf7 challenger; above 1 the challenger is better.\\n\\n- **The learned model\'s declared read-out lost in the conditions it was trained on**, and\\n  won only on targets that hold course (1.06). The panel showed the registered comparison\\n  measured its untuned manoeuvre detector: the median period is a tie, and the loss sits\\n  where the model declares fewer manoeuvres. Its own track leans on what targets do in\\n  general, winning where the bearings say little and losing by three where they say much.\\n  \\"This model with this read-out stops\\"; a learned proposer inside the search was not\\n  tested.\\n- **The right prior is worth about a tenth to a sixth on busier targets, and not in the\\n  typical period.** On seed 347 it is better than the control in 53% of periods, over\\n  twice as good in 20% and over twice as bad in 13%: two heavy tails that nearly cancel.\\n  (Spec 018\'s record says ten periods of 355 carry 87% of the gain. That statistic drops\\n  only the best periods and reads any widely spread ratio as a tail; trimmed at both ends\\n  the ratio is unchanged at 1.10.)\\n- **This evidence, on one period, does not choose among these 30 priors.** It does not\\n  rank them by accuracy; choosing by groups of periods does no better. The family\'s cost\\n  on Annex B targets is the design\'s: it lacked Annex B\'s own prior, which the control\\n  has, and its grid was set knowing the test targets\' statistics. That a prior cannot be\\n  adapted from one period in general was not shown.\\n- **A hedged mean is not better than a declared history**, and its spread could not be\\n  stated by one factor.\\n\\n## The last diagnostic\\n\\nSpec 018\'s panel left one thing confounded: the family of priors only refitted a shared\\nset of candidate histories, and it lacked parts of the true statistics. One run on 130\\nperiods already used (seed 347; development work, not a result; nothing in it is separated\\nfrom 1 in range) looks at both.\\n\\n- **Refitting was not the handicap.** The family member nearest the truth, given a search\\n  of its own, is 1.03 [0.91, 1.19] of the control; refitting shared candidates on the same\\n  periods it was 1.03 [0.91, 1.19].\\n- **The gain builds along the ladder, legs first.** Taking on the truth\'s legs, turn\\n  sizes, turn rates, speed changes and kinds of manoeuvre in that order gives 0.98, 1.05,\\n  1.06, 1.12, 1.10 and 1.11 [0.94, 1.32]. The first three parts reach the whole prior\'s\\n  figure in range. The other four parts each alone on the broadened prior, without the\\n  legs, do nothing (0.92 to 1.00). One order only; a direction, not a decomposition.\\n- **Neither typical nor a tail.** The true prior is better than the control in 55% of\\n  these periods, over twice as good in a quarter and over twice as bad in an eighth;\\n  trimmed of its ten best and ten worst periods the ratio is 1.14.\\n- **What the right prior changes steadily is the count**: manoeuvres missed fall from 0.52\\n  to 0.36 down the ladder, with false ones near a half a period throughout. In course and\\n  speed it is further ahead (1.21 and 1.24, not quite separated).\\n\\nSo the family was not held back by how it was scored. It was short of the truth mainly in\\nhow often these targets manoeuvre and how they turn, which it varied, and it still could\\nnot be chosen by one period\'s evidence.\\n\\n## What was withdrawn or corrected along the way\\n\\nThe record matters as much as the results. In six studies:\\n\\n- **Spec 013\'s first reading of the manoeuvre prior was withdrawn**: the evidence had been\\n  evaluated at a bearings-only fit, which produced thousands of units of artefact. The\\n  owner\'s decision made on that reading went back to the owner.\\n- **Gate T stopped at validation the first time**: its true-history reference started at\\n  the truth and stayed there, scoring better than the bound. It was re-registered with a\\n  reference fitted from neutral starts.\\n- **The evidence was wrong five times**: evaluated at a bearings-only fit; three small\\n  errors in the prior\'s density; a manoeuvre duration\'s volume (repaired in spec 015);\\n  and the edges of the prior\'s support (recorded, unrepaired, and applying to every system\\n  since). Separately, the rule for manoeuvres cut by a period\'s edge was wrong in the\\n  classing of histories and was repaired in spec 015.\\n- **Forecasts that were wrong**: the share of the search\'s gain that refitting obtains\\n  (0.75 forecast and 0.43 measured in spec 014; 0.50 forecast and 0.33 measured in spec\\n  015); spec 017\'s forecast that the control would not be separated from the\\n  detectable-only reference (it was, though the test value lay inside the tuning\\n  interval); spec 018\'s forecast of a trade. Forecasts read off one tuning run were\\n  guesses each time.\\n- **Rules that measured the wrong thing**: spec 016\'s comparison (a detector, not a\\n  model); spec 017\'s second condition (counts, not cost); spec 018\'s spread band (its edge\\n  sat on the tuning value).\\n- **Statistics that misled**: ratios of medians read as typical gains (spec 013); a gain\\n  \\"carried by its ten best periods\\" (spec 018 and this spec\'s first draft), which is what\\n  any widely spread ratio looks like when only its best cases are dropped.\\n- **Words the panels took back**: \\"ceiling\\", \\"bound\\", \\"upper limit\\" and \\"lower limit\\" for\\n  fits told the truth; \\"ambiguous\\" for a property of the Laplace evidence; \\"harm\\" for a\\n  tail with a level median.\\n\\n## What was never tested, and what was tested and left unremedied\\n\\nNever tested:\\n\\n- a learned proposer inside the search, training through the arc model, a larger training\\n  budget, or the control and the learned model\'s own track combined;\\n- a longer history of the same target than one period, to learn its habits;\\n- advice to ownship: where the bearings do not show a manoeuvre, ownship\'s own motion is\\n  what would;\\n- a family of priors that includes Annex B\'s own; a working diagnostic that the prior is\\n  wrong;\\n- the owner\'s bearing-rate thinning idea (the adaptive rule built for it broke its bins on\\n  noise, so the idea itself is untested);\\n- a whitened fit or a Huber cost in place of editing and an enlarged sd; a smoothed IMM as\\n  a reference; a spread for course and speed; real data of any kind.\\n\\nTested and left unremedied: a slowly varying bias (the control does nothing); the\\nevidence\'s fault at the edges of the prior\'s support. Thinly tested: whole engagements,\\non only 60 periods in each of three studies, and the control itself on 60.\\n\\n## What it adds to the project\'s question\\n\\nThe project asks whether a learned component can match the classical solution where the\\nsimple motion model holds and beat it where it does not. On the time-late consumer the\\nanswer is \\"not shown, at a small budget, with one direct model and one untuned read-out\\";\\nas a proposer inside the search a learned model is untested. The large gains came from\\nthe motion model (how a manoeuvre unfolds) and from handling bad bearings; the manoeuvre\\nstatistics added about a fifth. What is left on targets unlike Annex B\'s is partly\\nmanoeuvres the bearings do not show, and the closable part resisted one learned model and\\ntwo classical attempts.\\n\\nA learned approach would be worth trying again if one of three things changed: the data\\n(real bearings, whose errors and target habits no simulator prior states exactly); the\\ninformation (several periods of the same target, or ownship manoeuvres chosen to reveal\\nit); or the role (a proposer whose suggestions the evidence still judges). None is in the\\nSRD\'s present scope without the owner\'s decision.\\n\\n## Records\\n\\nSpecs 013 to 018: `experiments/20261112-gt2/`, `20261112-tl2/`, `20261113-tl3/`,\\n`20261114-tl4/`, `20261114-tl5/`, `20261115-tl6/`, each with `review.md`. The table of\\nrecord: `experiments/20261115-tl-synthesis/tables.md`, generated by\\n`scripts/research/tl_synthesis.py`. The diagnostic: `experiments/20261115-tl7-dev/`.\\nWrite-ups: [Gate T](/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t),\\n[classical follow-on](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical),\\n[repair](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair),\\n[controls and a learned model](/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned),\\n[the shifted-prior gap](/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap),\\n[a prior that adapts](/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior)."},{"id":"/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-15-time-late-synthesis-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: one last diagnostic, and the time-late track written up as a whole","description":"The owner has chosen option B after spec 018: write up the time-late track (specs 013 to","date":"2026-11-15T14:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-019","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-019"}],"readingTime":2.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: one last diagnostic, and the time-late track written up as a whole","date":"2026-11-15T14:00","tags":["kickoff","spec-019"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The time-late track as a whole: what six studies established, and what they did not","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: a manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period, and a hedged track","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior-slides"}},"content":"The owner has chosen option B after spec 018: write up the time-late track (specs 013 to\\n018) as a whole, with the classical control standing, after one diagnostic on periods\\nalready used. Spec: `specs/019-time-late-synthesis/`. Nothing is trained, no GPU is used\\nand no new seed is generated.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n[Spec 018](/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior) was decided by its panel in one round. A\\ncontrol that chooses among 30 manoeuvre priors by one period\'s evidence gains nothing on\\nbusier targets; the standing control stays. The search told the targets\' own statistics is\\nabout 1.15 better over three seeds, in a minority of periods. One thing was left\\nconfounded: the family\'s members refitted shared candidates while the true prior had its\\nown search, and the family differed from the truth in parts never varied.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\nSRD \xa71 and \xa75; a line in \xa79 records the decision (proposed; the owner merges).\\n\\n1. **A ladder of priors.** From the broadened prior to the true one, taking on the busier\\n   targets\' own statistics a part at a time (legs, turn sizes, turn rates, speed changes,\\n   kinds of manoeuvre), each with a search of its own; and each part alone. On 150 shifted\\n   periods of seed 347, already used: development work, not a result.\\n2. **Search against refit.** The family member nearest the truth, given its own search,\\n   against spec 018\'s refit of the same periods.\\n3. **The account.** One post covering specs 013 to 018: the standing system, each result\\n   as its panel left it, what was withdrawn, the forecasts that were wrong, what was never\\n   tested, and what the work adds to the project\'s founding question.\\n4. Independent reviewers check the account against the records; slides; PR.\\n\\nThe time-late track ends here unless the diagnostic shows something the owner wants\\npursued.\\n\\nRisks: the parts of a prior may interact, so that no rung \\"carries\\" the gain (both the\\nladder and the single parts are run for that reason); 150 used periods separate only\\nlarge effects; a synthesis invites tidy conclusions the panels did not allow.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- The ladder: each rung against the standing control, with the share of its gain in the\\n  ten best periods.\\n- Search of its own against refit for the nearest member.\\n- The track\'s gains in one chart: old system, search, control, by kind of target and\\n  bearing error, from the committed test records of specs 013 to 018.\\n- Track plots of example periods chosen by a rule, good and bad.\\n- All from committed experiment folders by scripts named in the slides post.\\n\\n**Update, 2026-11-15 (evening).** Done. The diagnostic ran on 130 used periods: a search\\nof its own did no better than a refit, and the true prior\'s gain builds along the ladder,\\nlegs first, with nothing separated. Three reviewers checked the account against the\\nrecords and corrected it. [The account](/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis) and\\n[the slides](/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-slides)."},{"id":"/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-15-adaptive-prior-slides.md","title":"Slides: a manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period, and a hedged track","description":"The illustrated companion to the spec 018 write-up:","date":"2026-11-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-018","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-018"}],"readingTime":1.71,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: a manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period, and a hedged track","date":"2026-11-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","spec-018"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: one last diagnostic, and the time-late track written up as a whole","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/time-late-synthesis-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"A manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period: no gain, and why one period cannot choose its prior","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the spec 018 write-up](/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior):\\nthe family of priors, the registered comparison on a fresh seed, where a gain was\\nexpected, the hedged track\'s spread, what the review panel found, and two periods as\\ntracks.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-15-adaptive-prior-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Verdict**: the standing control \xf7 the adaptive control (declared, hedged) and \xf7 the\\n  search told the true statistics, in range, on every split, with intervals and period\\n  counts. Grey ticks are median per-period ratios.\\n- **Quantities**: the same in range, course and speed on Annex B and shifted targets.\\n- **Kinds**: shifted targets by kind of period: where the standing control misses a\\n  manoeuvre the bearings show, where it does not, and by how tight the bound is.\\n- **Groups**: the prior chosen by one period, by groups of 5 and 20, and by the whole split.\\n- **Spread**: the share of rows within one and two stated sds of the hedged track\'s range,\\n  against the registered band.\\n- **Two periods as tracks**, chosen by a rule in the script: of the 20 stored shifted\\n  periods, the one where the hedged track is best against the control and the one where\\n  it is worst.\\n\\nThe panel\'s readings (how flat the choice among priors is, the pooled figures over three\\nseeds) are the reviewers\' recomputations; they are on a slide as a table and in\\n`experiments/20261115-tl6/review.md`, not in a generated figure.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\n```\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl6_tracks.py <results folder> experiments/20261115-tl6\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl6_charts.py --record experiments/20261115-tl6 --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-15-adaptive-prior-slides\\n```\\n\\nThe charts read only the committed record; each figure\'s `.json` sidecar names the inputs\'\\ndigests and the commit. `tracks.npz` holds the first 20 shifted periods\' tracks, extracted\\nfrom the results branch `results/timelate-tl-g-test-43677426621`. The data are seed 347,\\n5-second bearings, ranges files under `configs/timelate/`, generated in the CI run."},{"id":"/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-15-adaptive-prior.md","title":"A manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period: no gain, and why one period cannot choose its prior","description":"Spec 017 said the closable part of the gap on busier targets was largely the prior\'s. The","date":"2026-11-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-018","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-018"}],"readingTime":6.11,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"A manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period: no gain, and why one period cannot choose its prior","date":"2026-11-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["write-up","spec-018"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: a manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period, and a hedged track","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: a manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period, and a hedged track","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/adaptive-prior-kickoff"}},"content":"Spec 017 said the closable part of the gap on busier targets was largely the prior\'s. The\\nowner chose to try the classical remedy: a control that infers how the target manoeuvres\\nfrom the period itself, with a hedged track beside it. It is done, on a fresh seed, with\\nnothing trained. The adaptive control gains nothing on busier targets, so by the rule\\nregistered beforehand the standing control stays. My forecast, written from the tuning\\nseed, said otherwise and was wrong. The illustrated companion is\\n[the slides post](/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior-slides).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was built\\n\\n**Two designs, both on the tuning seed.** The first ran a search under each of three\\nmanoeuvre rates and let the period\'s evidence choose. It gained nothing: one period did\\nnot identify the rate. The second, which was registered, uses a family of 30 priors (how\\nlong the legs are, how regular, how large the turns), scores one shared set of candidate\\nhistories under each, and declares under the member with the most evidence. Beside it, a\\nhedged track: the weighted mean of every member\'s candidates, with a stated spread in\\nrange.\\n\\nThe plan, code and conditions were bound by digest before the validation and test seeds\\nexisted. The conditions read paired period error and coverage, not counts: a lesson from\\nthe last panel.\\n\\n## The registered outcome (test seed 347, 1,250 periods)\\n\\nStanding control \xf7 system, in range (above 1: the system is better):\\n\\n| Split (scored periods) | Adaptive, declared | Adaptive, hedged |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Shifted (355) | 1.00 [0.92, 1.08] | 1.00 [0.93, 1.08] |\\n| Shifted 2 (137) | 1.01 [0.92, 1.11] | 0.99 [0.89, 1.09] |\\n| Annex B (273) | 0.93 [0.85, 1.01] | 0.92 [0.84, 1.01] |\\n| Held course (172) | 1.05 [0.98, 1.13] | 1.02 [0.94, 1.12] |\\n| Outliers (93) | 0.95 [0.79, 1.16] | 0.94 [0.79, 1.11] |\\n| Correlated (93) | 0.85 [0.71, 1.00] | 0.89 [0.74, 1.06] |\\n\\nNo gain on either busier-target split for either output. The hedged track reads as harm\\nin course on two splits. Its stated spread covers 0.94 of rows on Annex B targets and 0.90\\non shifted, at the edge of the registered band. **The standing control stays.**\\n\\nOn the tuning seed the same comparison was 1.10 and 1.16 on shifted targets. I forecast a\\ntrade from that. The 60-period validation seed had already shown 0.95 and 1.04.\\n\\n## What the panel made of it\\n\\nFive reviewers recomputed all 36 cells and the registration; all approved with findings.\\nThe outcome stands. Several of my secondary sentences did not.\\n\\n**What the null means.** The evidence of one period does not rank these priors by how\\naccurate they are. Its preference among the 30 members is nearly flat (the favourite has\\na probability of about 0.13), and on shifted targets a member\'s probability and its error\\nare uncorrelated. The member chosen is 1.9 times worse than the member that would have\\nbeen best in hindsight, and only 3 to 5% better than one sensible fixed member. The choice\\navoids bad priors. It does not adapt. A period holds two to five manoeuvres: too few to\\nsay what kind of target made them.\\n\\n**How tight the null is.** The test could have detected a gain of 12%. Pooled over three\\nseeds the declared control is 1.01 [0.95, 1.08]: gains above about 8% are excluded, small\\nones are not. I wrote that the tuning seed had flattered the design \\"by all of the\\neffect\\"; that overstates what one test shows.\\n\\n**The cost on Annex B targets is real and is the design\'s.** Pooled over three seeds:\\n0.91 [0.84, 0.98]. The standing control searches under Annex B\'s own prior; my family did\\nnot include it. Where the control\'s answer came from that prior the adaptive control is\\n0.89; elsewhere 1.05.\\n\\n**The family was not blind.** I set its grid after the first tuning run had told me what\\nthe first grid lacked, and it sits close to the test splits\' own statistics. That favoured\\nit. It gained nothing all the same.\\n\\n**The reference it was chasing is a tail.** The search told the true statistics is 1.10\\nhere, 1.15 [1.07, 1.23] over three seeds. Ten shifted periods of 355 carry 87% of that\\ngain, and in 13% of periods it is over twice as bad as the control. I had called it a\\nceiling. It is not one.\\n\\n**The hedge is not worth having as built.** It costs about 10% in course on every split,\\nas a tail, and is level in range. Its spread cannot be stated by one factor: the factor\\nthat gave 95% coverage on the tuning seed was 1.95 and on the test seed would be 2.68,\\nboth set by about twenty periods. The spread is honest where the candidates disagree and\\ntoo narrow where the mixture is confident and wrong.\\n\\n## What was wrong in the first record\\n\\n- \\"The true statistics are worth less than spec 017 found\\": sampling. Three seeds agree.\\n- \\"The inferred rate barely moves\\": it moves, by about half the true difference, widely\\n  scattered per period.\\n- \\"It loses where the bearings say most\\": one seed\'s subgroup, a tail, not repeated.\\n- \\"None of the three is it\\": the members refit shared candidates and other parts of the\\n  prior were never varied.\\n- \\"Not honest by three parts in a thousand\\": shifted coverage is 0.897 [0.870, 0.923].\\n\\n## What was not delivered\\n\\nA family member with Annex B\'s own prior; a search under each member; unit tests of the\\nrunner and the spread code; the tuned IMM and EKF on these periods; a spread for course\\nand speed; a per-period rule for choosing between declared and hedged output that does\\nanything.\\n\\n## What this does and does not show\\n\\nIt shows that this evidence, on one period, cannot tell which of a family of manoeuvre\\npriors is the right one, and that a hedged mean of candidates is no better than the\\ndeclared history. On quiet targets the adaptive control invents fewer manoeuvres (3% of\\nperiods against 8%), a gain not separated in error and reversed on the tuning seed.\\n\\nIt does not show that priors cannot be adapted: a longer history of the same target, a\\ndifferent evidence, or a family that contained the truth\'s other dimensions were not\\ntested. It does not show a learned model would fail, though a model facing the same two\\nto five manoeuvres has the same information.\\n\\nAcross specs 013 to 018 the time-late picture is steady. The arc model and Annex B\'s\\nstatistics brought a factor of two over the old system. Editing and an honest bearing sd\\nremoved most of the damage from bad bearings. On targets unlike Annex B\'s, about two\\nthirds of the remaining gap is manoeuvres the bearings do not show, and the rest has now\\nresisted a learned model and a classical one.\\n\\n## What comes next\\n\\nThe panel\'s recommendation, which I adopt as mine: **stop trying to identify the prior,\\nand write up the time-late track (specs 013 to 018) as a whole**, with the classical\\ncontrol standing. The alternatives are in the question put to the owner. A learned\\nproposer or any GPU run would be over-reach.\\n\\n## Records\\n\\n`experiments/20261114-tl6-tuning/`, `experiments/20261115-tl6-tuning2/`, `-tl6-decision/`,\\n`-tl6-val/` and the test record `experiments/20261115-tl6/` (`review.md`). Seeds 345 to\\n347 are used; the next fresh seed is 348. Nothing was trained and nothing spent."},{"id":"/2026/11/14/adaptive-prior-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/adaptive-prior-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-14-adaptive-prior-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: a manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period, and a hedged track","description":"The owner has chosen option B after spec 017: a classical control whose manoeuvre prior is","date":"2026-11-14T22:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-018","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-018"}],"readingTime":2.86,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: a manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period, and a hedged track","date":"2026-11-14T22:00","tags":["kickoff","spec-018"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"A manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period: no gain, and why one period cannot choose its prior","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: the standing time-late control, and is the shifted-prior gap identifiability?","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-kickoff"}},"content":"The owner has chosen option B after spec 017: a classical control whose manoeuvre prior is\\ninferred from the period itself, and beside it a hedged track for the periods where the\\nbearings cannot decide. Spec: `specs/018-adaptive-prior/`. Nothing is trained and no GPU\\nis used.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n[Spec 017](/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap) was decided by its panel in one round. On targets\\nbusier than Annex B says, the standing control is 2.3 times a fit told the true history,\\nand that gap is two things. In about two thirds of periods the limit is manoeuvres the\\nbearings barely show. In under a third the control misses manoeuvres the bearings do show;\\nabout half of those are found by the same search when it is told the targets\' own\\nstatistics (1.17 better overall, at the price of more false manoeuvres). The control\\nstands as the time-late system. Open: a system that is not told the statistics.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\nSRD \xa71 and \xa75; a line in \xa79 records the owner\'s decision (proposed; the owner merges).\\n\\n1. **An adaptive prior.** The search runs under several manoeuvre rates and the period\'s\\n   own evidence weighs them, so a busy target is recognised as busy. Not told the split.\\n2. **Its price.** Annex B targets, targets that never manoeuvre, outliers and correlated\\n   errors: range, course and speed error, false manoeuvres, one-manoeuvre targets called\\n   quiet.\\n3. **A hedged track.** The probability-weighted mean of the candidate histories, across\\n   rates too, with a stated spread, and a check that the spread covers the truth as often\\n   as it says.\\n4. **Registration** before the fresh seeds (346 validation, 347 test): conditions that can\\n   come out either way at the planned size and that read cost, not counts.\\n5. Panel, write-up, slides.\\n\\nIf the adaptive control gains on busier targets without harm elsewhere, it is proposed as\\nthe standing system. If it gains with harm, the owner decides. If it does not gain, the\\nstanding control stays. The hedged track becomes an output only if its spread is honest.\\n\\nRisks: the inferred rate may follow noise, outliers or correlated errors and invent\\nmanoeuvres on quiet targets; the gain available is small (the ceiling is about 1.17 and\\nsits in a minority of periods), so the test may not separate it; a mean of mirror-image\\ncandidates is a track no target followed.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- The inferred rate against the true rate, by split (tuning seed 345, then test 347).\\n- Adaptive control \xf7 standing control and \xf7 the true-prior search on every split, pooled\\n  and by kind of period, with period counts.\\n- Manoeuvres missed and false for each, at three matching tolerances.\\n- The hedged track\'s error beside the declared track\'s, and the coverage of its spread.\\n- Track plots of periods chosen by a rule in the script.\\n- All from committed experiment folders by a script named in the slides post.\\n\\n**Update, 2026-11-15.** Done. The first design gained nothing on the tuning seed; a second\\nwas registered. Validation on seed 346 passed soundness; the test on seed 347 was scored\\nonce and decided by the panel in one round: no gain, the standing control stays. The\\nresult and what the panel made of it are in\\n[the write-up](/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior) and\\n[the slides](/blog/2026/11/15/adaptive-prior-slides)."},{"id":"/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-14-shifted-gap-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: the standing time-late control, and is the shifted-prior gap identifiability?","description":"The owner has chosen the panel\'s recommendation after study L: the classical control","date":"2026-11-14T18:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-017","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-017"}],"readingTime":2.66,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: the standing time-late control, and is the shifted-prior gap identifiability?","date":"2026-11-14T18:00","tags":["kickoff","spec-017"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: a manoeuvre prior that adapts to the period, and a hedged track","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/adaptive-prior-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: what the shifted-prior gap is made of","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-slides"}},"content":"The owner has chosen the panel\'s recommendation after study L: the classical control\\nbecomes the standing time-late system, and one cheap classical study asks whether the gap\\nleft under a shifted manoeuvre prior can be closed by anyone. Spec:\\n`specs/017-shifted-gap/`. Nothing is trained and no GPU is used.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n[Study L](/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned) was decided by its panel in one round. The\\nclassical control stands: it removes most of the damage from outliers and correlated\\nbearing errors and from quiet targets. The learned model\'s reported track beat it nowhere,\\nand the panel showed that loss was the model\'s untuned manoeuvre detector. Open for every\\nsystem: on targets that manoeuvre more often and less sharply than Annex B says, the\\ncontrol\'s error is 1.7 to 2.1 times that of a fit told the true history, with half the\\nmanoeuvres missed.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\nSRD \xa71 and \xa75; a line in \xa79 records the standing system (proposed; the owner merges).\\n\\n1. **Detectability.** For every true manoeuvre, how much worse the bearings are fitted\\n   when it is left out of the true history. No search, no evidence approximation.\\n2. **The gap, split.** A reference told only the detectable manoeuvres. The part of the\\n   gap that remains for it is identifiability; the part between it and the control is what\\n   a better search or evidence could win.\\n3. **The true prior.** The control\'s search given the shifted targets\' own statistics: what\\n   knowing them is worth.\\n4. **Registration** of the rule, thresholds and sizes before the fresh seeds (343\\n   validation, 344 test), with the rule\'s power stated.\\n5. **What study L\'s record owes**: the scored weights\' digests, the export check on the\\n   trained models, IMM and EKF tracks beside, a reference fitted to edited bearings, tests\\n   for the runner and scorer.\\n6. Panel, write-up, slides.\\n\\nIf the gap is mostly identifiability, the learned-proposer idea is not taken and the\\ntime-late track is written up. If it is mostly attainable, a proposer study using the\\nthree trained models is proposed to the owner.\\n\\nRisks: \\"detectable\\" depends on a threshold (three are registered and the result is given\\nat each); the reference told the true history drifts in a quarter of shifted periods (its\\ntimes are held, and the share stated); a split may come out mixed, which is a result.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- The distribution of detectability for Annex B and shifted manoeuvres, and the control\'s\\n  miss rate against it (tuning 342, then test 344).\\n- The gap as two stacked parts per split and threshold, with intervals.\\n- The true-prior search beside the control.\\n- Track plots of periods with undetectable manoeuvres, chosen by a rule in the script.\\n- All from committed experiment folders by a script named in the slides post.\\n\\n**Update, 2026-11-14 (evening).** Done. Validation on seed 343 passed soundness; the test\\non seed 344 was scored once and decided by the panel in one round. The result and what the\\npanel made of it are in [the write-up](/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap) and\\n[the slides](/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-slides)."},{"id":"/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-14-shifted-gap-slides.md","title":"Slides: what the shifted-prior gap is made of","description":"The illustrated companion to the spec 017 write-up: how","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-017","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-017"}],"readingTime":1.72,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: what the shifted-prior gap is made of","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","spec-017"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: the standing time-late control, and is the shifted-prior gap identifiability?","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Why the time-late control is twice the reference on busier targets: part hidden manoeuvres, part the wrong prior","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the spec 017 write-up](/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap): how\\ndetectable the busier targets\' manoeuvres are, what the control misses, the gap as a chain\\nof references, what the review panel found, and two periods as tracks.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-14-shifted-gap-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Detectable**: the share of true manoeuvres whose omission adds less than 10, 20 and 40\\n  to the \u03c7\xb2 of the true history\'s fit, by split.\\n- **Missed**: on the shifted split, the share of manoeuvres each system misses, by\\n  detectability band. The search under the targets\' own prior misses fewer at every band.\\n- **Chain**: the control against the reference told every manoeuvre, the cost of not being\\n  told the ones under 20, the control against that reference, and the control against the\\n  true-prior search. Grey ticks are median per-period ratios.\\n- **Share**: the attainable share of the control\'s gap at each threshold, by split (under\\n  outliers, against references fitted to the edited bearings).\\n- **Two periods as tracks**, chosen by a rule in the script: of the 20 stored shifted\\n  periods with at least two whole true manoeuvres, the one with the largest share under 20\\n  and the one with the smallest.\\n\\nThe panel\'s split of the gap into two kinds of period is in the write-up and\\n`experiments/20261114-tl5/review.md`; it is the reviewers\' recomputation, shown on a slide\\nas a table and not as a generated figure.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\n```\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl5_tracks.py <results folder> experiments/20261114-tl5\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl5_charts.py --record experiments/20261114-tl5 --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-14-shifted-gap-slides\\n```\\n\\nThe charts read only the committed record; each figure\'s `.json` sidecar names the inputs\'\\ndigests and the commit. `tracks.npz` holds the first 20 shifted periods\' tracks, extracted\\nfrom the results branch `results/timelate-tl-f-test-43618868241`. The data are seed 344,\\n5-second bearings, ranges files under `configs/timelate/`, generated in the CI run."},{"id":"/2026/11/14/shifted-gap","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-14-shifted-gap.md","title":"Why the time-late control is twice the reference on busier targets: part hidden manoeuvres, part the wrong prior","description":"Study L left one gap open on both sides: on targets that manoeuvre more often and less","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-017","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-017"}],"readingTime":5.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Why the time-late control is twice the reference on busier targets: part hidden manoeuvres, part the wrong prior","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["write-up","spec-017"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: what the shifted-prior gap is made of","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: time-late, classical controls and a learned model","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned-slides"}},"content":"Study L left one gap open on both sides: on targets that manoeuvre more often and less\\nsharply than Annex B says, the classical control is about twice a fit told the true\\nhistory. The owner asked for one cheap classical study of whether that gap is\\nidentifiability, before anyone considers a learned proposer. It is done, on a fresh seed,\\nwith nothing trained. The answer is not one number. In about two thirds of periods the\\nlimit is manoeuvres the bearings barely show; in under a third the control misses\\nmanoeuvres the bearings do show, and at least half of those misses are the prior\'s, not\\nthe search\'s. The illustrated companion is\\n[the slides post](/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap-slides).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was measured\\n\\nFor every true manoeuvre, its **detectability**: how much the \u03c7\xb2 of the true history\'s\\nfit rises when that one manoeuvre is left out, the others\' times held. Bearings only, no\\nprior, no search. Then three kinds of reference:\\n\\n- a fit told every manoeuvre (`ref-full`);\\n- fits told only the manoeuvres above a detectability of 10, 20 or 40;\\n- the control\'s own search given the busier targets\' true statistics.\\n\\nThe thresholds bracket what the evidence charges for a manoeuvre under Annex B\'s prior\\n(about 22). The plan, code and estimands were bound by digest before the validation and\\ntest seeds existed. No decision rule was registered, because on tuning data the plan\'s\\nrule was a coin\'s toss; instead the logic of the recommendation was fixed in advance.\\n\\n## The numbers (test seed 344; shifted split, 451 scored periods)\\n\\n| | Threshold 10 | Threshold 20 | Threshold 40 |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Share of true manoeuvres under it | 0.28 | 0.37 | 0.47 |\\n| Not being told them costs | 1.34 | 1.58 | 2.02 |\\n| Control \xf7 the reference not told them | 1.70 [1.52, 1.90] | 1.43 [1.28, 1.61] | 1.12 [0.99, 1.27] |\\n| Attainable share of the control\'s gap | 0.65 | 0.44 [0.33, 0.54] | 0.14 |\\n\\nThe whole gap is 2.27 [2.04, 2.55]. On Annex B data 0.16 of manoeuvres are under 20; on\\nthese targets 0.37 are. The control misses 0.83 of those and 0.18 of the ones above 40.\\nGiven the targets\' own statistics, the same search is 1.17 [1.06, 1.29] better than the\\ncontrol.\\n\\nBy the logic fixed beforehand, a proposer study would be recommended only if the control\\nwere separated from the threshold-20 reference by more than 1.10 **and** it missed over a\\nthird of the manoeuvres above 40. The first held (against my forecast); the second did not\\n(0.18). Output of the logic: not recommended.\\n\\n## What the panel made of it\\n\\nFive reviewers recomputed everything; all approved with findings. The registration held\\nand every number reproduces. They changed the reading in four ways.\\n\\n**The pooled split is two kinds of period.** Where some manoeuvre is under 20 (336\\nperiods), the control is only 1.16 times the reference that is not told it: there the\\nlimit is what the bearings can show. Where nothing is under 20 (115 periods), the control\\nis 2.65 times the reference. Cut the other way: in the 134 periods where the control\\nmisses a manoeuvre above 20 it is 2.73 times the reference, and those periods carry 83% of\\nthe attainable gap; in the other 317 it is 1.09. \\"0.44 attainable\\" is an average of\\n\\"mostly identifiability\\" and \\"all attainable\\". By geometry, the attainable share is 0.70\\nwhere the bound is tight and 0.20 where it is loose.\\n\\n**My rule\'s second condition measured the wrong thing.** It counted manoeuvres; the cost\\nis per period. And it depends on how closely a found manoeuvre must match in time: the\\nmiss rate above 40 is 0.30 at 60 seconds, 0.18 at the registered 120, 0.11 at 240. The\\n\\"no\\" is the rule\'s output. It does not follow from the evidence, and at a tuning value of\\n0.18 the condition could hardly have fired. I registered it; that was a poor choice.\\n\\n**The attainable part is at least half a prior problem.** Of the 138 detectable\\nmanoeuvres the control misses, the same search under the right prior finds 66. Where it\\nfinds more, it is 2.6 times better; elsewhere, 1.07. In most periods with such a miss the\\ncontrol declares fewer manoeuvres than happened: it under-counts because Annex B\'s prior\\ntells it these targets are quiet. A learned proposer feeding the same evidence would\\nmostly be vetoed by it.\\n\\n**The measure is not a bound in either direction.** Detectability is not additive: on 39\\nperiods a reviewer refitted, leaving out two or three \\"undetectable\\" manoeuvres together\\ncost 20 or more in 44% (in one case 18, 0.2 and 2 singly, 2,481 together). So the\\n\\"hidden\\" share and the cost of hiding are overstated. And the references are flattered by\\nbeing told true times: with the right history the control is still 1.6 to 1.7 times the\\nfull reference. I wrote that one estimand was an upper limit and another a lower limit;\\nneither is.\\n\\n## What was wrong in the first record\\n\\n- \\"The tuning seed differed by more than its intervals\\": false. The test\'s values lie\\n  inside the tuning intervals. A point estimate from 134 periods was read as a forecast.\\n- The IMM and EKF ratios (4.8 to 22) were on the 40% of periods where a filter has a state\\n  on most rows; on common rows they are 3.8 to 10.5. Far behind either way.\\n- Shifted 2\'s \\"mostly attainable\\" rests on one clipped reference and 19 manoeuvres.\\n\\n## What was not delivered\\n\\nThe split under each way of fitting the reference; course and speed in the estimands (the\\npanel\'s recomputation: more attainable than range); a joint detectability; unit tests for\\nthe new runner and the detectability code; a smoothed IMM.\\n\\n## What this does and does not show\\n\\nIt shows that on busier targets a good part of the gap cannot be closed by any detector\\nworking from these bearings, that the closable part is concentrated in a minority of\\nperiods, and that the cheapest lever on it is the manoeuvre prior, not the search.\\n\\nIt does not give \\"the share that is identifiability\\": that number moves from 0.35 to 0.86\\nwith the threshold, and the measure behind it is biased both ways. It does not show a\\nproposer would fail, only that the evidence would veto much of what it proposed. And the\\n1.17 for the right prior is one seed, a minority of periods, and bought with more false\\nmanoeuvres (0.48 a period against 0.34).\\n\\n## What comes next\\n\\nThe panel\'s recommendation, which I adopt as mine: **a prior that adapts to the period**\\n(a manoeuvre rate estimated from the period itself, or a mixture over rates), classical,\\non CPU, registered against the standing control with the true-prior search as its ceiling\\nand the false-manoeuvre rate on quiet targets reported. Beside it, a hedged track for the\\nperiods where the limit is identifiability. A GPU proposer now would be over-reach. The\\nchoice is the owner\'s.\\n\\n## Records\\n\\n`experiments/20261114-tl5-tuning/`, `-tl5-decision/`, `-tl5-val/` and the test record\\n`experiments/20261114-tl5/` (`review.md`). Seeds 342 to 344 are used; the next fresh seed\\nis 345. Nothing was trained and nothing spent."},{"id":"/2026/11/14/time-late-learned-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-14-time-late-learned-slides.md","title":"Slides: time-late, classical controls and a learned model","description":"The illustrated companion to the spec 016 write-up:","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-016","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-016"}],"readingTime":1.58,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: time-late, classical controls and a learned model","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","spec-016"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Why the time-late control is twice the reference on busier targets: part hidden manoeuvres, part the wrong prior","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/shifted-gap"},"nextItem":{"title":"Time-late, classical controls and a learned model: the controls stand, and what the learned model\'s loss is made of","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the spec 016 write-up](/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned):\\nthe classical controls, the learned whole-period model, the registered comparison on a\\nfresh seed, what the review panel found the loss was made of, and track plots.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-14-time-late-learned-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **The registered comparison**: declared control \xf7 learned track for three training seeds\\n  on every criterion split, with intervals; the orange ticks are the median per-period\\n  ratio, which is 1.00 everywhere.\\n- **Every system** on every split: C\u2033 as a track, the standing search, the classical\\n  control, the model\'s own track and its arc read-out, and the fit told the true history.\\n- **What the classical control adds**, on the tuning, validation and test seeds.\\n- **Manoeuvres missed and false**: the model\'s detector misses more and invents far fewer.\\n- **Training**: the monitor curve of the three seeds.\\n- **Three periods as tracks.** They are chosen by a rule in the script, not by eye: of the\\n  20 stored periods of the shifted split, the one where the learned track is worst against\\n  the control and the one where it is best; and the first stored outlier period with a\\n  true manoeuvre.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\n```\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl4_charts.py --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-14-time-late-learned-slides\\n```\\n\\nThe script reads only committed records (`experiments/20261113-tl4-tuning/`,\\n`20261113-tl4-val/`, `20261114-tl4/`, `20261114-tlm-runs/`); each figure\'s `.json`\\nsidecar names the inputs\' digests and the commit. `experiments/20261114-tl4/tracks.npz`\\nholds the tracks at scored rows for the first 20 periods of three splits, extracted from\\nthe results branch `results/timelate-tl-e-test-43563825793`. The data are seeds 338\\n(tuning), 340 (validation) and 341 (test), 5-second bearings, ranges files under\\n`configs/timelate/`, generated in the CI runs."},{"id":"/2026/11/14/time-late-learned","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-14-time-late-learned.md","title":"Time-late, classical controls and a learned model: the controls stand, and what the learned model\'s loss is made of","description":"The owner asked for two things at once: the classical controls the last panel wanted, and a","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-016","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-016"}],"readingTime":6.33,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Time-late, classical controls and a learned model: the controls stand, and what the learned model\'s loss is made of","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["write-up","spec-016"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: time-late, classical controls and a learned model","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, classical controls and a learned whole-period model","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-learned-kickoff"}},"content":"The owner asked for two things at once: the classical controls the last panel wanted, and a\\nlearned whole-period model trained in parallel under a plan fixed before any result. Both\\nare done. On a fresh test seed the learned model\'s reported track beats the classical\\ncontrol on no split and loses to it on clean data, so by the registered rule the classical\\ncontrols stand and the learned track stops. The review panel approved that outcome and\\nchanged what it means: the loss is not a typical period, it is the model\'s untuned\\nmanoeuvre detector, and the comparison we registered was narrower than \\"learned against\\nclassical\\". The illustrated companion is\\n[the slides post](/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned-slides).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was built\\n\\n**Classical controls**, nothing trained, composed from the search spec 015 left standing:\\n\\n- wild bearings edited out before the search;\\n- one larger bearing sd when the bearings\' second differences show the errors are\\n  correlated in time;\\n- the search run under Annex B\'s manoeuvre prior and a broadened one, pooled;\\n- a manoeuvre declared only when it is worth an evidence margin of 1, with a stated 1%\\n  chance that the target never manoeuvres;\\n- a hedged track: the probability-weighted mean of the candidates.\\n\\nSettings came from a tuning seed. The combined control is not told which condition it is\\nin.\\n\\n**A learned model**: a bidirectional transformer reading a whole period of 5-second\\nbearings and giving the target\'s state every 30 seconds and a manoeuvre probability. Three\\nseeds, 12.5 million simulated periods each (Annex B, randomised manoeuvre statistics,\\nquiet targets, correlated errors and outliers), a T4 each, $14 in all. Its reported track\\nis the arc model fitted to the bearings at the manoeuvres the model declares.\\n\\nThe plan, the training mix, the criteria and the code were bound by digest before the first\\ntraining run and before the validation and test seeds existed. The scorer refused to score\\na learned track until the controls\' own validation was on record.\\n\\n## The registered outcome (test seed 341, 2,860 periods)\\n\\nDeclared control \xf7 learned track, paired per period, three seeds (above 1: the learned\\ntrack is better):\\n\\n| Split | Ratio | Verdict |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Shifted prior | 0.82, 0.80, 0.82 | not shown |\\n| Shifted prior 2 | 0.87, 0.87, 0.94 | not shown |\\n| Correlated errors | 0.83, 0.83, 0.84 | not shown |\\n| Outliers | 0.78, 0.77, 0.81 | not shown |\\n| Bias, absent from training | 0.98, 1.00, 0.97 | not shown |\\n| Annex B | 0.82, 0.81, 0.82 | harm |\\n| Held course | 1.06, 1.06, 1.06 | no harm |\\n\\nLearning is shown on no split and there is harm on Annex B data: **the classical controls\\nstand as the time-late system, and the learned track stops.** The forecast written before\\ntraining said this was the most likely outcome.\\n\\n## What the classical controls did\\n\\nAgainst the search they started from, the declared control is 1.68 [1.46, 1.94] better\\nwith 2% outliers, 2.44 [2.03, 3.00] with 5%, 1.33 [1.16, 1.53] with correlated errors and\\n1.70 [1.48, 1.95] on targets that never manoeuvre (false manoeuvres in 8% of periods\\nagainst 49%). These replicate across the tuning, validation and test seeds. This is the\\nresult the panel says is worth carrying.\\n\\nIt has limits the first record understated. Under a slowly varying bias it does nothing\\n(1.00). On clean data its range error is not separated from the search\'s over three seeds\\n(0.96 to 1.09). It calls a one-manoeuvre target quiet more often, markedly so with\\ncorrelated errors (0.29 against 0.05). Its correlated-error form knows the simulator\'s\\nerror family. And under a shifted manoeuvre prior it gains nothing (1.03): the control is\\nstill 1.7 to 2.1 times a fit told the true history, whichever way that reference is fitted.\\n\\n## What the learned model\'s loss is made of\\n\\nFive reviewers recomputed the outcome and it holds: all 42 ratios reproduce, no verdict is\\nnear its boundary, the registration held. Then they took the 20% apart.\\n\\n**The typical period is a tie.** The median per-period ratio is 1.00 on every split. Where\\nthe model declares the same number of manoeuvres as the control, the two tracks are level\\n(0.95 on Annex B, 1.01 under the shifted prior), because the read-out is then the same\\nclassical fit. Where the model declares fewer, in a quarter to a half of periods, its\\ntrack is about twice as bad. The whole loss is there.\\n\\n**So the registered comparison measured a detector.** The model\'s manoeuvre probability\\nwas thresholded at 0.5, a value never tuned, while the control\'s margin and quiet\\nprobability were tuned. That asymmetry was in the registration and was mine. A reviewer\\ntried the obvious repairs on spent tuning data: a lower threshold makes the read-out worse\\n(the fit can no more remove a false manoeuvre than add a missed one), and a broader prior\\nchanges nothing. Starting the control\'s own search from the model\'s answer equals the\\ncontrol at a third of the search time, and does not beat it.\\n\\n**The model as an estimator** (its own output, reported beside and deciding nothing) is\\n0.71 of the control on Annex B data. Under the shifted prior it is level in range, 1.01,\\nbut behind in course and speed (0.79, 0.77), and the level figure is a cancellation: it\\nbeats the control where the bearings determine little and is nearly three times worse\\nwhere they determine much. That is the signature of an estimator that leans on what it\\nlearned about targets in general, which this project has now seen three times.\\n\\n**The \\"mismatch\\" was the classical side\'s only.** The shifted, correlated and outlier\\nconditions all lie inside the model\'s training ranges. The model lost in-distribution.\\n\\n## What was not delivered\\n\\n- A working prior-mismatch diagnostic: the one computed is not a Bayes factor and fires on\\n  quiet targets, not on a shifted prior.\\n- A Huber cost and a whitened fit (editing and an effective sd stand in), and the repair\\n  of the evidence at the edges of the prior\'s support.\\n- The export check on the trained models (it ran on a toy), IMM and EKF tracks beside, and\\n  the weights\' digests in the test record.\\n- Unit tests for the runner and the scorer.\\n- Two launches of the GPU pilot failed at start-up on a launcher form that had never been\\n  run; the ledger under-counts a run whose container is replaced (39 cents against the\\n  owner\'s dashboard).\\n\\n## What this does and does not show\\n\\nIt shows that a small direct model, trained on the simulator, does not beat an evidence\\nsearch that knows the motion model, in or out of the conditions it was trained on, and\\nthat classical editing and an honest bearing sd remove most of the damage from outliers\\nand correlated errors.\\n\\nIt does not show that learning cannot help the time-late consumer. A learned proposer\\ninside the search, the model\'s probabilities as a prior, training through the arc model,\\nand a larger budget were not tested. Nor does it show that the shifted-prior gap can be\\nclosed by anything: busier targets with smaller turns may simply not be identifiable from\\nbearings, and that is the cheapest next thing to find out.\\n\\nAcross specs 012 to 016 the founding question stands at \\"not shown, at small budgets, with\\ndirect models\\". Each large gain so far has gone to a classical element.\\n\\n## Records\\n\\n`experiments/20261113-tl4-tuning/`, `-tl4-decision/` (two addenda), `-tl4-val/`,\\n`experiments/20261114-tlm-runs/`, and the test record `experiments/20261114-tl4/`\\n(`review.md`). Seeds 338 to 341 are used; the next fresh seed is 342. GPU spending in\\nNovember stands at about $41 of the owner\'s $50."},{"id":"/2026/11/13/time-late-learned-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-learned-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-13-time-late-learned-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: time-late, classical controls and a learned whole-period model","description":"The owner has chosen to build the classical controls the last panel asked for and, at the","date":"2026-11-13T18:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-016","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-016"}],"readingTime":3.32,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, classical controls and a learned whole-period model","date":"2026-11-13T18:00","tags":["kickoff","spec-016"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Time-late, classical controls and a learned model: the controls stand, and what the learned model\'s loss is made of","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: time-late, the classical follow-on","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical-slides"}},"content":"The owner has chosen to build the classical controls the last panel asked for and, at the\\nsame time, to train a learned whole-period model on GPU under a plan registered before any\\nresult. The model is scored only once the controls exist. Spec:\\n`specs/016-time-late-learned/`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n[Spec 015](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair) repaired the evidence, the edge rule and the\\nreference and decided the same quantities again on fresh seeds. The registered rule again\\ngave no GPU budget for identification on Annex B data, and the panel showed that 400 periods\\ncannot decide a 10% line there. Carried: the repaired evidence-scored search as the standing\\ntime-late system, and C\u2033 read as a track as the control beneath it. Open: against a\\nreference told the true history the search is 1.7 times worse under a shifted manoeuvre\\nprior, 1.4 under correlated bearing errors and 1.5 with outliers, and it invents a manoeuvre\\non half of quiet targets. No classical remedy has been tried for any of these, and neither\\nstudy said anything about hedging.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\nSRD \xa71 (the time-late consumer), \xa73 (the reported estimate comes from a declared read-out)\\nand \xa75 (a new row, proposed; the owner merges). Two tracks:\\n\\n1. **Controls, CPU, nothing trained.** A hedged track (the probability-weighted mean of the\\n   search\'s candidates) and a declaration margin; a robust likelihood and a correlated-error\\n   likelihood in the search; a broadened manoeuvre prior with a mismatch diagnostic. Settings\\n   on tuning seed 338.\\n2. **A learned model, GPU.** A sequence model reads the whole period and gives the whole\\n   track, trained on the fly on a declared mix (Annex B, shifted and broadened statistics,\\n   quiet targets, correlated errors, outliers), three seeds, within the unspent part of the\\n   $50 budget.\\n3. **Registration first.** The model\'s plan, training mix, comparison and criteria are bound\\n   by digest in a decision record before the first training job. No criterion is registered\\n   that the sample size cannot decide.\\n4. **Controls\' validation** (seed 340) is recorded; only then may the model be scored.\\n5. **One test scoring** (seed 341): the model against the best classical control on each\\n   split, with the hedged track beside it; a win is named for the control that explains it,\\n   and only what remains is called learning.\\n6. Panel, write-up, slides.\\n\\nRisks: the budget buys too little training to be a fair test; the controls close the gaps\\nand leave nothing to compare (still a result); a sixth error in the evidence; the model\\nwins by knowing the simulator\'s training mix, which is why one condition is held out of it.\\nWe stop before the test seed if the controls\' validation run is unsound, and no GPU job runs\\nbeyond the ledger\'s cap.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- Each control against the plain search on its own split, at two severities (validation 340,\\n  test 341): paired ratio and share of the gap to the reference closed.\\n- Held course: false manoeuvres against one-manoeuvre targets called quiet, as a curve over\\n  the declaration margin (tuning 338).\\n- The learned model\'s training curves for three seeds and the ledger in dollars.\\n- One table per split: plain search, best control, hedged track, learned model, reference.\\n- Track plots of a few periods where the systems differ, if the tracks are stored this time.\\n- All from committed experiment folders by a script named in the slides post.\\n\\n**Update, 2026-11-14.** The study ran. The classical controls remove most of the damage from\\noutliers and correlated bearing errors and from quiet targets; the learned model\'s reported\\ntrack beats the control on no split, so by the registered rule the controls stand. The\\npanel approved and showed the loss is the model\'s untuned manoeuvre detector, not a typical\\nperiod. See [the write-up](/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned) and\\n[the slides](/blog/2026/11/14/time-late-learned-slides)."},{"id":"/2026/11/13/time-late-classical-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-13-time-late-classical-slides.md","title":"Slides: time-late, the classical follow-on","description":"The illustrated companion to the spec 014 write-up:","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-014","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-014"}],"readingTime":1.45,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: time-late, the classical follow-on","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","spec-014"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, classical controls and a learned whole-period model","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-learned-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Time-late, the classical follow-on: what the search is worth, what it gets wrong, and two faults the panel found","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the spec 014 write-up](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical):\\nthe controls that split the search\'s gain, the classes of wrong histories, a target that\\nnever manoeuvres, the owner\'s thinning idea, and the two code defects and narrowed claims\\nfrom the review panel.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-13-time-late-classical-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Every system as a track** on Annex B data, the shifted prior and held course (test seed\\n  335).\\n- **The decided quantities on three seeds**: the refit\'s share of the gain and the two\\n  attainable-headroom figures, with intervals and the 10% line.\\n- **Why the first candidate is wrong**: periods by class. The panel showed these shares\\n  move when manoeuvres cut by the period\'s edge are left out.\\n- **A target that never manoeuvres**: false-manoeuvre rate by system on three seeds. Part of\\n  the search\'s rate is a recorded defect in the evidence.\\n- **Thinning**: each form\'s cost against the search\'s run time, clean and with outliers\\n  (tuning seed 333). The orange points used a rule that broke bins on noise.\\n- **Bearing errors the fits are not told about**: correlated and outlying errors.\\n\\nThere are no track plots: the per-period tracks are not stored, only their errors.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\n```\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl2_charts.py --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-13-time-late-classical-slides\\n```\\n\\nThe script reads only committed records (`experiments/20261112-tl2-tuning/`,\\n`20261112-tl2-val/`, `20261112-tl2/`, `20261112-tl2-thin/`); each figure\'s `.json` sidecar\\nnames the inputs\' digests and the commit. The data are seeds 333 (tuning), 334 (validation)\\nand 335 (test), 5-second bearings, ranges files under `configs/timelate/`, generated in the\\nCI runs."},{"id":"/2026/11/13/time-late-classical","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-13-time-late-classical.md","title":"Time-late, the classical follow-on: what the search is worth, what it gets wrong, and two faults the panel found","description":"Gate T showed that a classical search over manoeuvre histories halves the track error of the","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-014","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-014"}],"readingTime":8.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Time-late, the classical follow-on: what the search is worth, what it gets wrong, and two faults the panel found","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["write-up","spec-014"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: time-late, the classical follow-on","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, repair and re-registration","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-kickoff"}},"content":"Gate T showed that a classical search over manoeuvre histories halves the track error of the\\nstanding classical system, and its review panel showed that this did not yet say where the\\ngain comes from or how much is left. The owner chose classical work first. Spec 014 trained\\nnothing: it built the missing controls, tested the owner\'s bearing-rate thinning idea, looked\\nat targets that never manoeuvre, and registered a study on a fresh test seed. The numbers\\nreproduce. The panel found two defects in the registered code and narrowed most of what was\\nfirst said. This post reports both; the illustrated companion is\\n[the slides post](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical-slides).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was asked\\n\\nFour questions, with forecasts written before the fresh seeds existed\\n(`experiments/20261112-tl2-decision/`), and a rule fixed before any tuning result for the\\nfifth:\\n\\n1. How much of the search\'s gain over C\u2033 comes without a search, by refitting C\u2033\'s own\\n   manoeuvre times with the same arc model and prior?\\n2. When the search names the wrong history, is that the search\'s fault, the evidence\'s, or\\n   can the bearings not decide?\\n3. How much would a better identifier of histories gain?\\n4. How often is a manoeuvre declared on a target that made none?\\n5. Is a learned whole-period model worth the GPU budget? Only if the answer to 3 is material\\n   against 10%.\\n\\nIt was registered as a study, not a gate: nothing is carried or stopped by a threshold. The\\npanel\'s view is that the fifth question is in effect a gate with a failable line, and should\\nbe read as a rule\'s output.\\n\\n## The result on test seed 335\\n\\nFour hundred periods on Annex B data, 300 under a shifted manoeuvre prior, 400 in which the\\ntarget holds course, 100 each with correlated and outlying bearing errors, and 60 whole\\nengagements.\\n\\n| | Question | Forecast | Result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| D1 | Share of the gain from refitting C\u2033\'s times | 0.75 | 0.43, interval 0.28 to 0.58 |\\n| D2 | Wrong histories: ambiguous, search failure, evidence failure | 0.45, 0.10, 0.45 | 0.53, 0.08, 0.39 |\\n| D3 | Headroom with a perfect search; with a perfect evidence as well | inconclusive; material | 1.04, not material; 1.10 (1.04 to 1.18), inconclusive |\\n| D4 | False manoeuvres on held course: search, C\u2033, flat prior, mixed prior | 0.80, 0.03, 0.02, 0.10 | 0.76, 0.02, 0.01, 0.03 |\\n| D5 | Learned whole-period model | by the rule | no GPU budget |\\n\\nThree of the decided forecasts were wrong, all in the direction one tuning seed of 134\\nperiods had pointed.\\n\\n![Every system on three splits](/img/2026-11-13-time-late-classical-slides/systems.svg)\\n\\nThe search\'s gain over C\u2033 holds on a third seed: C\u2033 \xf7 search is 2.13 period by period\\n(1.88 to 2.43) on Annex B data, 1.53 under the shifted prior, and 4.09 on whole engagements.\\n\\n## Two defects in the registered code\\n\\nThe panel\'s code reviewer found faults that move decided numbers. They are recorded, not\\nrepaired: the code is registered, and a repair is a new registration.\\n\\n**The evidence over-credits small, short manoeuvres.** The prior says a minimum-size change\\nlasts 20 to 120 seconds, but the volume the evidence allows for a manoeuvre\'s duration was\\nleft at about 600 seconds. Each small manoeuvre gets a bonus it has not earned. The\\nmanoeuvres the search invents on quiet targets are exactly of this kind: speed changes of\\nabout a metre a second over twenty seconds. Re-ranking the stored candidates with a\\ncorrected volume gives a false-manoeuvre rate of 0.47 to 0.57, not 0.76. This is the fourth\\nerror of this family in the prior; the first three were found by Gate T\'s panel and fixed\\nhere, and all three penalised an extra manoeuvre. The one left rewards it.\\n\\n**The classing counted manoeuvres cut by the period\'s edge**, which the spec says to leave\\nout. Without them the wrong histories divide 0.57, 0.11, 0.33 and the search\'s first\\ncandidate is right in about 60% of periods, not 51%.\\n\\nA third weakness is not a bug but matters as much: the fit \\"told the true history\\" is free\\nto move its manoeuvre times, and in 13% of periods it moved them far enough to be a\\ndifferent history. The composite used for headroom is therefore a noisy oracle, not an\\nupper limit.\\n\\n## What the panel narrowed\\n\\n**The search\'s share is about half.** The registered 0.43 depends on four refits that ran\\naway numerically; without them it is 0.52, and by other reasonable definitions 0.39 to\\n0.58. Over three seeds, 0.47. Gate T\'s panel said \\"mostly smoothing\\"; the fair statement is\\n\\"about half\\", and the refit is handicapped because it inherits C\u2033\'s missed manoeuvres. The\\ncleaner split was sitting in the results: a search told nothing of how targets manoeuvre\\nobtains 0.78 of the gain. Annex B\'s statistics are worth about a fifth.\\n\\n![The decided quantities on three seeds](/img/2026-11-13-time-late-classical-slides/decided.svg)\\n\\n**The headroom is on the line, not below it.** With a perfect evidence it is 1.10 at the\\nregistered threshold, 1.18 and 1.05 at the other two thresholds registered as\\nsensitivities, 1.17 if the reference\'s times are held, and 1.25 if every wrong period is\\ncounted. \\"Inconclusive\\" is robust. \\"Little left to win\\", which the first draft said, is not.\\n\\n**\\"Ambiguous\\" does not mean the bearings cannot decide.** It means this approximate\\nevidence was within a factor of twenty, and the evidence\'s own noise is that large. On\\nthose periods the search\'s track is still twice as bad as the fit told the history.\\n\\n**The rule reads the easy case.** On Annex B data the search holds the simulator\'s own\\nmotion model, prior and noise law, so little headroom is expected. Under the shifted prior,\\ncorrelated errors and outliers the same statistic is 1.5 to 1.6, and no remedy of any kind\\nwas built: the prior-mismatch work in the spec was cut.\\n\\n![Correlated and outlying bearing errors](/img/2026-11-13-time-late-classical-slides/conditions.svg)\\n\\n## A target that never manoeuvres\\n\\n![False manoeuvres on held course](/img/2026-11-13-time-late-classical-slides/quiet.svg)\\n\\nOn 400 held-course periods the informed search declares a manoeuvre in 76% and its track is\\ntwice as bad as C\u2033\'s. Part of that is the defect above. Part is the prior: Annex B makes an\\nhour with no manoeuvre improbable, and over an hour the rate is 98 to 100%. But under thirty\\nminutes it is still 46%, and it happens on 4 of Annex B\'s own 11 quiet periods. A small\\nmanoeuvre the bearings cannot rule out costs nothing net under this evidence.\\n\\nA prior mixed half and half with \\"the target never manoeuvres\\" brings the rate to 3%. The\\nfirst draft said \\"at no cost\\". The panel showed the cost: it now calls a manoeuvring target\\nquiet in 7.8% of Annex B periods against 1.9%, and on targets that manoeuvre once, 20%\\nagainst 6%. The 3% also depends on the half. And the search with no manoeuvre statistics at\\nall already gives 1%.\\n\\n## The thinning idea\\n\\nThe owner asked whether runs of bearings at a steady bearing rate could be replaced by fewer\\nobservations (`experiments/20261112-tl2-thin/`, tuning seed only).\\n\\n![Thinning forms](/img/2026-11-13-time-late-classical-slides/thinning.svg)\\n\\n- Dropping bearings loses information and saves no time.\\n- Fixed normal points (one fitted bearing per bin) cost 1% to 9% and halve the search\'s run\\n  time, but the intervals reach 20% to 30%: not separated from free, nor from a real cost.\\n- Bins set by bearing-rate steadiness cost 25% to 50%. The panel showed why this says\\n  nothing about the idea: the rule that ends a bin was tripping on noise, so the bins were\\n  set by noise, not by the bearing rate.\\n- With 2% wild bearings, editing them out before forming points brought the search back to\\n  clean-data quality. The edit was not separated from the binning, and a robust cost in the\\n  fit was not tried.\\n\\nBy the rule written beforehand nothing was adopted. The rule needed more periods than were\\nrun, and that was knowable. The idea is not tested yet.\\n\\n## What this supports\\n\\nOn simulated data, with a fitting model that is the simulator\'s own:\\n\\n- A whole-period search halves C\u2033\'s track error where targets manoeuvre as Annex B says, and\\n  about half of that is available without a search.\\n- With the evidence as it stands, faults included, the search is 5% to 25% from a fit told\\n  the history\'s structure, depending on convention.\\n- That evidence invents small manoeuvres on quiet targets.\\n\\nIt does not show that learning cannot help, that half of wrong histories are beyond any\\nidentifier, or that 76% is what a sound evidence would do. It is silent on a model that\\nhedges across histories instead of naming one, which is what the spec 012 regressor did.\\n\\n## The recommendation, read fairly\\n\\nThe registered rule says no GPU budget for a learned whole-period model, and it was applied\\nas written. What the evidence says is narrower. Where targets behave exactly as the\\nsimulator says, a model trained to identify histories has about 10% to win, on the line.\\nWhere the prior or the error model is wrong, the gap is 50% to 60%, and neither a classical\\nnor a learned remedy has been tried.\\n\\n## What was not achieved\\n\\nThe prior-mismatch diagnostic and a broadened prior; an IMM smoother (the smoothed EKF that\\nwas run is no use on manoeuvring targets); the wild-bearing edit on test data; a test of the\\nowner\'s thinning idea that is not confounded by noise; a posterior-weighted track from the\\ncandidates. The per-row errors are on the results branch, not in the repository.\\n\\n## What comes next\\n\\nThe panel\'s order: repair the evidence, the classing and the reference and run again under\\na new registration; then the classical work against the real gaps (a robust likelihood in\\nthe search, a correlated-error model, a prior that can be wrong); then a hedged track as the\\nclassical control; and only then a learned model. The choice is the owner\'s.\\n\\nRecords: `experiments/20261112-tl2-decision/`, `20261112-tl2-tuning/`, `20261112-tl2-thin/`,\\n`20261112-tl2-val/`, `20261112-tl2/` (`review.md`)."},{"id":"/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-13-time-late-repair-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: time-late, repair and re-registration","description":"Spec 014\'s study reproduced, and its review panel found two defects in the code it was","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-015","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-015"}],"readingTime":2.64,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, repair and re-registration","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["kickoff","spec-015"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Time-late, the classical follow-on: what the search is worth, what it gets wrong, and two faults the panel found","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: time-late, repaired and re-registered","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-slides"}},"content":"Spec 014\'s study reproduced, and its review panel found two defects in the code it was\\nregistered on. The owner has chosen the smallest next step: repair them, and decide the same\\nquestions again on fresh seeds. Spec 015 trains nothing.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n- [Spec 014](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical) was decided by the panel in one round,\\n  five approve with findings. The whole-period search halves the track error of C\u2033; about\\n  half of that gain is available without a search; the search declared a manoeuvre on 76% of\\n  targets that made none.\\n- Carried: C and C\u2033, frozen; Gate T\'s and spec 014\'s searches, kept runnable as registered.\\n- Open: the evidence credits a manoeuvre\'s duration with about 600 seconds of room where the\\n  prior allows 20 to 120, which rewards small short manoeuvres (re-ranked, the 76% is 47% to\\n  57%). The classing counts manoeuvres cut by the period\'s edge. The fit \\"told the true\\n  history\\" drifts into another history in 13% of periods. What the decided numbers are\\n  without these faults is not known.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\n- SRD \xa75 and \xa79 (owner, 2026-11-13); the spec is `specs/015-time-late-repair/`.\\n- Judged by: every prior factor\'s density times its volume equal to 1 in a test; no reading\\n  built on a reference that fails the match rule; the four decided quantities stated again on\\n  a fresh test seed beside spec 014\'s, at every registered sensitivity value; tables\\n  generated from the summary file.\\n- Plan:\\n  1. A new prior version in which every factor without a Gaussian residual gets the volume\\n     its density implies; the two smaller inconsistencies fixed with it.\\n  2. One rule for cut manoeuvres, used by the classing, the candidates and the counts.\\n  3. A reference that is held to the true times when it drifts, and flagged.\\n  4. A refit control that falls back to C\u2033\'s track when its fit diverges.\\n  5. The effect of each repair on the spent seeds 333 to 335.\\n  6. Register (forecasts first), validation seed 336, test seed 337, panel.\\n- Risks: a fifth volume error; the repaired evidence may under-count manoeuvres more than\\n  the old one over-counted; forecasts from spent seeds may again be wrong. We stop, as\\n  before, if the reference is not sound on validation.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- False-manoeuvre rate on held course by period length, old evidence against repaired\\n  (test seed 337).\\n- The four decided quantities, spec 014 beside spec 015, with intervals.\\n- Headroom at each class threshold and on each split.\\n- Manoeuvring targets declared quiet, by number of true manoeuvres, for each prior.\\n- All from committed experiment folders by a script named in the slides post.\\n\\n**Update, 2026-11-13 (later).** The study ran: validation seed 336 passed soundness and test\\nseed 337 is scored. The registered figures reproduce; the rule again gives no GPU budget; the\\npanel approved with findings and showed the headroom figure depends most on how the repaired\\nreference is fitted. See [the write-up](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair) and\\n[the slides](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-slides)."},{"id":"/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-13-time-late-repair-slides.md","title":"Slides: time-late, repaired and re-registered","description":"The illustrated companion to the spec 015 write-up: the","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-015","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-015"}],"readingTime":1.35,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: time-late, repaired and re-registered","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","spec-015"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, repair and re-registration","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Time-late, repaired and re-registered: the numbers hold, the rule says no, and the reference turns out to matter most","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the spec 015 write-up](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair): the\\nthree repairs, the decided quantities on fresh seeds, a target that never manoeuvres by\\nperiod length, the headroom at every registered sensitivity, and what the review panel\\nnarrowed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-13-time-late-repair-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **A target that never manoeuvres**: false-manoeuvre rate by period length, spec 014\'s\\n  search against the repaired one, with the flat and mixed priors and C\u2033 (test seed 337).\\n- **The decided quantities on three seeds**: the refit\'s share of the gain and the two\\n  headroom figures, with intervals and the 10% line (development seed 335 is spent data).\\n- **Headroom by sensitivity and split**: the perfect-evidence figure at each class threshold\\n  and match tolerance, and on every split. The panel showed it also moves with how the\\n  reference is fitted, which this figure does not show.\\n- **Manoeuvring targets called quiet**, by number of true manoeuvres: the cost of the repair\\n  and of the mixed prior.\\n\\nThere are no track plots: the per-period tracks are not stored, only their errors.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\n```\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl3_charts.py --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-13-time-late-repair-slides\\n```\\n\\nThe script reads only committed records (`experiments/20261113-tl3-dev/`,\\n`20261113-tl3-val/`, `20261113-tl3/`); each figure\'s `.json` sidecar names the inputs\'\\ndigests and the commit. The data are seeds 335 (development, spent), 336 (validation) and\\n337 (test), 5-second bearings, ranges files under `configs/timelate/`, generated in the CI\\nruns."},{"id":"/2026/11/13/time-late-repair","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-13-time-late-repair.md","title":"Time-late, repaired and re-registered: the numbers hold, the rule says no, and the reference turns out to matter most","description":"Spec 014\'s panel found two defects in registered code: the evidence gave a manoeuvre\'s","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-015","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-015"}],"readingTime":6.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Time-late, repaired and re-registered: the numbers hold, the rule says no, and the reference turns out to matter most","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["write-up","spec-015"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: time-late, repaired and re-registered","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: Gate T, the track after the fact","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t-slides"}},"content":"Spec 014\'s panel found two defects in registered code: the evidence gave a manoeuvre\'s\\nduration far too much volume, and the classing counted manoeuvres cut by a period\'s edge. It\\nalso found the true-history reference drifting into other histories. The owner chose to\\nrepair and re-register. Spec 015 fixed the three, decided the same five quantities on fresh\\nseeds (336 validation, 337 test) and trained nothing. The registered figures reproduce, and\\nthe registered rule again says no GPU budget. The panel approved with findings, and its main\\none is that the headroom figure now depends most on the thing we repaired last: how the\\nreference is fitted. The illustrated companion is\\n[the slides post](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair-slides).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was repaired\\n\\n- **The evidence** (prior version 3). Every factor of the prior that has no Gaussian\\n  residual now gets a Laplace volume equal to one over its density at the fit, so a density\\n  and its volume cancel when the bearings say nothing. Spec 014\'s version kept about 600 s\\n  of volume for a duration whose density is tens of seconds wide.\\n- **One edge rule** (`timelate/match.py`). A manoeuvre cut by the period\'s start or end is\\n  neither required nor counted against a history, wherever two histories are compared.\\n- **The reference.** If the true-history fit moves a manoeuvre more than 120 s from the\\n  truth it is refitted with the times held, and the period is flagged.\\n- **Tables** are generated from `summary.json` by a script; a test checks them.\\n\\n## What was decided (test seed 337, Annex B data, 364 scored periods)\\n\\n| | Quantity | Spec 014 (seed 335) | Spec 015 (seed 337) |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| D1 | Share of the search\'s gain obtained by refitting C\u2033\'s own manoeuvre times | 0.43 [0.28, 0.58] | 0.33 [0.20, 0.45] |\\n| D2 | First candidate right | about 0.60 edge-aware | 0.64 [0.59, 0.69] |\\n| D2 | Wrong histories: ambiguous / search failure / evidence failure | 0.57 / 0.11 / 0.33 edge-aware | 0.44 / 0.13 / 0.43 |\\n| D3 | Headroom with a perfect search | 1.04 | 1.06 [1.01, 1.11], inconclusive |\\n| D3 | Headroom with a perfect evidence as well | 1.10 [1.04, 1.18] | 1.17 [1.10, 1.25], inconclusive (lower limit 1.099) |\\n| D4 | Held-course periods given a manoeuvre: search | 0.76 | 0.53 [0.48, 0.58] |\\n| D4 | ... C\u2033 / flat prior / mixed prior | 0.02 / 0.01 / 0.03 | 0.02 / 0.01 / 0.00 |\\n| D5 | A learned whole-period model | no GPU budget by the rule | no GPU budget by the rule |\\n\\nC\u2033 read as a track against the repaired search is 2.10 [1.82, 2.44] paired per period (0.083\\nagainst 0.039 in median period range error); the true history with the prior is at 0.028.\\nSoundness on both fresh seeds passed.\\n\\n## What the panel made of it\\n\\nFive reviewers, all approve with findings, one round (`experiments/20261113-tl3/review.md`).\\nThey recomputed every decided number from the per-row errors and found no bug that changes\\none. What they changed is what the numbers mean.\\n\\n**The headroom figure rests on the repaired reference.** In the composite that stands for \\"a\\nperfect evidence\\", 73 of 364 periods are replaced by the true-history fit. Nineteen of those\\nfits were held to the true times, and they carry a third of the gain. A fit told when the\\ntarget manoeuvred knows more than which history is right. By reference convention the figure\\nruns from 1.11 to 1.19, and only \\"times held everywhere\\" clears the line. Ten periods carry\\nover half of it; several are runaways of the search that a guard might remove. So the first\\nrecord\'s \\"between 10% and 30% to win, most likely 17%\\" is withdrawn: an oracle is 8% to 30%\\nahead depending on convention, and what a real identifier would obtain is less and unmeasured.\\n\\n**The rule says no, and the first record tried to soften that.** The lower limit missed the\\n10% line by a thousandth, and the record called the decision \\"a judgement, and the owner\'s\\".\\nThe panel: the rule was written so that inconclusive counts as not material; an override is\\na departure from it, not a reading of it. It also notes that a rule we forecast as a coin\'s\\ntoss should not have been registered as a decision instrument: at 364 periods it could\\nbarely decide.\\n\\n**The repair of the evidence is real and not finished.** On held course the false-manoeuvre\\nrate fell from 0.80 (spec 014\'s search on the same periods) to 0.53, and under 30 minutes\\nfrom 0.50 to 0.09. A reviewer integrated the evidence numerically on 34 periods: the\\nremaining rate is the prior\'s (Annex B says a target probably manoeuvres within the hour),\\nnot approximation error. But the same integration shows the low rate on short periods is\\nnot what a correct evidence gives either: summed over where a manoeuvre might be, the prior\\nfavours \\"some manoeuvre\\" almost everywhere, because the bearings are silent about it. And\\nthe new pairing fails at the edges of the prior\'s support, where most invented manoeuvres\\nsit (1 to 3.5 nats). That is the fifth error of this family.\\n\\n**The repair is not free.** The repaired search calls a one-manoeuvre target quiet in 13% of\\ncases against 4% before. Those are not suppressed manoeuvres: integrated numerically they\\nare within a nat of undecidable. A mixed prior with a stated half chance of a quiet target\\nremoves the false manoeuvres (0.00) and raises the misses to 20%; re-ranked at a 1% chance\\nit gives 0.09 and 14%. The right setting is a small one, and no split here has a mixed\\npopulation to tune it on.\\n\\n**D1 is \\"between a third and a half\\"** and mostly restates how often C\u2033 has the history\\nright: there the refit obtains all of the search\'s gain, elsewhere a quarter.\\n\\n**D2\'s split between \\"ambiguous\\" and \\"evidence failure\\" is the threshold\'s.** At thresholds\\n1, 3 and 5 the ambiguous share is 0.11, 0.44 and 0.66. Only \\"search failure is the smallest\\nclass\\" survives.\\n\\n## What was not delivered\\n\\n- The effect of each repair on each decided quantity on the same periods: only D4 and the\\n  first candidate were shown, and the repairs were not separated. The panel separated them\\n  for the first candidate (edge rule +0.10, evidence +0.04 to +0.05).\\n- D2 at every registered sensitivity (the panel supplied it).\\n- Unit tests for the code that computes the decided statistics.\\n- The refit\'s fall-back, built to catch runaway refits, caught one of seven.\\n- The forecasts held in five of seven rows, but they restated a development run made\\n  minutes earlier; the one forecast of a quantity known to move, D1, missed.\\n\\n## Where this leaves a learned model\\n\\nNothing here shows that learning cannot help, nor that it would. Where the simulator\'s\\nassumptions hold (Annex B statistics, independent Gaussian errors) an oracle is 10% to 20%\\nahead of the search and 400 periods cannot decide a 10% line. Where they fail the gaps are\\nlarge: the search is 1.7 times the oracle under a shifted prior, 1.4 under correlated errors,\\n1.5 with outliers. No classical remedy has been tried on any of the three, so a learned win\\nthere today could not be told from a missing Huber cost or a broader prior. This project has\\nbeen caught that way three times.\\n\\nThe panel\'s order, for the owner to choose from:\\n\\n1. A hedged track from the stored candidates and a declaration margin: cheap, classical.\\n2. A robust likelihood, a correlated-error likelihood and a prior that can be wrong, scored\\n   on the shifted, correlated and outlier splits at more than one severity.\\n3. Then a learned whole-period model, registered against 1 and 2 on those splits.\\n4. No fourth Annex B identification study.\\n\\nNo GPU run starts without the owner.\\n\\n## Records\\n\\n`experiments/20261113-tl3-decision/` (registration), `-dev/`, `-val/`,\\n`experiments/20261113-tl3/` (test record, `tables.md`, `review.md`). Seeds 336 and 337 are\\nused; the next fresh seed is 338."},{"id":"/2026/11/12/gate-t-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-12-gate-t-slides.md","title":"Slides: Gate T, the track after the fact","description":"The illustrated companion to the Gate T write-up: the question","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-t","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-t"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-013","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-013"}],"readingTime":1.68,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: Gate T, the track after the fact","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","gate-t","spec-013"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Time-late, repaired and re-registered: the numbers hold, the rule says no, and the reference turns out to matter most","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-repair"},"nextItem":{"title":"Gate T: a classical search over manoeuvre histories halves the track error, and what that does not show","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the Gate T write-up](/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t): the question\\n(the whole track over a period, after the fact), why a test of fit cannot answer it, the\\nclassical search that uses the owner\'s manoeuvre statistics, the gate on test seed 331, and\\nwhat the review panel narrowed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-12-gate-t-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Two histories, the same bearings.** One tuning period in which a history with no\\n  manoeuvre fits the bearings as well as the true one.\\n- **Told the manoeuvres, the arc model reaches the bound.** Tuning seed 329: the chord\\n  solve and the arc solve against the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound.\\n- **The ratio on three seeds.** C\u2033 \xf7 search with its 95% interval, on Annex B data and the\\n  shifted prior, beside the registered thresholds.\\n- **Every system as a track.** Median period range error on the test seed.\\n- **The reference by how tight the bound is.** The fit from neutral starts against the fit\\n  started at the truth, which sits below the bound where the bound is loose.\\n- **Stated probability against hit rate**, and **manoeuvres found minus true**.\\n\\nThere are no track plots with uncertainty ellipses: the search states candidate tracks\\nwith probabilities and no covariance.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\n```\\nuv run python scripts/research/gt_charts.py --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-12-gate-t-slides\\nuv run python scripts/research/tl_charts.py --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-12-time-late-slides --data <dir>\\n```\\n\\nThe first script reads only committed records (`experiments/20261112-gt-val/`,\\n`20261112-gt2-val/`, `20261112-gt2/`); the second made the first two figures from tuning\\nrecords and, for the ambiguity figure, from tuning seed 329 (count 400,\\n`configs/timelate/tl-ranges.json`). Each figure\'s `.json` sidecar names the inputs\' digests\\nand the commit. The gate\'s data are seeds 330 and 332 (validation) and 331 (test), 5-second\\nbearings, `configs/timelate/tl-ranges.json` and `tl-shift-ranges.json`, generated in the\\nscoring runs."},{"id":"/2026/11/12/gate-t","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-12-gate-t.md","title":"Gate T: a classical search over manoeuvre histories halves the track error, and what that does not show","description":"Spec 013 asked a different question from every gate before it: not \\"where is the target","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-t","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-t"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-013","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-013"}],"readingTime":8.24,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Gate T: a classical search over manoeuvre histories halves the track error, and what that does not show","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["write-up","gate-t","spec-013"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: Gate T, the track after the fact","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: the scaled regressor","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor-slides"}},"content":"Spec 013 asked a different question from every gate before it: not \\"where is the target\\nnow?\\" but \\"what did the target do over the last half hour?\\", answered after the fact with\\nall the bearings in hand. Nothing was trained. A classical search over manoeuvre histories,\\nscored by how probable each history is given the bearings and the owner\'s manoeuvre\\nstatistics, was put through a gate on a fresh test seed. It passed five of six checks, and\\nthe one it failed was forecast. The review panel accepted the measurements and narrowed\\nmost of what was first said about them. This post reports both. The illustrated companion\\nis [the slides post](/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t-slides).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was built\\n\\nPeriods of 20 to 60 minutes are cut at random from longer simulated engagements, with a\\nbearing every 5 seconds. Each system must give the target\'s position, course and speed at\\nevery moment of the period. A period is scored by its median relative range error over the\\nrows the bearings can determine; a system by the median over periods.\\n\\n- **C\u2033 read as a track**: the standing classical system (a causal segmenter and a\\n  chord-model solve), its answer read back over the period. It uses no knowledge of how\\n  targets manoeuvre.\\n- **The search**: a set of manoeuvre histories (how many manoeuvres, when, of which kind),\\n  each fitted with a model in which a turn is an arc at a constant rate and a speed change\\n  is at a constant rate, and scored by a Laplace approximation to its evidence under Annex\\n  B\'s manoeuvre statistics. The best few are returned as candidate tracks with\\n  probabilities.\\n- **The reference**: the same fit told the true history\'s structure, started from neutral\\n  guesses.\\n\\nHow the path got here is in [the kickoff post](/blog/2026/11/11/time-late-kickoff) and its\\ndated updates. Two things from it matter for reading the result. First, a test of fit alone\\ncannot find the history: wrong histories fit the bearings as well as the true one, so the\\nmanoeuvre statistics are what separate them. Second, one reading along the way was\\nwithdrawn because the evidence had been evaluated at the wrong fit; the histogram of\\nper-period differences is what exposed it.\\n\\n## Two registrations\\n\\nThe gate was registered twice. The first (`experiments/20261112-gt-decision/`) stopped at\\nvalidation on its own first check: the reference scored 0.67 of the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound, and\\nanything below 1 is a fault. The reference fit had been started at the truth and, where the\\nbearings pin the target down weakly, it stayed there. By the rule written beforehand the\\ngate stopped and the test seed was not generated.\\n\\nThe owner chose to re-register (`experiments/20261112-gt2-decision/`): a reference fitted\\nfrom neutral starts, the soundness check read on well-determined rows, a calibration check\\nadded on the shifted split with a forecast of \\"fail\\", a new validation seed (332), and\\nevery threshold unchanged. The comparison for headroom became harder for the search to\\nshow room against. The plan, run configurations and the time-late code were last changed\\nbefore validation ran.\\n\\n## The result on test seed 331\\n\\nThree hundred periods on Annex B data and three hundred from a split whose targets\\nmanoeuvre more often and by less than the search assumes.\\n\\n| Check | Question | Result | Outcome |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| T-a | Is the reference at the bound where the bound is tight? | 1.05 against a band of 0.8 to 1.3 | pass |\\n| T-b | C\u2033 \xf7 search above 1.10? | 2.27, interval 1.76 to 2.67 | pass |\\n| T-c | The same under the shifted prior, above 1.00? | 1.69, interval 1.39 to 2.14 | pass |\\n| T-d | True history in the first three candidates at least 0.60; calibration gap at most 0.10 | 0.71; \u22120.03 | pass |\\n| T-d-shift | Calibration gap at most 0.10 under the shifted prior | +0.32 | fail, as forecast |\\n| T-e | Search \xf7 reference above 1.10? | 1.46, interval 1.24 to 1.91 | material |\\n\\nIn plain terms: C\u2033 read as a track has a median period range error of 0.088; the search\\n0.039; the reference 0.022 with times held. Under the shifted prior, 0.145, 0.086 and 0.040.\\n\\n![C\u2033 \xf7 search on three seeds and two splits](/img/2026-11-12-gate-t-slides/ratios.svg)\\n\\n## What the panel made of it\\n\\nFive reviewers re-computed the figures from the raw files and checked the order of commits.\\nAll five approved with findings (`experiments/20261112-gt2/review.md`). The measurements\\nstand. Most of the first interpretation does not.\\n\\n**The headline is a ratio of medians, not a typical gain.** Period by period, the median\\nratio of C\u2033 to the search is 1.68 on Annex B data and 1.53 under the shift. The search is\\nbetter than C\u2033 in about 70% of periods, worse in about 30%, and more than twice as bad in\\n12% (8% under the shift).\\n\\n**T-b is mostly smoothing, not finding histories.** Where the search\'s first candidate is\\nthe true history it is at 0.032 against C\u2033\'s 0.081; where it is wrong, 0.057 against 0.093.\\nIt wins either way. So most of the gain comes from the arc model, the prior and the use of\\nthe whole period, which C\u2033, a causal filter with none of them, cannot have. The control\\nthat would separate the search from those (C\u2033\'s own manoeuvre times refitted with the same\\nmodel and prior) was not in the gate.\\n\\n**T-c is one draw of a variable quantity.** On three seeds the shifted ratio was 1.70, 1.26\\n(interval 0.89 to 1.84, which would have failed) and 1.69. The shift also makes the task\\nharder in itself (3.2 manoeuvres a period against 1.9), and no search with the right\\nshifted prior was run, so the cost of the wrong prior is not separated from the cost of\\nbusier targets. What holds: at this shift the search remains better than C\u2033.\\n\\n**The search under-counts manoeuvres.** On Annex B data it misses 23% of true manoeuvres\\nand reports the right number in 73% of periods; under the shift it misses 41% and reports\\ntoo few in 67%. In the twelve periods with no manoeuvre it invents one in seven of them.\\n\\n![Manoeuvres found minus true](/img/2026-11-12-gate-t-slides/counts.svg)\\n\\n**The candidate probabilities are honest on average only.** On Annex B data the stated\\nprobability matches the hit rate overall and by stated-probability bin. It does not by\\nperiod complexity: with one true manoeuvre the first candidate is right 77% of the time\\nand claims 53%; with three or more, 40% against 56%. The check passes at its registered\\nsettings (a 120-second match, temperature 3) and not at a 60-second match. Under the\\nshifted prior no temperature calibrates them: they carry a ranking and no probability, and\\nnothing in the output tells an analyst which regime applies.\\n\\n![Stated probability against hit rate](/img/2026-11-12-gate-t-slides/calibration.svg)\\n\\n**The headroom is an upper limit.** 1.46 is the cost of not knowing the history. Where the\\nsearch names the true history it already matches the reference (1.01); the whole gap sits\\nin the periods where it does not, and an untested share of those are ambiguous in the\\nbearings themselves. Period by period the figure is 1.17, interval 1.01 to 1.32, which\\nwould not clear the 1.10 line.\\n\\n**The soundness check is weaker than registered.** On the tightly bounded rows it reads,\\nthe faulty truth-started fit would also have passed (0.91). It was the loosely bounded rows\\nthat exposed the fault.\\n\\n![The reference by how tight the bound is](/img/2026-11-12-gate-t-slides/reference.svg)\\n\\n## What this supports\\n\\nOn simulated data with independent Gaussian bearing errors of 1\xb0, and a fitting model that\\nis the generator\'s own:\\n\\n- A whole-period fit with an arc model and the manoeuvre prior gives a track with about\\n  half the median range error of C\u2033 read as a track, when targets manoeuvre as the prior\\n  says, and remains better under one moderate shift of the prior.\\n- Its ranked candidates contain the true history in the first three in about 70% of\\n  periods when the prior is right, and in about 35% when it is not.\\n- Its probabilities are usable on average when the prior is right and not otherwise.\\n\\nIt does not support any claim that the search finds histories well, that the result\\nsurvives realistic bearing errors, or that there is a known amount left for a learned\\nmodel to win.\\n\\n## What was not achieved\\n\\nSpec 013 promised more than the gate delivered. None of these was run; all are owed:\\n\\n- The owner\'s bearing-rate thinning idea (dropping, normal points, adaptive bins).\\n- Manoeuvre counts and timing error as gate outputs; splits by ownship manoeuvre, position\\n  in the period and range band.\\n- A correlated-error variant scored beside; whole engagements as a stratum; a smoothed IMM\\n  as a classical whole-period line (the forward filter is silent on 60% of rows).\\n- Periods where the target never manoeuvres, and the false-manoeuvre rate there. This is\\n  the commonest real case, and the worst false alarm for a system whose prior expects\\n  manoeuvres.\\n\\nThe prior\'s code also has three small errors, each penalising an extra manoeuvre slightly\\n(at most about a third of a nat). They are recorded and not yet fixed, because fixing them\\nchanges the registered code.\\n\\n## What comes next\\n\\nThe panel\'s order is: the owed classical studies and controls first, then a search that is\\nrobust to a wrong prior with a diagnostic for it, and only then a learned whole-period\\nmodel, registered against the search under the same prior. That is the recommended\\nfollow-on, and the choice is the owner\'s. No GPU run starts before it is made.\\n\\nRecords: `experiments/20261112-gt-decision/`, `20261112-gt-val/` (first registration),\\n`20261112-gt2-decision/`, `20261112-gt2-val/`, `20261112-gt2/` (test record, `review.md`)."},{"id":"/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-12-scaled-regressor-slides.md","title":"Slides: the scaled regressor","description":"The illustrated companion to the spec 012 write-up: a","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-012","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-012"}],"readingTime":1.42,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: the scaled regressor","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","spec-012"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Gate T: a classical search over manoeuvre histories halves the track error, and what that does not show","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t"},"nextItem":{"title":"The scaled regressor: a learned control that beat the classical system, and what that does not show","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the spec 012 write-up](/blog/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor): a\\nlearned control trained on a rented GPU, its two registered readings on the test seed, what\\nthe review panel withdrew, and the classical control that did not explain the result.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-12-scaled-regressor-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Held course.** Error against the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound by geometry quartile, least squares\\n  beside the regressor (test seed 328, 7,816 rows). The regressor is furthest from the bound\\n  where the geometry is best and below it where the geometry is worst.\\n- **After manoeuvres.** Median relative range error by true-range quartile, regressor against\\n  C\u2033, on the test seed and on the split with longer initial ranges. The margin grows with\\n  range on one and vanishes at the far end of the other.\\n- **Efficiency and the control.** Error against the bound on settled rows after manoeuvres\\n  (the regressor is worse than C\u2033 in course), and the registered range-prior control beside\\n  C\u2033 and the regressor.\\n\\nThere are no track plots: the regressor is a control that states no covariance and no\\nmanoeuvre times, so there is no track with an uncertainty to draw beside the classical one.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\n```\\nuv run python scripts/research/r_charts.py --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-12-scaled-regressor-slides\\n```\\n\\nThe script reads only committed records (`experiments/20261111-r-test/`,\\n`experiments/20261112-r-control/test.json`); each figure\'s `.json` sidecar names the inputs\'\\ndigests and the commit. The data behind those records are test seed 328 (5,600 engagements,\\n`configs/man2-ranges.json` and `configs/regress/held-course-ranges.json`), generated in the\\nscoring runs."},{"id":"/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-12-scaled-regressor.md","title":"The scaled regressor: a learned control that beat the classical system, and what that does not show","description":"Spec 012 trained a direct sequence-to-state model on about twenty million simulated","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-012","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-012"}],"readingTime":6.25,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"The scaled regressor: a learned control that beat the classical system, and what that does not show","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["write-up","spec-012"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: the scaled regressor","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, the classical follow-on to Gate T","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/time-late-classical-kickoff"}},"content":"Spec 012 trained a direct sequence-to-state model on about twenty million simulated\\nengagements, on a rented GPU, as a control: what does a learned model with no physics layer\\nand no segmenter reach, and what does it not? On a fresh test seed it is partial against the\\nbound on a steady target and it beats the standing classical system after target manoeuvres.\\nThe second result is the first time in this project that something learned has clearly beaten\\nthe classical line. The review panel accepted the measurements and rejected most of what was\\nfirst said about them. This post reports both.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was built\\n\\nA causal transformer (8 layers, width 256, 6.4 million parameters) reads the bearings and the\\nownship\'s track and outputs range, course and speed with a stated spread for range and speed.\\nIt never sees a physics layer. Training data are drawn on the fly from generator 0.6.0 (seed\\n326), a fifth of them with the target holding course. Three seeds, a model trained on a tenth\\nof the data, and a model that sees only the ownship\'s track were trained on Tesla T4s through\\nModal: 38 GPU-hours, $25.81 of the owner\'s $50 budget. Two of the three seeds stopped at 91%\\nand 97% of their planned updates when their time limits ran out; this was disclosed before\\nscoring and not repaired.\\n\\nTwo readings were registered before any scored run, with thresholds\\n(`experiments/20261111-r-decision/`): efficiency against the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound where the target\\nholds course (reaches: below 1.25 in range, course and speed; floor: above 2.0), and paired\\ndifferences from the standing classical system C\u2033 and the tuned IMM after manoeuvres.\\nForecasts were written down too.\\n\\n## The readings on test seed 328\\n\\n**Held course: partial, as forecast.** Error as a multiple of the bound, 7,816 rows from\\n1,990 engagements:\\n\\n| System | Range | Course | Speed |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Least squares | 0.99 | 0.97 | 0.98 |\\n| Regressor, three seeds | 1.13 to 1.16 | 1.74 to 1.81 | 1.42 to 1.43 |\\n| A tenth of the data | 1.22 | 2.05 | 1.57 |\\n| Ownship only | 11.5 | 28.7 | 15.6 |\\n\\nThe pooled range figure flatters the model. By geometry quartile it runs 1.81, 1.20, 1.00\\nand 0.84 from best to worst: furthest from the bound where the bearings fix the target best,\\nand below the bound where they fix it worst. An unbiased estimator cannot be below the bound.\\nThe model is biased toward what the simulator usually does, and where the bearings say\\nlittle that bias helps.\\n\\n**After manoeuvres: the regressor beats C\u2033, against the forecast.** Median relative range\\nerror on rows both answer, with engagement-clustered 95% intervals:\\n\\n| | Regressor (seed 1) | C\u2033 | Difference |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Range | 0.066 | 0.105 | \u22120.039 [\u22120.045, \u22120.033] |\\n| Range, metres | 1,008 | 1,665 | \u2212657 [\u2212765, \u2212565] |\\n| Course | 9.6\xb0 | 12.7\xb0 | \u22123.1\xb0 [\u22124.0, \u22122.3] |\\n| Speed | 1.34 kt | 1.47 kt | \u22120.14 kt [\u22120.23, \u22120.05] |\\n\\nSeeds 2 and 3 agree (range differences \u22120.038). The forecast was \\"worse than C\u2033, better than\\nthe IMM\\"; the first half was wrong on validation and wrong again on test.\\n\\nMedians are not the whole picture. Against the bound on settled rows the regressor is better\\nthan C\u2033 in range (1.71 against 2.52), about level in speed (2.48 against 2.63) and **worse\\nin course** (3.40 against 3.19). The solve told the true manoeuvre times is at 1.65, 2.04\\nand 1.86.\\n\\n## What the panel made of it\\n\\nThree independent reviewers; all three approved with findings\\n(`experiments/20261111-r-test/review.md`). The measurements were checked against the raw\\nfiles and stand. Four claims in the first draft of the record do not:\\n\\n1. **\\"The first clear result for learning\\" was too broad.** The model knows the simulator\'s\\n   ranges, speeds and manoeuvre statistics, and its loss targets the quantity that is scored.\\n   C\u2033 uses none of that. The supportable statement is narrower: a prior-informed estimator\\n   trained to minimise the scored error beats a prior-free fit, in median range.\\n2. **\\"It holds on ranges the model never saw\\" was wrong.** The second test split differs only\\n   in initial range (20 to 30 km against 2 to 20 km); the manoeuvre statistics are the same\\n   and the realised ranges overlap training. On it the margin falls from \u22120.054 in the\\n   nearest quartile to \u22120.014 [\u22120.050, +0.025] in the farthest: it vanishes exactly where the\\n   model has to extrapolate. On the test seed the margin grows with range.\\n3. **The explanation offered was untested.** The draft said the model probably hedges between\\n   manoeuvre histories that the bearings cannot separate. That was carried over from spec\\n   013\'s tuning data, has not been tested on this model, and the far-range collapse is not\\n   what hedging predicts.\\n4. **\\"More data or a bigger model would not help\\" did not follow.** The tenth-of-data model\\n   varies data, not capacity, and on held course it is clearly worse.\\n\\n## The control the panel asked for\\n\\nIf the margin were simply C\u2033\'s answer pulled toward the usual range, a classical rule could\\nreproduce it. We built that rule: C\u2033\'s range combined, by precision weighting, with a range\\nprior (either one fixed distribution or the ownship-only model\'s output). Its one setting was\\nfitted on the validation seed and committed before the rule touched the test seed\\n(`experiments/20261112-r-control/`).\\n\\n| System | Median range error, test seed |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| C\u2033 | 0.103 |\\n| C\u2033 with a fixed range prior | 0.104 |\\n| C\u2033 with the ownship-only prior | 0.102 |\\n| Regressor | 0.066 |\\n\\nThe control closes about 3% of the margin. **It is not simple shrinkage.** The rule has a\\nknown weakness: C\u2033\'s stated uncertainty is overconfident exactly when it has missed a\\nmanoeuvre, so a precision weighting cannot shrink where shrinking would matter. What the\\ncontrol rules out is the easy explanation, not every use of prior knowledge.\\n\\n## What this supports\\n\\n- After manoeuvres, on this simulator, a model that has learnt the simulator is better in\\n  median range than the best classical system we have, by about 650 m at these ranges, and\\n  the gap is not explained by a range prior bolted onto the classical answer.\\n- It is still a control and is not carried. It states no usable uncertainty, gives no\\n  manoeuvre times, is 1.75 times the bound in course on a steady target (3.2 times in the\\n  best geometry), and is worse than C\u2033 in course efficiency after manoeuvres.\\n- Nothing here supports a claim outside the simulator. The model\'s advantage may be made of\\n  knowing Annex B\'s statistics exactly, which no real target obeys.\\n\\n## What was not achieved\\n\\n- The source of the margin. The second control the panel asked for (C\u2033 hedged between its\\n  histories by evidence) is not built.\\n- A naive estimator on the same rows, which the specification requires, is missing from\\n  this scorer\'s output.\\n- The seeds are reported singly, not pooled as registered.\\n- The larger-model run (an addendum to the decision record) was not run; the owner paused\\n  GPU work to turn to the time-late problem.\\n\\n## What comes next\\n\\nSpec 013 (time-late) is now building the classical estimator this result needs as its\\nproper comparison: a search over manoeuvre histories scored by evidence under the same\\nmanoeuvre statistics the regressor learnt. A learned model that beats *that* would be a\\nresult about learning. One that only beats a prior-free fit is a result about priors. The\\nremaining GPU budget, about $24, is held until that control exists.\\n\\nRecords: `experiments/20261111-r-decision/`, `20261111-r-runs/`, `20261111-r-val/`,\\n`20261111-r-test/` (with `review.md`), `20261112-r-control/`. Slides:\\n[the illustrated companion](/blog/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor-slides)."},{"id":"/2026/11/12/time-late-classical-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/time-late-classical-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-12-time-late-classical-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: time-late, the classical follow-on to Gate T","description":"Gate T showed that a classical search over manoeuvre histories halves the track error of the","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-014","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-014"}],"readingTime":3.69,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, the classical follow-on to Gate T","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["kickoff","spec-014"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The scaled regressor: a learned control that beat the classical system, and what that does not show","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/scaled-regressor"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: the robust segmenter study","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter-slides"}},"content":"Gate T showed that a classical search over manoeuvre histories halves the track error of the\\nstanding classical system, and the review panel showed that this does not yet say where the\\ngain comes from or how much is left to win. The owner has chosen classical work first: spec\\n014 trains nothing, runs the studies spec 013 owed, and turns Gate T\'s upper limit on headroom\\ninto a figure a decision on a learned model can rest on.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n- [Gate T](/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t) was decided by the panel in one round: five of six checks\\n  passed, the sixth failed as forecast. The search is at 0.039 in median period range error\\n  against 0.088 for C\u2033 read as a track, and 0.022 for the reference.\\n- Carried: C and C\u2033, frozen; the search as registered for Gate T. Nothing learned is carried\\n  into the time-late problem.\\n- Open: the search wins even where it names the wrong history, so the gain may be mostly the\\n  arc model, the prior and the whole period. The headroom of 1.46 is an upper limit. The\\n  search misses a quarter of manoeuvres and declares one in 7 of 12 periods that had none. Its\\n  probabilities mean nothing when the target does not manoeuvre as assumed. The owner\'s\\n  bearing-rate thinning idea has not been tested.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\n- SRD \xa75 (Gate T row and the rules it inherits) and \xa79 (owner, 2026-11-12); the spec is\\n  `specs/014-time-late-classical/`.\\n- Judged by: a stated share of the gain that refitting C\u2033\'s own manoeuvre times obtains; the\\n  share of wrong-history periods that are ambiguous in the bearings, and an attainable\\n  headroom judged against 10%; a false-manoeuvre rate on targets that hold course, to within\\n  5 points; a thinning verdict by a rule written beforehand; and one recommendation on a\\n  learned whole-period model.\\n- Plan:\\n  1. Correct the three small errors in the prior under a new code digest and report the effect.\\n  2. Build the two controls (C\u2033 refitted with the arc model and prior; the search with an\\n     uninformative prior) and the classing of wrong-history periods, on tuning seed 333.\\n  3. Run the thinning study and apply its adoption rule.\\n  4. Add the scored outputs and conditions Gate T lacked: manoeuvre counts and timing, splits,\\n     held-course periods, correlated and outlying errors, whole engagements, a smoothed filter.\\n  5. Register what is decided on fresh seeds, with forecasts; validation seed 334, then test\\n     seed 335; panel.\\n  6. If time allows: a prior-mismatch diagnostic and a broadened prior, reported only.\\n- Risks: the search takes minutes a period, so run time limits split sizes; the classing rule\\n  may leave most wrong periods \\"ambiguous\\" by construction and must be fixed on tuning data;\\n  a reference fitted from neutral starts may fail to converge on hard periods. We stop, as\\n  Gate T did, if the references are not sound on validation.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- A bar chart of track error: C\u2033, C\u2033 refitted, flat-prior search, search, reference, with\\n  paired per-period ratios (test seed 335).\\n- The classes of wrong-history periods and the attainable headroom beside Gate T\'s 1.46.\\n- False-manoeuvre rate on held-course periods for the search and C\u2033, with intervals.\\n- Accuracy against run time for each thinning form, clean and with outliers (tuning seed 333).\\n- Track plots of one ambiguous period and one search failure, both candidate tracks drawn.\\n- All from committed experiment folders; scripts and commits named in the slides post.\\n\\n## Update, 13 November: the study is decided\\n\\nThe registered study ran on a fresh test seed and went to the panel: five approve with\\nfindings. The search\'s gain over the standing classical system held (a factor of 2.1), and\\nabout half of it is available without a search. On targets that never manoeuvre the search\\ninvents a manoeuvre three times in four. The panel found two defects in the registered code,\\none of which inflates that last figure, and narrowed most of the first readings; by the rule\\nwritten beforehand no GPU budget goes to a learned whole-period model, though the evidence\\nsupports a narrower statement than the rule. The owner\'s thinning idea was not fairly tested.\\nWrite-up: [time-late, the classical follow-on](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical), with\\n[slides](/blog/2026/11/13/time-late-classical-slides)."},{"id":"/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-11-robust-segmenter-slides.md","title":"Slides: the robust segmenter study","description":"The illustrated companion to the spec 011 write-up: what","date":"2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-011","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-011"}],"readingTime":1.15,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: the robust segmenter study","date":"2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","spec-011"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late, the classical follow-on to Gate T","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/12/time-late-classical-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"The robust segmenter: what broke C, and why the fix was a threshold","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the spec 011 write-up](/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter): what\\nbreaks the frozen segmenter under bearing-error mismatch, the most a better segmenter could\\nyield, and why the robust test we built was not carried.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-11-robust-segmenter-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Diagnosis.** The frozen C\'s false transitions and missed changes under each mismatch kind\\n  at central severity (tuning seed 323, 150 engagements, 593 scored prefixes).\\n- **Yield.** Median relative range error with the robust final solve: the true manoeuvre times,\\n  C\u2033\'s clean-data segmentation applied to the mismatched bearings, and C\u2033 on the bearings it is\\n  given.\\n- **The settings choice.** For every confirmation test on the same 150 engagements: the share\\n  of the gap closed with its 95% interval, and false transitions against misses.\\n\\nThere are no track plots: the study stopped on the tuning seed before a segmenter was carried,\\nso there is no pair of systems whose tracks would show a measured difference.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\nAll three are drawn by `scripts/research/gr_charts.py` from the committed records under\\n`experiments/20261110-gr-diagnosis/` and `experiments/20261111-gr-choice/`; each figure\'s\\n`.json` sidecar names its inputs\' digests and the commit.\\n\\n```bash\\nuv run python scripts/research/gr_charts.py --out-dir site/static/img/2026-11-11-robust-segmenter-slides\\n```"},{"id":"/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-11-robust-segmenter.md","title":"The robust segmenter: what broke C, and why the fix was a threshold","description":"Spec 011 set out to build a robust classical segmenter and gate it. It stops earlier, on the","date":"2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"write-up","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/write-up"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-011","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-011"}],"readingTime":5.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"The robust segmenter: what broke C, and why the fix was a threshold","date":"2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["write-up","spec-011"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: the robust segmenter study","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late TMA, the whole track over a given period","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/time-late-kickoff"}},"content":"Spec 011 set out to build a robust classical segmenter and gate it. It stops earlier, on the\\ntuning seed, with no gate: the robust test we built does no more than raising the frozen\\nsegmenter\'s threshold, and neither closes most of the gap. The owner chose to publish this and\\nmove on. Nothing here is a gate result; every figure is from tuning seed 323.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was asked\\n\\nGate H\'s last comparison showed that bearing-error mismatch costs most in the segmentation:\\nthe standing classical system C\u2033 went from 0.102 to 0.280 in median relative range error under\\nthe primary condition. [Spec 011](/blog/2026/11/10/robust-segmenter-kickoff) was to find out\\nwhat breaks C, make its tests robust by classical means, and measure the share of the gap\\nclosed on fresh seeds.\\n\\n## What broke C\\n\\nOn 150 engagements, with each mismatch kind alone and all four together\\n(`experiments/20261110-gr-diagnosis/`):\\n\\n| Mismatch | C: median range error | False transitions | Missed changes |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| None | 0.131 | 248 | 406 |\\n| Bias | 0.128 | 283 | 394 |\\n| Untold \u03c3 | 0.120 | 230 | 389 |\\n| Correlated | 0.214 | 643 | 350 |\\n| Outliers | 0.236 | 923 | 368 |\\n| All four | 0.306 | 1,126 | 358 |\\n\\n- Outliers, then correlated error, make C declare transitions that are not there. Misses do not\\n  rise. Bias and an untold \u03c3 do nothing at this severity.\\n- Outliers\' loss is the segmentation\'s (+0.091 [+0.054, +0.148] of +0.105). Correlated error\'s\\n  is mostly lost on the true manoeuvre times too.\\n- Handing C the true change starts as nominations does not help: the false transitions enter\\n  at confirmation.\\n\\n## What a better segmenter could yield\\n\\nWith the robust final solve everywhere (`.../yield/`):\\n\\n- True manoeuvre times: 0.116 under mismatch, 0.057 clean.\\n- C\u2033 on the bearings it is given: 0.250 under mismatch, 0.122 clean.\\n- C\u2033\'s segmentation of the *clean* bearings, applied to the mismatched ones: 0.165.\\n\\nA segmenter as good under mismatch as C\u2033 is on clean data would close 0.63 [0.46, 0.81] of the\\ngap and still sit 43% above the reference. On clean data C\u2033 is already about twice the\\nreference. The 10% \\"closed\\" verdict the owner registered could not have been met by any\\nsegmenter of C\'s quality; that was known, and stated, before any design was scored.\\n\\n## What we built, and what it did\\n\\nC-r is C with only its confirmation test changed: each hypothesis fitted by Huber-weighted\\nleast squares and compared on Huber cost (`src/tma2/segment/robust.py`). C\'s code and\\nparameter digests are unchanged, and with plain costs C-r reproduces C exactly.\\n\\nOn the same 150 engagements (`experiments/20261111-gr-choice/`):\\n\\n| Confirmation test | Range error | Share of the gap closed | False transitions | Misses |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| C\u2033 as frozen | 0.254 | 0 | 1,137 | 361 |\\n| Huber 2.0 | 0.236 | 0.13 [\u22120.11, +0.38] | 815 | 380 |\\n| Plain C, threshold \xd7 2 | 0.223 | 0.22 [\u22120.02, +0.41] | 807 | 383 |\\n| Plain C, threshold \xd7 3 | 0.213 | 0.29 [+0.05, +0.50] | 600 | 442 |\\n| Huber 2.0, threshold \xd7 2 | 0.217 | 0.27 [+0.01, +0.52] | 464 | 439 |\\n\\n- The robust test removes 28% of the false transitions under mismatch and leaves clean data\\n  alone, as designed. Doubling the threshold removes as many.\\n- No pair of variants is separated. Under the rule declared before these data, the robust\\n  variant is not carried: the \\"robust segmenter\\" would be C with its threshold tripled.\\n- Removing 59% of the false transitions moves the error from 0.254 to 0.217. Every cut in false\\n  transitions brings more misses, and about 30% of true changes are missed even on clean data.\\n\\n## Criticism\\n\\n- **The design was aimed at half the problem.** The diagnosis counted false transitions rising\\n  and misses not rising, and the design followed the change. But the gap that matters is to\\n  the true manoeuvre times, and misses are a large part of it with or without mismatch. The\\n  yield measurement said so before the design was scored; the design was not revisited.\\n- **The trivial control was registered only because of an earlier panel.** Without the\\n  raised-threshold lines, Huber\'s 0.13 would have been reported as a modest success.\\n- **The sample decides nothing.** 150 engagements cannot separate a share of 0.13 from 0.29.\\n  The plan allowed no more on a tuning seed; a gate would have been needed, and the owner\\n  judged a gate on a threshold re-tune not worth its cost.\\n- **C\'s threshold looks low on clean data too.** Every raised-threshold variant reads better\\n  than the frozen C with no mismatch (none with an interval excluding zero). C was frozen at\\n  Gate S with a different final solve. This is untested on a fresh seed and is not a result.\\n- **The outlier model is the one Huber weights were made for** (independent, additive, 10 \u03c3).\\n  That the robust test still did not beat a threshold is the more notable for it.\\n- **Two CI runs were lost to plan and quoting bugs** in the workflow, about two and a half\\n  hours. An early failure check is now part of every launch.\\n\\n## What is kept\\n\\n- The diagnosis and yield measurements, and the tools: `tma2-hpredict --segments-from`,\\n  `--segmentation c2r`, `--dump-segmentation`; `scripts/research/gr_diagnosis.py`.\\n- C-r\'s code, tested, as a reference implementation of a robust confirmation test.\\n- Seeds 324 and 325 are unused; the next fresh seed stays 324 for whoever needs it, after\\n  spec 012\'s 326 to 328.\\n\\n## What follows\\n\\n- C stays frozen and C\u2033 stays the standing classical system.\\n- In [spec 012](/blog/2026/11/10/scaled-regressor-kickoff) the regressor is also reported\\n  beside C with a raised threshold, so that it faces the best classical system we know of. That\\n  line is reported, not a reading.\\n- The open question this leaves is misses: changes that leave too little trace to confirm.\\n  Research note 08 found most changes are like that. It is a limit of information before it is\\n  a limit of method, and no spec is proposed for it here.\\n\\nSlides: [the illustrated companion](/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter-slides).\\n\\n## Update, 11 November (later)\\n\\nThe hint that C\'s threshold is low on clean data did not survive a fresh seed. On validation\\nseed 327 (1,000 engagements, plain final solve, `experiments/20261111-r-val/`), C at three\\ntimes its threshold is worse than C\u2033: +0.018 [+0.010, +0.026] in median relative range error.\\nC\u2033 stays the classical system to beat."},{"id":"/2026/11/11/time-late-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/time-late-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-11-time-late-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: time-late TMA, the whole track over a given period","description":"Everything this project has scored so far is the real-time question: the best estimate now,","date":"2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-013","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-013"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-t","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-t"}],"readingTime":9.32,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late TMA, the whole track over a given period","date":"2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["kickoff","spec-013","gate-t"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The robust segmenter: what broke C, and why the fix was a threshold","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: Gate H, headroom under bearing-error mismatch","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h-slides"}},"content":"Everything this project has scored so far is the real-time question: the best estimate now,\\nfrom the bearings so far. The owner has asked for considered steps towards the other mode,\\ntime-late: given a period of bearings, the most consistent solution across the whole period.\\nSpec 013 defines that solution and its score, gives the classical approach its best shot, and\\nmeasures what is left before anything is trained.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n- **Spec 011** closed at tuning ([write-up](/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter)): the classical\\n  segmenter\'s largest weakness is missed manoeuvres, about 30% of true changes.\\n- **Spec 012** ([kickoff](/blog/2026/11/10/scaled-regressor-kickoff)): three seeds of a direct\\n  regressor are trained. On validation it sits at 1.2 to 1.8 times the bound on held course and\\n  beats C\u2033 after manoeuvres (0.065 against 0.105 in median relative range error). Its test\\n  reading is being scored. GPU training is paused by the owner.\\n- **Carried**: C frozen, C\u2033 the standing classical system, least squares at the bound when the\\n  manoeuvre times are right.\\n- **Never done**: scoring a whole track. C\u2033 run to the end of a window already produces one.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\n- **SRD** \xa73 (both consumers remain) and \xa75; a new row, Gate T, proposed for the owner to merge.\\n  Spec: `specs/013-time-late/`.\\n- **Owner\'s decisions** (11 November): a time-late solution is judged on the whole track across\\n  the period; bearings come every 5 seconds; the analyst does not choose the period.\\n- **Plan**:\\n  1. The solution and its score: position, course and speed at every moment, the manoeuvre\\n     times, and an honest \\"undetermined\\" where the data cannot say; a bound at every moment.\\n  2. Data: 5-second bearings, and periods cut at random from longer engagements, so a period\\n     may hold several ownship manoeuvres or none.\\n  3. The classical side, nothing trained: C\u2033 read as a track; a full-period pass that re-tests\\n     every declared change with all the data and looks for missed ones; least squares told the\\n     true manoeuvre times as the reference.\\n  4. The owner\'s bearing-rate idea: thinning where the rate is steady, as dropped bearings, as\\n     fixed normal points and as adaptive bins; accuracy, run time, and wild bearings.\\n  5. Decision record, fresh seeds (329, 330, 331), the panel.\\n- **Judged by**: the headroom, the best classical system\'s whole-track range error over the\\n  reference\'s. Material above 10% (proposed): the next spec trains a full-period proposer with\\n  least squares producing the answer. Not material: the classical full-period system is the\\n  time-late answer.\\n- **Risks**: independent errors at 5 seconds overstate the information (a correlated condition\\n  is scored beside); periods with no ownship manoeuvre, where the right answer is that range is\\n  not determined; a reference that is not a ceiling for systems that do not segment.\\n- **We stop** if the reference is not near its bound on determined rows: then the scoring is\\n  wrong, and nothing else means anything.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- **One period\'s bearings and bearing-rate curve**, with the compression bins drawn on it.\\n- **Whole-track plots**: truth, C\u2033 read as a track, the full-period system and the reference,\\n  on periods with and without an ownship manoeuvre.\\n- **Headroom chart** with intervals, by number of ownship manoeuvres and by position in the\\n  period.\\n- **Misses recovered** by the full period, against the causal system\'s.\\n- **Compression trade-off**: accuracy against run time for each form of thinning.\\n\\n## Update, 11 November (evening): the reference was the weak point\\n\\nThe first checkpoint tripped the plan\'s stop rule. The reference (the physics layer told the\\ntrue manoeuvre times) sat at about twice its bound on cut periods and 4.5 times on whole\\nengagements. The scoring was not at fault: with no target manoeuvre, least squares read as a\\ntrack is at the bound. The cause, tested, is that the layer gives each manoeuvre four free\\nunknowns where a turn at constant speed has one.\\n\\nThe owner chose to add a classical arc-model solve: a manoeuvre is a turn at a constant rate\\nand a speed change at a constant rate between two times. On 60 tuning periods it reaches 1.2\\ntimes the bound told the kind of manoeuvre and 1.4 judging the kind from the data, with the\\ntrue times. On C\u2033\'s own transitions it is no better than the chord and probably worse: a\\nstricter model forgives a missed manoeuvre less. So the segmentation remains the larger\\nfactor, about three in median range error, and the full-period re-test is the next step.\\nRecords: `experiments/20261111-tl-diagnosis/`, `experiments/20261111-tl-arc/` (tuning data,\\nnot a gate result).\\n\\n## Update, 11 November (late): a better search does not help\\n\\nThe full-period re-test was built on the arc model: refine every manoeuvre\'s times, add a\\nmanoeuvre where the whole-period fit demands one, remove one it does not support. On the same\\n60 tuning periods it is no better than C\u2033 read as a track (median range error 0.11 to 0.14\\nagainst 0.11; the reference told the true times is at 0.035). It fits the bearings exactly as\\nwell as the fit told the truth, with a third of the manoeuvres missed. A missed manoeuvre\\ncosts almost nothing in fit and a factor of five in range.\\n\\nSo the limit is not the search. Structures with different manoeuvres explain the same\\nbearings, and what separates them is knowledge of how targets manoeuvre, which a test of fit\\ndoes not use and a model trained on millions of engagements does. That is the likeliest\\nreason spec 012\'s regressor beat C\u2033. Record: `experiments/20261111-tl-retest/` (tuning data).\\n\\n## Update, 12 November: prior knowledge does not pick the history either\\n\\nThe owner chose to try a classical estimator that uses Annex B\'s manoeuvre statistics. The\\ncheap test came first: under those statistics, is the true manoeuvre history more probable\\nthan the wrong one the re-test finds? It is not. Where the re-test is badly wrong, the truth\\nis clearly ahead in about four periods in ten and the wrong history clearly ahead in about\\nfour in ten. The true history fits slightly better and pays more than that for its extra\\nmanoeuvres. The re-test was already close to the most probable history.\\n\\nTold only the right history, with every time left free, the fit sits at the bound. So the\\nproblem is neither the solve nor the timing: in about half the difficult periods the bearings\\nand the statistics together leave two histories standing, and they put the target in different\\nplaces. An honest system would say so, with candidate tracks and probabilities. Record:\\n`experiments/20261112-tl-prior/` (tuning data).\\n\\n## Correction, 12 November (later): the prior does help\\n\\nThe update above is wrong in its main claim and is withdrawn. The comparison had scored each\\nhistory\'s plausibility at a fit to the bearings alone, where a loosely determined manoeuvre\\nduration often lands on an impossible turn rate; the true history was then charged thousands\\nof units for a rate it does not have. The slides\' histogram showed it at once.\\n\\nWith the manoeuvre statistics inside the fit, the picture changes. Where the re-test is\\nwrong, the true history is clearly the more probable in half or more of the periods and the\\nwrong one in fewer than one in ten. And the re-test\'s own history, refitted with the\\nstatistics and no truth, is at about half the error of the standing classical system read\\nas a track (0.057 against 0.113 in median range error; ratio 1.99, interval 1.01 to 3.11,\\non 57 tuning periods). About a third of the difficult periods remain genuinely ambiguous.\\nThe direct next step is a classical search over histories scored by that evidence. Record:\\n`experiments/20261112-tl-prior/` (the withdrawn files are kept beside the corrected ones).\\n\\n## Update, 12 November (evening): a classical search, twice as good as the standing system\\n\\nThe owner chose to continue classically. A search over manoeuvre histories, scored by how\\nprobable each is given the bearings and Annex B\'s statistics, was run once on 99 tuning\\nperiods it had not seen during development. Its median range error over the whole track is\\n0.042 against 0.088 for the standing classical system read as a track (ratio 2.1, interval\\n1.6 to 3.0). It does as well started from nothing as started from the standing system\'s\\nanswer. The fit told the true history is at 0.018.\\n\\nThe search is no longer the weak point: where it is wrong it has usually found a history that\\nscores above the true one. What limits it is the score, and periods that really do admit two\\nexplanations. The probabilities it attaches to its candidate tracks are not yet trustworthy.\\nNext comes a gate on fresh seeds, with a test split whose targets manoeuvre differently from\\nwhat the search assumes. Record: `experiments/20261112-tl-search/` (tuning data).\\n\\n## Update, 12 November (midday): the gate stopped at its first check\\n\\nGate T was registered and its validation seed run. The search did what the tuning seed\\npromised: about two and a half times better than the standing classical system read as a\\ntrack, and 1.7 times better on targets that manoeuvre otherwise than it assumes. But the\\ngate\'s first check, on the soundness of its own reference, failed, and by the rule written\\nbeforehand the gate stops and the test seed is not generated.\\n\\nThe reference was a fit told the true manoeuvre history. It scored better than the\\ntheoretical bound allows, which is a fault, not a success. The fit had been started at the\\ntruth, and where the bearings pin the target down only weakly it simply stayed there. On\\nwell-determined rows it is exactly at the bound; on the rest it is the truth handed back.\\nThe check that caught it was registered with a forecast of \\"pass, 0.90\\".\\n\\nTwo other things the validation run showed. The search\'s candidate probabilities are\\nhonest when its assumptions about manoeuvres are right (it names the true history first in\\n61% of periods and claims 54%), and badly overconfident when they are wrong (22% against\\n52%). Record: `experiments/20261112-gt-val/`. What happens next is the owner\'s decision.\\n\\n## Update, 12 November (evening): the gate is decided\\n\\nThe owner chose to register the gate again with a reference fitted from neutral starts and\\nevery threshold unchanged. On a new validation seed the soundness check passed, and the\\ntest seed was run: five of six checks passed and the sixth failed as forecast. The review\\npanel approved with findings and narrowed most of the first interpretation. The search\\nhalves the median track error of the standing classical system, mostly by smoothing with a\\nbetter model and the manoeuvre statistics rather than by finding histories; it under-counts\\nmanoeuvres; and its probabilities are honest only on average and only when its assumptions\\nhold. Several studies this spec promised, the bearing-rate thinning idea among them, were\\nnot run and are owed. The write-up is [Gate T](/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t), with\\n[slides](/blog/2026/11/12/gate-t-slides)."},{"id":"/2026/11/10/gate-h-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-10-gate-h-slides.md","title":"Slides: Gate H, headroom under bearing-error mismatch","description":"The illustrated companion to the Gate H write-up: the four kinds of","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-h","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-h"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-010","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-010"}],"readingTime":2.62,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: Gate H, headroom under bearing-error mismatch","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","gate-h","spec-010"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: time-late TMA, the whole track over a given period","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/11/time-late-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Gate H: knowing the bearing-error model is worth almost nothing to this solve","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the Gate H write-up](/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h): the four kinds of\\nbearing-error mismatch, the three estimators that share one solve, the verdict on the test seed\\n(headroom not material) and what the review panel narrowed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-10-gate-h-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Mismatch.** The bearing error of one engagement (tuning seed 320, the first with a stated \u03c3\\n  of 1\xb0) with no mismatch, then under correlated error, slow bias and outliers at central\\n  severity, then all four together.\\n- **Track plot.** Engagement 3 of a demo split under the primary condition: ownship blue, target\\n  red, and the trails of the plain solve, the robust control and the error-model-informed fit\\n  from the zig, with marks and 1-\u03c3 ellipses at zig + 10 and + 20 min.\\n- **Every condition.** Median relative range error of the three estimators on the 16 validation\\n  conditions (seed 321, 1,000 engagements each, true segmentation).\\n- **Verdict.** R, the control\'s median range error over the informed fit\'s, with its 97.5%\\n  interval on the six test conditions (seed 322), against the 10% threshold.\\n- **By range.** R by quartile of true range on the test seed\'s primary condition. Post hoc.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\nThe four charts are drawn by `scripts/research/gh_charts.py` from the committed records under\\n`experiments/20261110-gh-val/` and `experiments/20261110-gh/` and from one engagement generated\\nin the script; the range-band record is made by `scripts/research/gh_range_bands.py` from the\\ntest run\'s `errors.npz`. The track plot\'s demo split:\\n\\n```bash\\nuv run tma2-generate --seed 320 --count 400 --out demo --ranges configs/man2-ranges.json \\\\\\n  --heldout-ranges configs/man2-heldout.json --format f32pack \\\\\\n  --mismatch configs/gateh/conditions.json#all-central\\nuv run tma2-hpredict --in demo/train.f32pack --kind nominal --start 0 --count 16 --out demo/nominal.ndjson\\nuv run tma2-hpredict --in demo/train.f32pack --kind robust --plan configs/gateh/gh-a.json --start 0 --count 16 --out demo/robust.ndjson\\nuv run tma2-hpredict --in demo/train.f32pack --kind attainable --mismatch-truth demo/train.mismatch.npz --start 0 --count 16 --out demo/informed.ndjson\\nuv run tma2-plot track --truth demo/train.f32pack --predictions demo/nominal.ndjson demo/robust.ndjson demo/informed.ndjson \\\\\\n  --labels \\"plain solve,robust control,error-model-informed fit\\" --scenario 3 --at 2060,2660 \\\\\\n  --out site/static/img/2026-11-10-gate-h-slides/track-3.svg\\n```\\n\\nSidecars next to each SVG name the digests, the command and the commit (clean tree).\\n\\n## Not achieved\\n\\n- Nothing was shown about other error families, target-motion mismatch or real data.\\n- The solve\'s 25% distance from the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound is unexplained.\\n- The comparison on the segmenter\'s own output was delivered after the verdict, for the combined\\n  condition only; it shows a factor of two and does not say which kind of mismatch causes it.\\n- No error-against-time figure: the estimators answer at four scored times only.\\n\\n## Next\\n\\n- Learning the bearing-error model for this solve is not pursued; the robust control is kept.\\n- Recommended next, if the owner does not object: a robust classical segmenter, to measure what\\n  is left of the factor of two. Then the scaled end-to-end regressor."},{"id":"/2026/11/10/gate-h","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-10-gate-h.md","title":"Gate H: knowing the bearing-error model is worth almost nothing to this solve","description":"Gate H trained nothing. It asked whether realistic bearing errors leave room worth learning","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"gate-h","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-h"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-010","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-010"}],"readingTime":8.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Gate H: knowing the bearing-error model is worth almost nothing to this solve","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["gate-h","results","spec-010"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: Gate H, headroom under bearing-error mismatch","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: a robust classical segmenter, Gate R","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/robust-segmenter-kickoff"}},"content":"Gate H trained nothing. It asked whether realistic bearing errors leave room worth learning\\nfor: once the errors are correlated, biased, mis-scaled and include wild values, how much better\\nis an estimator that is told the true error model than one with textbook robust weighting? On a\\nfresh test seed the answer is: not measurably. The review panel upheld the verdict and narrowed\\nwhat it may be taken to mean. A comparison it asked for then showed where the mismatch does\\nhurt: in the segmentation, by a factor of two.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe illustrated companion is the [slides post](/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h-slides). The kickoff was\\n[here](/blog/2026/11/09/headroom-mismatch-kickoff).\\n\\n## The question\\n\\nEvery learned component in this project so far has turned out, under its control, to be a simple\\nclassical rule ([research note 10](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/blob/main/docs/research/10-reflection-headroom.md)).\\nThe suspected reason was that our simulated bearing errors are exactly the errors the estimator\\nassumes: white, Gaussian, with a known \u03c3. Where an estimator\'s model is the truth, there is\\nnothing left to learn. So before any more learning work, the owner asked for a measurement: make\\nthe errors unlike the model, and see how much an estimator that *knows* the true error model\\ngains over one that does not.\\n\\n## What was compared\\n\\nBearing error became the sum of four parts, each of which can be switched on alone:\\n\\n| Kind | Central severity |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Correlated in time | half the variance, 60 s correlation time |\\n| Slow bias | 0.5\xb0, wandering over 30 min |\\n| Untold \u03c3 | true \u03c3 within a factor 1.5 of the stated 1\xb0 |\\n| Outliers | 2% of bearings, 10 \u03c3 |\\n\\nThe severities are a declared grid the owner accepted, not measured sensor figures. The gate is\\ndecided on all four together.\\n\\nThree estimators share one solve (a least-squares fit of constant-velocity legs) and are given\\nthe true manoeuvre times:\\n\\n- the **plain solve**, which assumes white noise;\\n- a **classical robust control**: Huber weights against wild bearings, \u03c3 estimated from the\\n  residuals, and a correlation model fitted to the residuals. Nothing is learned;\\n- an **error-model-informed fit**: the same solve told the true error covariance. The records\\n  call this the \\"attainable ceiling\\"; the panel found that name overstates it (below).\\n\\nThe statistic is R: the control\'s median range error over the informed fit\'s, minus 1. The owner\\nfixed the threshold before any data existed: headroom is material if R\'s interval lies wholly\\nabove 10%.\\n\\n## The result\\n\\nTest seed 322, 5,203 engagements, 20,473 scored rows, all four kinds at central severity:\\n\\n**R = \u22120.4%, 97.5% interval \u22122.4% to +1.4%. Not material.**\\n\\n| | Plain solve | Robust control | Informed fit |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Median relative range error | 0.134 | 0.118 | 0.119 |\\n| Median range error | 2,110 m | 1,880 m | 1,885 m |\\n| Median course error | 13.3\xb0 | 11.2\xb0 | 11.3\xb0 |\\n| Median speed error | 1.82 kt | 1.50 kt | 1.47 kt |\\n| Range within 10% by zig + 10 min | 22% | 26% | 27% |\\n\\nThe plain solve is 12.9% worse than the informed fit. The robust control closes all of that gap.\\nBoth soundness checks passed on the validation seed before the test seed was generated. The\\nforecast, written from the tuning seed, gave this verdict a probability of 0.85.\\n\\nBy kind, on 1,000 engagements each: outliers alone hurt the plain solve by 43% and Huber weights\\nremove nearly all of it (R +4.2%); correlated error raises every estimator\'s error and leaves the\\nmost (R +3.1%); a slow bias costs little (R +1.2%).\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive reviewers (statistics, estimation, domain, process, red team) each approved the verdict\\nwith findings; none raised a blocker. Three recomputed R from the per-row arrays and matched it.\\nThe process reviewer regenerated the test data from a clean clone and matched every digest.\\nTheir findings change how the result should be read.\\n\\n**The pooled zero is two effects cancelling.** By true range:\\n\\n| True range | R |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| under 10.7 km | +4.9% [+1.8, +8.2] |\\n| 10.7 to 17.2 km | +3.0% [\u22120.2, +7.1] |\\n| 17.2 to 25.3 km | \u22122.6% [\u22125.7, +0.7] |\\n| over 25.3 km | \u22125.2% [\u22128.3, \u22122.1] |\\n\\nAt short range knowing the error model is worth about 5%. At long range the informed fit is\\n*worse* than the robust control.\\n\\n**The reference is not a ceiling.** Both the control and the informed fit sit about 25% above the\\nCram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound for known manoeuvre times. That gap is larger still with no mismatch at all, so\\nit is not error-model headroom, but it is room that a better motion model or an end-to-end\\nestimator could claim. An earlier version of the record explained it by the solve fitting each\\nmanoeuvre with a chord. The estimation reviewer tested that and it accounts for only a part; the\\nrest is unexplained.\\n\\n**The severity grid came close to fixing the outcome.** For the Gaussian part of the primary\\ncondition, a linearised calculation puts the plain solve only about 3% behind exact generalised\\nleast squares. Within this error family only outliers could reach 10%, and Huber weights remove\\nthose. Stretching the grid (a 180 s correlation time, or 90% of the variance correlated) gives\\n8 to 12%.\\n\\n**The simulated mismatch is the robust control\'s own model family.** The errors are stationary,\\nindependent of the geometry, with zero-mean independent outliers. Real bearing error is tied to\\nownship heading, relative bearing and the zig. The domain reviewer\'s candidates for hidden\\nheadroom: a bias that steps or lags at the ownship zig (range comes from the bearing-rate change\\nacross the zig), bursty or one-sided outliers, and \u03c3 that varies within an engagement.\\n\\n**The true manoeuvre times hide the likeliest operational cost.** Correlated error and a\\nwandering bias can look like target manoeuvres. Whether our segmenter degrades under mismatch\\nwas a registered comparison that had not been run when the verdict was computed.\\n\\n## On our own segmenter\'s output\\n\\nThe comparison the panel asked for, on the validation seed (1,000 engagements), reported and\\nnot gated:\\n\\n| Median relative range error | No mismatch | Primary condition |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Robust control, true manoeuvre times | 0.060 | 0.114 |\\n| C\u2033: our segmenter, plain solve | 0.102 | 0.280 |\\n| C\u2033 + robust control | 0.103 | 0.230 |\\n\\nWith the true manoeuvre times, mismatch doubles the error. On our segmenter\'s own output it\\nnearly triples it: C recovers 0.92 of the gap between one leg and the oracle on clean data and\\n0.66 under mismatch. The robust control helps (\u221217.6% [\u221222.2, \u221213.7]), but it only treats the\\nfinal solve, and a factor of two remains (3,460 m against 1,710 m in median range error).\\n\\nSo the gate\'s verdict and this table are both true and answer different questions. Knowing the\\nerror model is worth nothing measurable once the segmentation is right. Getting the segmentation\\nright under mismatch is worth a factor of two. It is not yet evidence for learning: C was tuned\\non clean data and run unchanged, and the obvious first fix is classical.\\n\\n## What it means\\n\\nThe statement the panel accepted:\\n\\n> On the true segmentation, under the declared stationary Gaussian-plus-outlier bearing-error\\n> family at the stated severities, knowing the true error model is worth no more than about 1.5%\\n> (median) to 3% (P90) in range error to the piecewise-constant-velocity least-squares solve,\\n> over a textbook robust fit. Learning the bearing-error model for this solve is not pursued.\\n\\nNot shown: that this solve is near the best possible; that segmentation survives mismatch;\\nanything about other error families or real data.\\n\\nThe SRD row said that with this verdict \\"the learning track on this estimator stops\\". The\\nevidence supports the narrower sentence above. Whether the learning track as a whole stops is\\nthe owner\'s decision. One useful by-product stands regardless: the robust control is a cheap,\\nclassical 12% improvement over the plain solve under these errors, and costs under 2% where\\nnothing is wrong.\\n\\n## What was not achieved\\n\\n- The comparison on our own segmenter\'s output was promised and run late, after the verdict, on\\n  the validation seed and for the combined condition only. Which kind of mismatch breaks the\\n  segmenter, and whether it nominates falsely or misses, is not measured.\\n- The cause of the solve\'s 25% distance from the bound.\\n- Target-motion mismatch, other error families, a classical bias state, real data.\\n- The IMM and EKF references are not competitive on these rows (median range error near 0.6)\\n  and leave a quarter unanswered; they were re-tuned in one pass only.\\n\\n## Process notes\\n\\n- Criteria, plan and decision record were committed together before either the validation or the\\n  test seed was generated. Validation and test ran from the pull request\'s branch on the owner\'s\\n  instruction while the SRD amendment was still open; that is recorded in SRD \xa79.\\n- Two of the gate\'s checks were shaped after tuning results existed and are disclosed: the form\\n  of the clean-data check (the first draft could not be decided at the sample size; the owner\\n  chose its replacement) and the efficiency band.\\n- The author\'s first explanation of the efficiency gap was wrong and is withdrawn in the record.\\n- The reviewers are agents of the same model family as the author.\\n\\n## Next\\n\\n1. **Recommended first, because it is cheap and trains nothing: a robust classical segmenter.**\\n   Give C the robust control\'s \u03c3 and correlation estimates and Huber costs in its own tests,\\n   measure what is left of the factor of two, and find which kind of mismatch causes it. That\\n   is the control any learned segmenter would have to beat. If the owner does not object, it is\\n   adopted as the next spec.\\n2. **The scaled end-to-end regressor** (research note 04, option B), already decided by the\\n   owner: was gitma\'s 3 \xd7 bound a matter of compute? It needs a Modal account and two repository\\n   secrets from the owner. The 25% between this solve and the bound, and the factor of two on\\n   the segmenter\'s output, give it something to win.\\n3. A zig-coupled bias and bursty outliers (the domain reviewer\'s proposal), on the same harness.\\n4. Find out why the solve is 25% above the bound.\\n\\nRecords: [decision](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261110-gh-decision),\\n[validation](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261110-gh-val),\\n[test and panel](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261110-gh),\\n[segmenter comparison](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261110-gh-c2)."},{"id":"/2026/11/10/robust-segmenter-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/robust-segmenter-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-10-robust-segmenter-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: a robust classical segmenter, Gate R","description":"Gate H found that knowing the bearing-error model is worth nothing to the solve once the","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-011","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-011"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-r","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-r"}],"readingTime":3.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: a robust classical segmenter, Gate R","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["kickoff","spec-011","gate-r"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Gate H: knowing the bearing-error model is worth almost nothing to this solve","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: was it compute? The scaled end-to-end regressor","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/scaled-regressor-kickoff"}},"content":"Gate H found that knowing the bearing-error model is worth nothing to the solve once the\\nmanoeuvre times are right, and that getting the manoeuvre times right under mismatch is worth a\\nfactor of two. Spec 011 goes after that factor by the cheapest means first. It trains nothing:\\nit finds out what breaks our segmenter, makes the segmenter\'s own tests robust, and measures how\\nmuch of the gap that closes. What is left is the headroom a better segmenter could claim.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n- **Gate H: headroom not material** ([write-up](/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h),\\n  [slides](/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h-slides)). On the true manoeuvre times, a textbook robust fit\\n  is as accurate as a fit told the true error model.\\n- **On our own segmenter\'s output the picture is different.** Under the primary mismatch\\n  condition the standing classical system\'s median range error is 0.280, or 0.230 with the\\n  robust control in its final solve, against 0.114 with the true manoeuvre times. C recovers\\n  0.92 of the gap between one leg and the oracle on clean data and 0.66 under mismatch.\\n- **Carried**: C, frozen, as the segmenter and control; C\u2033 (C with the zig veto and fixed fade);\\n  the robust control for the final solve; the mismatch model and its severity grid.\\n- **Open**: which kind of mismatch breaks C, and whether it declares manoeuvres that did not\\n  happen or misses ones that did. Only the combined condition was run, and only the downstream\\n  error was measured.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\n- **SRD** \xa77 step 5 (realistic bearing error), Annex A \xa7A.3 (segmentation measures) and \xa7A.9\\n  (segmenter C); the gate is a new \xa75 row, proposed as an amendment the owner merges. Spec:\\n  `specs/011-robust-segmenter/`.\\n- **Judged by**: the share of the gap between C\'s segmentation and the true manoeuvre times that\\n  the robust segmenter C-r closes, each with the robust final solve, under the primary condition\\n  on a fresh test seed. C-r must not cost more than a declared limit on clean data. The panel\\n  decides.\\n- **Plan**:\\n  1. Diagnose on a fresh tuning seed (323): C\u2033 under each mismatch kind alone and all four, with\\n     false transitions, misses and delay measured against the truth, and what the design could\\n     yield at most.\\n  2. Design C-r from what the diagnosis measured: scatter and correlation estimated from\\n     residuals, wild bearings discounted, in C\'s own nomination and confirmation tests. C itself\\n     stays frozen.\\n  3. Choose C-r\'s settings on the tuning seed, by a declared rule, after a pilot.\\n  4. Decision record before any validation or test data: criteria, forecasts, every breakdown\\n     that will be reported.\\n  5. Validation (seed 324), test (seed 325), panel, write-up.\\n- **Risks**: fixing the wrong mechanism (hence the diagnosis first); estimating correlation from\\n  the residuals of the very hypothesis under test; a gain under mismatch bought with a loss on\\n  clean data; a pooled figure hiding a split by range (the range-band table is registered in\\n  advance this time).\\n- **We stop and revise the plan** if the diagnosis shows the loss is not in C\'s nominations or\\n  confirmations at all.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- **The diagnosis**: segmentation measures and range error by mismatch kind (tuning seed 323).\\n- **One engagement\'s segmentation** by C and by C-r against the truth, under the primary\\n  condition.\\n- **The three systems\' errors** with the share of the gap closed and its interval (test seed\\n  325), and the same by range band.\\n- **Track plots** of the three systems on a representative engagement, at zig + 10 and + 20 min.\\n- **Operational table**: metres, degrees, knots and the share of engagements settled.\\n\\n## Updates\\n\\n- 2026-11-11: the study stopped on the tuning seed with no gate. The robust confirmation test\\n  did no more than a raised threshold. See [the write-up](/blog/2026/11/11/robust-segmenter)."},{"id":"/2026/11/10/scaled-regressor-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/scaled-regressor-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-10-scaled-regressor-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: was it compute? The scaled end-to-end regressor","description":"Spec 012 runs the experiment research note 04 proposed and the project never ran: one large","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-012","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-012"}],"readingTime":2.69,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: was it compute? The scaled end-to-end regressor","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["kickoff","spec-012"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: a robust classical segmenter, Gate R","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/robust-segmenter-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: Gate 4, usable uncertainty","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4-slides"}},"content":"Spec 012 runs the experiment research note 04 proposed and the project never ran: one large\\nmodel that regresses the target\'s state straight from the bearings, trained on a GPU with about\\na hundred times gitma\'s data. gitma\'s networks sat at about three times the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound,\\nbut they were small and trained on 80,000 scenarios. Was that floor compute and data, or the\\narchitecture? The owner asked for it to start now, alongside spec 011.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n- **Gate H** ([write-up](/blog/2026/11/10/gate-h)): under the declared bearing-error mismatch,\\n  learning the error model on the true segmentation is not worth pursuing; the cost of mismatch\\n  is in the segmentation.\\n- **Spec 011** ([kickoff](/blog/2026/11/10/robust-segmenter-kickoff)) is in progress: the\\n  diagnosis found that outliers make the classical segmenter declare false transitions, and a\\n  robust version (C-r) is in its pilot.\\n- **Carried**: C\u2033 as the standing classical system; the tuned IMM and EKF; the oracle.\\n- **Open since gitma**: whether a large direct model reaches the bound on straight legs\\n  (research note 10, \xa75).\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\n- **SRD** \xa75 and \xa77; spec `specs/012-scaled-regressor/`. No gate: the model is a **control**,\\n  never the project\'s estimator (the constitution requires the reported estimate to come from a\\n  physics layer, and this model has none).\\n- **Read by**, fixed before any GPU run:\\n  - *Held course*: efficiency (error over the bound) on settled rows. **Reaches the bound** if\\n    its interval is wholly below 1.25; **flat floor** if wholly above 2.0 and not improving with\\n    geometry; otherwise partial. Batch least squares on the same rows should read 0.95\u20131.01.\\n  - *After manoeuvres*: paired differences against C\u2033 and the IMM.\\n- **Plan**:\\n  1. Training code and an on-the-fly data stream (about 10\u2077 scenarios from seed 326), tested at\\n     toy size on CPU.\\n  2. A `gputrain/**` workflow that starts a detached GPU run and brings weights and metrics back.\\n  3. Decision record: architecture, sizes, budget split, readings, forecasts.\\n  4. Pilot, then three seeds, inside the cap.\\n  5. Score on CPU with the existing scorer (validation seed 327, test seed 328); panel review.\\n- **Budget** (owner, 10 November): one month of free GPU credit, about 50 T4-hours, as a hard cap.\\n- **Owner-only prerequisites**: the GPU account, a spending limit and two repository secrets.\\n  Steps 1 to 3 do not need them.\\n- **Risks**: the cap is too small to train the model to convergence (the pilot decides the size,\\n  once, before the three seeds); a result between the two readings; GPU runs are not\\n  bit-reproducible (three seeds, hardware and versions recorded).\\n- **What each outcome means**: reaches the bound, and the physics layer is not needed for\\n  precision, so the design question reopens with evidence. A flat floor, and gitma\'s result was\\n  architecture: the hybrid design stands.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- **Efficiency by geometry band**, the regressor\'s three seeds beside batch least squares (test\\n  seed 328).\\n- **Error against scenarios seen** during training, with the tenth-of-the-data control.\\n- **After manoeuvres**: the regressor, C\u2033, the IMM and the oracle, with paired intervals.\\n- **Track plots** of the regressor, C\u2033 and the oracle on one representative engagement, at\\n  zig + 10 and zig + 20 min."},{"id":"/2026/11/09/gate-4-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-09-gate-4-slides.md","title":"Slides: Gate 4, usable uncertainty","description":"The illustrated companion to the Gate 4 write-up: the course statement","date":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-4","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-4"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-009","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-009"}],"readingTime":0.97,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: Gate 4, usable uncertainty","date":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","gate-4","spec-009"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: was it compute? The scaled end-to-end regressor","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/10/scaled-regressor-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Gate 4: a course statement that is honest on average, but not yet where it matters","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the Gate 4 write-up](/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4): the course statement\\n(\\"within \xb1X with probability P, otherwise unknown\\"), how honest it is on average and by slice, the\\nvalidation verdict and what the review panel found.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-09-gate-4-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Reliability diagram.** Stated P against the hit rate in five equal-count bins of P, on the\\n  3,934 validation rows (seed 314, ordinals 500\u20131499): the oracle and E with statements derived\\n  from their raw sds, the sd^\u03b2 temperature and the carried head (st-s3). The diagonal is an honest\\n  statement.\\n- **By slice.** Mean stated P beside the hit rate for the temperature and the head: overall,\\n  settled or not (defined from the truth), with a transition open at the row or not, and by\\n  minutes after the zig.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\nBoth figures are drawn by `scripts/research/g4_charts.py` from the committed records\\n`experiments/20261109-g4-val/calibration.json` and `experiments/20261108-g4-base-val/calibration.json`;\\neach `.svg.json` sidecar names the inputs\' digests and the code commit (4b707b3, clean)."},{"id":"/2026/11/09/gate-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-09-gate-4.md","title":"Gate 4: a course statement that is honest on average, but not yet where it matters","description":"Gate 4 asked whether the carried Gate 2 estimator E can state an uncertainty an operator can act","date":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"gate-4","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-4"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"}],"readingTime":5.01,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Gate 4: a course statement that is honest on average, but not yet where it matters","date":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["gate-4","results"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: Gate 4, usable uncertainty","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: is there anything for learning to win? Gate H","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/headroom-mismatch-kickoff"}},"content":"Gate 4 asked whether the carried Gate 2 estimator E can state an uncertainty an operator can act\\non. The course statement is \\"within \xb1X with probability P, otherwise unknown\\", and range and speed\\nget 95% intervals. On validation the statement is honest on average. A learned head beats the\\npre-registered classical control. But one per-bin reliability check missed its band, so by the\\nrule fixed beforehand the gate stopped at validation and the fresh test seed (320) was not\\ngenerated.\\n\\nThe review panel upheld the stop unanimously. It also found that the record\'s first reading was\\ntoo generous on three points:\\n- the miss was not a near-miss of one unlucky seed;\\n- the head\'s gain is mostly a classical function of E\'s own standard deviations;\\n- P is reliable on average but not on the rows just after a manoeuvre.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\nGate 3 showed that a Gaussian interval on E\'s course can only be honest at 95% by covering the\\nwhole circle on half the rows: E\'s course errors are heavy-tailed (35% over 30\xb0) and mostly not\\npredictable from causal signals. Gate 4 (spec 009, `specs/009-usable-uncertainty/`) states\\nuncertainty differently:\\n\\n- **Course:** a mixture, a von Mises around E\'s course plus a uniform. The row states an arc \xb1X\\n  (X at most 45\xb0) and the probability P that the true course lies inside it. P is mapped through\\n  an isotonic recalibration fitted on validation tuning rows.\\n- **Range and speed:** 95% half-widths c\xb7sd, trained on the interval score.\\n- **The learned head:** a small network on E\'s cached features (its encoder state, its sds, C\'s\\n  code) gives the four scales per row, trained on the exact errors.\\n- **The classical control:** a per-cell temperature (minutes since the zig \xd7 open transition). It\\n  is stated on sd^\u03b2, with \u03b2 chosen per quantity on tuning rows. With plain sd the shuffled head\\n  beat it in sample, so the redesign made the control stronger.\\n- **The criteria** (SRD amendment, owner to merge; decision record\\n  `experiments/20261108-g4-decision/`):\\n  - **G4-a:** range and speed coverage in [0.92, 0.98];\\n  - **G4-b:** hit rate minus P within \xb10.03 overall and \xb10.05 in each of five bins of P;\\n  - **G4-c:** a course log score better than \\"unknown\\", and mean P of at least 0.5;\\n  - **G4-d:** the head beats the temperature, unless a shuffled head also does;\\n  - **G4-e:** points unchanged.\\n\\n## The result (validation, 3,934 rows)\\n\\nThe carried seed, st-s3, is the median of three by the registered rule.\\n\\n| Check | Value [97.5% interval] | Verdict |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G4-a range coverage | 0.940 [0.926, 0.950] | pass |\\n| G4-a speed coverage | 0.929 [0.915, 0.941] | inconclusive |\\n| G4-b overall hit \u2212 P | \u22120.002 [\u22120.024, +0.021] | pass |\\n| G4-b top bin of P | stated 0.675, hit 0.729 | **fail** (limit 0.05) |\\n| G4-c course log gain over \\"unknown\\" | +1.01 [0.92, 1.10]; mean P 0.62 | pass |\\n| G4-d head \u2212 temperature | \u22120.235 [\u22120.355, \u22120.137] | pass |\\n| G4-e points | identical | pass |\\n\\nFor contrast, E\'s raw sds claim P 0.90 for a \xb117\xb0 arc and are right on 0.51 of rows. Their course\\nstatement is 2.6 nats per row worse than saying \\"unknown\\".\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\n- **The miss is real, and the check was fragile.** Under resampling, some bin misses \xb10.05 in 87%\\n  of draws for the carried seed, and in 43\u201347% for the other two. The top bin is under-stated for\\n  every seed. The per-bin band is about 2.5 sampling standard deviations wide, so a perfectly\\n  calibrated statement would still miss it a quarter of the time. That is a design flaw in G4-b,\\n  and it is fixed in the next spec rather than here.\\n- **The isotonic map caused it.** In the top bin the raw head is, if anything, over-confident:\\n  it states 0.76 against a hit rate of 0.73. The 10-knot map, fitted on 1,971 tuning rows, pooled\\n  its top two knots and capped P at 0.677.\\n- **P is reliable on average, not where it matters.**\\n  - On rows still settling after a target manoeuvre, a quarter of all rows, P is 0.62 and the hit\\n    rate 0.30. On settled rows it is 0.63 against 0.73.\\n  - P barely varies across rows (0.46\u20130.68).\\n  - Rows whose course is more than 90\xb0 off still state P \u2248 0.62.\\n  - The temperature behaves the same way.\\n- **The learning is mostly a classical rule.** The head beats the registered temperature in\\n  course, range and speed alike. But a classical rule fitted on the tuning rows alone recovers 72%\\n  of that margin: each scale is quadratic in E\'s three log sds, 43 parameters in all. The shuffled\\n  control could not show this, because it keeps the sds while it shuffles the errors. The residual\\n  0.07 is unattributed.\\n- **The course statement contradicts itself.** P is recalibrated, but the density (scored by the\\n  log score) is not, so the two disagree by up to 0.2 on a row. The normal approximation to the\\n  von Mises adds up to 0.12.\\n- **The intervals are honest but wide.** The 95% range interval spans a factor of 1.74 either way\\n  at the median, so at 20 km it runs 11\u201335 km at the 95th percentile. The speed half-width is\\n  about 6.7 kn. Coverage falls short at minute 5 and beyond 30 km.\\n\\nThe full record is in `experiments/20261109-g4-val/`, with the panel\'s findings in its\\n`review.md`.\\n\\n## What follows\\n\\nRecommended default for spec 010, adopted unless the owner objects:\\n- Keep the statement\'s shape (arc plus probability, interval scores). Make it one probability:\\n  use the exact von Mises mass, recalibrate the density too, and use a smoother recalibration.\\n- Gate reliability by slice, including minutes 1\u20135, open transitions and range bands. Add a\\n  causal \\"unsettled\\" proxy that lowers P after a suspected manoeuvre.\\n- Replace the per-bin point band with an interval or calibration-slope test.\\n- Register two controls before any result exists: a cross-sd classical rule fitted on the\\n  training stream, and a shuffled control that permutes only E\'s encoder state.\\n\\nSeed 320 stays unused."},{"id":"/2026/11/09/headroom-mismatch-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/headroom-mismatch-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-09-headroom-mismatch-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: is there anything for learning to win? Gate H","description":"Spec 010 trains nothing. After Gate 4 the owner asked for a reflection on the whole programme,","date":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-010","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-010"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-h","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-h"}],"readingTime":3.63,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: is there anything for learning to win? Gate H","date":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["kickoff","spec-010","gate-h"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Gate 4: a course statement that is honest on average, but not yet where it matters","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"Gate 2: E beats the classical segmenter, mostly by rules a designer could write","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-2"}},"content":"Spec 010 trains nothing. After Gate 4 the owner asked for a reflection on the whole programme,\\nand its finding is uncomfortable: every learned component so far has turned out, under its\\ncontrol, to be a simple classical rule. The likely reason is that our simulated bearing errors\\nare exactly the errors the estimator assumes, so the classical solve is already close to the best\\npossible. Before any more learning work, this gate measures whether realistic bearing errors\\nleave room worth learning for.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n- **Gate 4 stopped at validation** ([write-up](/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4),\\n  [slides](/blog/2026/11/09/gate-4-slides)): the course probability missed its top reliability\\n  bin, and 72% of the learned head\'s margin was a quadratic rule in three standard deviations.\\n- **The pattern across gates** (research note 10, `docs/research/10-reflection-headroom.md`):\\n  Gate 1\'s heads died; Gate D\'s detector read the time since the zig; Gate S\'s learned segmenter\\n  did not separate from the classical C; at Gate 2 a zig veto and a fixed fade (C\u2033) gave +0.018\\n  of the gap and learning +0.007 beyond it.\\n- **Carried**: C\u2033 as the standing classical estimator, C as its segmenter, the re-tuned IMM and\\n  EKF, the oracle. E is scored for information only.\\n- **Set aside** by the owner\'s decision of 9 November: the Gate 4 follow-on (course-probability\\n  fixes).\\n- **Open**: whether learning has anything to win once the error model is wrong.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\n- **SRD** \xa71 and \xa77 step 5 (realistic bearing error, deferred until Gate 2 passed); the gate is a\\n  new \xa75 row, proposed as an amendment the owner merges. Spec: `specs/010-headroom-mismatch/`.\\n- **Judged by**: headroom, the share of the gap between C\u2033 and an *attainable ceiling* that\\n  remains after a classical robust control. The ceiling is the same solve told the true\\n  segmentation and the true error model, but not which bearings were wild. The owner\'s threshold for\\n  \\"material\\": the robust control\'s median range error at least 10% above the ceiling\'s, interval\\n  wholly above, on the primary condition. The panel decides.\\n- **Plan**:\\n  1. Generator: four mismatches, separately switchable (correlated error, slow bias, unknown \u03c3,\\n     outliers), three severities each from open literature. Mismatch off reproduces today\'s data\\n     byte for byte.\\n  2. Two ceilings and the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound under the true error model.\\n  3. A classical robust control on C\u2033\'s solve: robust loss, \u03c3 from residuals, whitening by an\\n     estimated correlation. Settings fixed on a tuning seed.\\n  4. Decision record before any scoring: conditions, threshold, seeds 320 (tuning), 321\\n     (validation), 322 (test), forecasts.\\n  5. Score on CI; results as gap fractions and in metres, degrees, knots and minutes to a settled\\n     solution; panel verdict.\\n- **What follows**: material headroom means a learned component is trained under mismatch, with\\n  C\u2033 and the robust control as standing controls. None means the learning track stops and the\\n  null result is published. Either way the next step after that is the scaled end-to-end\\n  regressor (research note 04, option B), which answers a question gitma left open: was its\\n  3 \xd7 bound a matter of compute?\\n- **Risks**: severities chosen until headroom appears (they are fixed with sources before\\n  scoring); a ceiling that knows too much (hence two ceilings, and only the attainable one\\n  counts); loss that is really segmentation failure counted as estimation headroom (C\u2033 is also\\n  scored on the true segmentation); a constant bias, which no estimator can remove.\\n- **We stop** before the test seed only if the pipeline is unsound: a ceiling far from its bound,\\n  or a robust control that damages C\u2033 on clean data. A small headroom on validation is a result\\n  to confirm, not a reason to stop.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- **One engagement\'s bearings under each mismatch kind**, beside the clean bearings (tuning seed\\n  320, one scenario, named in the figure\'s sidecar).\\n- **Headroom chart** per condition: C\u2033\'s gap to the attainable ceiling, the part the robust\\n  control closes, the part that remains, with intervals (validation seed 321, then test 322).\\n- **Operational table**: the same gaps in metres, degrees, knots and minutes to a settled\\n  solution.\\n- **Track plots** of C\u2033, the robust control and the attainable ceiling on a representative\\n  engagement of the primary condition, at zig + 10 and zig + 20 min.\\n- **Efficiency per slice** against the mismatch-aware bound, for every estimator."},{"id":"/2026/11/08/gate-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-2","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-gate-2.md","title":"Gate 2: E beats the classical segmenter, mostly by rules a designer could write","description":"Gate 2 asked whether learning, trained end to end through the physics layer, does better than","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"gate-2","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-2"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"}],"readingTime":7.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Gate 2: E beats the classical segmenter, mostly by rules a designer could write","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["gate-2","results"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: is there anything for learning to win? Gate H","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/09/headroom-mismatch-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: Gate 3, uncertainty calibration","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3-slides"}},"content":"Gate 2 asked whether learning, trained end to end through the physics layer, does better than\\nthe classical segmenter C on the owner\'s manoeuvring scenarios. On the fresh test seed it passes\\nall four checks. The learned model E recovers more of the range-error gap between a one-leg fit and\\nthe oracle than C does: +0.025, with a 97.5% interval of [+0.018, +0.031].\\n\\nThe agent review panel then asked what E had actually learned. Most of it turned out to be two\\nsimple rules. The first is to raise the bar for a target turn at the ownship\'s own manoeuvre. The\\nsecond is to trust recent bearings more. Those two rules, with nothing learned, reproduce about\\n72% of E\'s margin over C. Learning adds a small but real increment beyond them: +0.007\\n[+0.001, +0.012].\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\nC is Gate S\'s classical segmenter, frozen by digest\\n(see [steady-leg segmentation](/algorithms/segmentation)). It nominates target transitions, confirms\\nthem by the coupled physics-layer fit, and hands each prefix to the\\n[physics layer](/algorithms/physics-layer). E adds learning at C\'s two decision points\\n(see [the Gate 2 model](/algorithms/gate2-model)):\\n\\n- **E-s**: a causal evidence network that shifts C\'s confirmation threshold, by at most \u03c4/2 either\\n  way. It is trained on decision tables, which hold each near-threshold decision\'s downstream\\n  error with the transition and without it.\\n- **E-w**: a causal transformer that adds per-bearing log-weights and a start correction to C\'s\\n  hand-over, trained through the physics layer with 12 knots.\\n\\nUntrained, E is C, to the last bit. Three seeds of each part were trained on stream seed 318, with\\na shuffled-label control. On validation every seed passed every check, and the median seed (e-s3)\\nwas frozen before the test seed was generated.\\n\\nThe [decision record](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261107-g2-decision)\\nfixed everything before training: the four criteria (G2-d added by the owner on 6 November), the\\nlimits, the carried-seed, shuffled and validation rules, and a forecast. The forecast was E \u2212 C\\n+0.005 [\u22120.005, +0.015], with a pass judged about a one-in-four chance.\\n\\n## The result\\n\\nSeed 319: all 4,983 scenarios at \u03c3 = 1\xb0, 19,609 G5 rows, 97.5% scenario-clustered paired\\nintervals.\\n\\n| Check | Statistic | Result |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G2-a | E / frozen IMM, median range; course | 0.182 [0.173, 0.192]; 0.252 [0.236, 0.271]: **pass** |\\n| G2-b | E / one leg on settled rows: range; course; speed (limit 1.1) | 0.166; 0.136; 0.212: **pass** |\\n| G2-c | E\'s gap recovered in range (limit 0.5) | 0.924 [0.915, 0.931]: **pass** |\\n| G2-d | E \u2212 C, gap recovered in range | +0.025 [+0.018, +0.031]: **pass** |\\n\\n| | Range | Course \xb0 | Speed kt | No estimate | Range > 50% | Efficiency | Gap recovered |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| One leg | 0.580 | 67.0 | 6.31 | 24.0% | 55.5% | 15.4 | 0 |\\n| Frozen IMM | 0.567 | 56.5 | 6.03 | 23.3% | 54.8% | 14.3 | \u2014 |\\n| EKF | 0.520 | 59.9 | 5.59 | 20.0% | 51.3% | 12.6 | \u2014 |\\n| Naive | 0.398 | 90.8 | 3.73 | 0% | 37.0% | 7.9 | \u2014 |\\n| GLR + confirm | 0.498 | 47.5 | 4.78 | 21.2% | 49.9% | 11.7 | 0.159 |\\n| C | 0.116 | 15.1 | 1.61 | 0.5% | 11.5% | 2.70 | 0.899 [0.891, 0.908] |\\n| **E** | **0.103** | **14.2** | **1.49** | **0.5%** | **10.0%** | **2.46** | **0.924 [0.915, 0.931]** |\\n| E-w alone | 0.109 | 14.6 | 1.56 | 0.5% | 11.1% | 2.57 | 0.914 |\\n| Oracle | 0.064 | 7.5 | 0.94 | 0% | 2.5% | 1.57 | 1 |\\n\\nRange and course are medians over all rows (a row with no estimate counts as infinitely wrong).\\nEfficiency is the median error over the known-manoeuvres Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound.\\n\\nWhere the gain sits:\\n\\n- **By row type.** It is largest on the 5.8% of rows that end inside an open transition, which\\n  are C\'s weakest: +0.139 [+0.077, +0.202]. On the other 94% it is +0.023 [+0.016, +0.028].\\n- **By range.** It grows with range, from nothing below 5 km to +0.05 beyond 30 km.\\n- **Gross errors.** The share of rows with range error over 50% falls from 11.5% to 10.0%.\\n\\nThe gain is stable. It is +0.020 to +0.024 at each of the four scored times, and removing any\\none scenario moves it by at most 0.0003.\\n\\n## What E learned\\n\\nRound 1 of the panel looked inside the two learned parts:\\n\\n- **E-s** puts almost all its evidence within a few rows of the ownship zig. It makes no\\n  distinction between rows inside true target manoeuvres and rows outside them. In effect it\\n  learned not to accept a target turn exactly when the ownship turns. A target knot at the only\\n  ownship manoeuvre lets the target\'s velocity absorb the bearing-rate change, and range becomes\\n  unobservable.\\n- **E-w** learned mostly a recency profile. It raises the log-weights of the newest bearings by\\n  about 0.7 and lowers those of bearings half an hour old by about as much.\\n\\nSo the panel asked for classical versions of both, registered before any of their results\\nexisted:\\n\\n- **C\u2032**: C with a veto. Candidates at the ownship zig \xb1 6 rows get E-s\'s largest threshold shift.\\n  The window was chosen on E-s\'s own tuning tables.\\n- **fade**: C with a fixed recency profile, clip(0.73 \u2212 0.0079 \xd7 age, \xb12), read off E-w\'s mean\\n  weights.\\n- **C\u2033**: both rules together, with nothing learned.\\n\\n| On test | Gap difference |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| C\u2032 \u2212 C | +0.008 [+0.004, +0.011] |\\n| fade \u2212 C | +0.011 [+0.007, +0.014] |\\n| C\u2033 \u2212 C | +0.018 [+0.013, +0.022] |\\n| **E \u2212 C\u2033** | **+0.007 [+0.001, +0.012]** |\\n| E \u2212 (C\u2032 + learned E-w) | +0.003 [\u22120.002, +0.008] |\\n| E-w \u2212 fade | +0.004 [+0.001, +0.007] |\\n\\nReading these as registered:\\n\\n- **The two rules reproduce about 72% of E\'s margin over C.** Both rules took their settings from\\n  the learned models. So 72% is an upper bound on what a designer would get without E, and\\n  E \u2212 C\u2033 is a conservative test of learning.\\n- **Learning adds +0.007 beyond the rules.** It is small and rests on one test look. The\\n  validation split, five times smaller, is inconclusive (+0.008 [\u22120.004, +0.017]). The result\\n  survives a correction for the six comparisons made against controls.\\n- **E-s adds nothing shown beyond the zig veto.**\\n- **On test, the increment is consistent with E-w doing more than a fixed fade.** On validation\\n  that comparison points the other way (\u22120.003), and its test value was seen before C\u2033 was\\n  registered. It is descriptive only.\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive reviewers covered TMA geometry, estimation and numerics, evaluation statistics,\\nreproducibility and a red team. A sixth, causality audit ran before any test result existed. The\\npanel took two rounds.\\n\\n- **Causality.** Replacing every bearing after the scored row with noise leaves E\'s estimate at\\n  that row bit-identical. There is no leak, and a test now enforces this.\\n- **The numbers.** Statistics recomputed every gated number from the per-row errors, bit for bit,\\n  and with an independent bootstrap. Reproducibility regenerated seed 319 byte for byte and checked\\n  every digest and the order of events: decision, carried model, test seed, plans, results.\\n- **Two majors in round 1** (E-s is mostly a zig veto; E-w mostly a fading memory). Both were\\n  answered by the classical controls above and closed in round 2.\\n- **Wording.** Round 2\'s findings were about wording: no claim that E-s *is* a zig veto, the\\n  attribution to E-w resting on test alone, and where the rules\' settings came from.\\n\\nAll findings and responses are in\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261107-g2/review.md).\\n\\n## Caveats\\n\\n- **The shuffled control could not void G2-d as run.** Both shuffled parts stopped at epoch 0, so\\n  the shuffled E is C. The real evidence that labels matter is in the training logs: both\\n  shuffled runs got worse when trained (E-s 52.3 \u2192 54.0, E-w 38.1 \u2192 44.9), while every real seed\\n  improved.\\n- **No time-late variant was trained.** Constitution IV requires one. It is a follow-on.\\n- **The score depends slightly on the CPU.** The scorer re-solves C and the references. On a\\n  different CPU family C\'s errors move by up to 6e-4 relative and G2-d by 1.4e-5. E\'s errors,\\n  read from its prediction file, do not move.\\n- **Every transition is handed over as a course change.** The speed prior applies even across\\n  speed changes. This affects C and E alike, so it does not touch G2-d, but it costs both of them\\n  against the oracle.\\n- **C is still 2.7 times the bound, and E 2.5.** The oracle, at 1.6, shows what knowing the change\\n  times is worth.\\n\\n## What follows\\n\\nThe SRD leaves the follow-on to the owner, with a recommended default. **Recommended:**\\n\\n- **Carry E (e-s3) as the estimator of record,** and keep C\u2033 (zig veto + fade) as a standing\\n  classical control in every later gate.\\n- **Next is SRD \xa77 step 5\'s first item, uncertainty calibration (spec 008).** E-w and the fade\\n  rescale the bearing weights, so their covariances are not on C\'s scale and must be calibrated\\n  before any covariance-based gating.\\n- **Spec 008 also takes the panel\'s follow-ons:**\\n  - a time-late variant;\\n  - scoring the last-epoch shuffled weights;\\n  - coverage assertions in the scorer;\\n  - storing C\'s scored errors instead of re-solving them.\\n\\nThis is adopted if the owner has not objected by the time spec 008 is written."},{"id":"/2026/11/08/gate-3-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-gate-3-slides.md","title":"Slides: Gate 3, uncertainty calibration","description":"The illustrated companion to the Gate 3 write-up: what each estimate now","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-3","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-3"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-008","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-008"}],"readingTime":3.38,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: Gate 3, uncertainty calibration","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","gate-3","spec-008"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Gate 2: E beats the classical segmenter, mostly by rules a designer could write","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-2"},"nextItem":{"title":"Gate 3: E\'s stated uncertainty can be widened, but not made honest, by a scale","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe illustrated companion to [the Gate 3 write-up](/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3): what each estimate now\\nstates about its own uncertainty, what a learned calibration did to it, two validation scenarios\\ndrawn with their 1-\u03c3 ellipses, the validation verdict and the time-late check.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-08-gate-3-slides\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n- **Calibration chart.** For the oracle, the frozen IMM, E raw, the temperature and the carried\\n  head: the share of the 3,934 validation rows whose error lies inside the stated 95% interval in\\n  log range, course and speed, and the share within the \u03c7\xb2\u2084 95% NEES point. The shaded band is\\n  Gate 3\'s [0.92, 0.98]. From the committed records `experiments/20261108-g3-val/` and\\n  `experiments/20261108-g3-base-val/`.\\n- **Track plot, ordinal 648** (validation seed 314, scenario 81299): a settled target leg. Marks\\n  and ellipses at zig + 5 and 15 min. Raw E\'s ellipses are too small to see; the calibrated ones\\n  are long slivers along the line of sight, covering the truth but wide in range.\\n- **Track plot, ordinal 514** (scenario 81048): a target manoeuvre mid-window. E\'s course error\\n  reaches 67\xb0; the reference estimators (batch least squares, the frozen IMM) do far worse in\\n  range, and the oracle, with the true segmentation, does best.\\n- **Errors against time, ordinal 514.** Range, course and speed errors; E\'s raw and calibrated\\n  rows share their points (calibration changes only the covariance).\\n\\nTrack plots: ownship blue, target red, each estimator\'s trail from the zig with its marker and\\n1-\u03c3 ellipse at the marked times. Rows before the zig are hidden.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\nThe demo split holds two scenarios of validation seed 314 (`configs/man2-ranges.json`,\\n`configs/man2-heldout.json`, generated at d72cb81, digest `e8150cb0\u2026`), cut by\\n`scripts/research/g3_demo_split.py`. E\'s files come from `results/g2predict-g3-base-val-42471500526`\\n(raw) and `results/g3predict-g3-a-val` (calibrated).\\n\\n```bash\\nuv run tma2-generate --seed 314 --count 100000 --ranges configs/man2-ranges.json --heldout-ranges configs/man2-heldout.json --format f32pack --out val314\\nuv run python scripts/research/g3_demo_split.py --pack val314/validation.f32pack --ordinals 514,648 --out demo --predictions e-raw=e-s3.ndjson e-cal=cal-s3.ndjson\\nuv run tma2-solve --in demo/demo.f32pack --out demo/lsq.ndjson --per-timestep\\nuv run tma2-track --in demo/demo.f32pack --out demo/imm.ndjson --filter imm --sigma-cv 0.03 --sigma-ct 1 --sigma-omega 0.01 --switch 0.0003 --p0-scale 4 --r-scale 2\\nuv run tma2-oracle --truth demo/demo.f32pack --out demo/oracle.ndjson\\nuv run tma2-plot track --truth demo/demo.f32pack --bound-to val314/validation.f32pack --predictions demo/lsq.ndjson demo/imm.ndjson demo/oracle.ndjson demo/e-raw.ndjson demo/e-cal.ndjson --labels \\"batch LS,IMM (frozen),oracle,E raw,E calibrated (cal-s3)\\" --scenario 81299 --at 2810,3410 --out site/static/img/2026-11-08-gate-3-slides/track-648.svg\\nuv run python scripts/research/g3_calibration_chart.py --out site/static/img/2026-11-08-gate-3-slides/calibration.svg\\n```\\n\\nFigures were written from a clean worktree at the commit their sidecars name, to a scratch folder, then copied here. The other track plot and the error curve differ only in `--scenario 81048 --at 4580,5180` and\\n`tma2-plot errors`. The `.json` sidecar next to each SVG names the inputs\' digests, the command\\nand the commit.\\n\\n## Not achieved\\n\\n- An honest joint uncertainty: 0.835 of rows within the \u03c7\xb2\u2084 95% point against 0.92 required,\\n  mostly because the calibration keeps the solve\'s near-singular correlations.\\n- Meaningful course intervals: on half the rows the 95% course interval is the whole circle.\\n- Learning: the head is nearly a constant and no better than a per-slice temperature.\\n- Time-late E-w: no gain over the causal model (\u22120.0018 [\u22120.0082, +0.0057] in gap recovered).\\n\\n## Next\\n\\nSpec 009, recommended and adopted unless the owner objects: course reported as circular or as\\nundetermined; the stated uncertainty gated on observability by a causal proxy, with a\\n\\"manoeuvre suspected\\" flag; correlation shrinkage and training on the exact errors the gate\\nscores. E\'s points stand; its raw covariance must not be shown as an uncertainty."},{"id":"/2026/11/08/gate-3","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-gate-3.md","title":"Gate 3: E\'s stated uncertainty can be widened, but not made honest, by a scale","description":"Gate 3 asked whether the carried Gate 2 estimator E can state an uncertainty that can be trusted.","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"gate-3","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-3"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"}],"readingTime":4.32,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Gate 3: E\'s stated uncertainty can be widened, but not made honest, by a scale","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["gate-3","results"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: Gate 3, uncertainty calibration","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: reporting plans and progress, two posts per task","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/reporting-kickoff"}},"content":"Gate 3 asked whether the carried Gate 2 estimator E can state an uncertainty that can be trusted.\\nThe answer on validation is no, and the gate stopped there: the calibration did not meet the\\npre-registered validation rule, so the fresh test seed was not generated.\\n\\nE\'s raw covariance, from the physics solve, is badly overconfident: its 95% interval covers 0.60 of\\nrows in log range and only a third of rows fall within the \u03c7\xb2\u2084 95% point. A learned calibration\\ncan widen the stated standard deviations until 92\u201399% of each quantity is covered. It cannot make\\nthe joint 4-state statement honest (0.835 within the \u03c7\xb2\u2084 point, against 0.92 required), and it does\\nno better than a classical per-slice temperature. The review panel found why, and the reasons\\npoint to a different design.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\nEvery prediction row now states the covariance of (x, y, vx, vy) at the row and the standard\\ndeviations of log range, course and speed derived from it (see\\n[uncertainty calibration](/algorithms/calibration)). On a validation baseline before any training,\\nevery estimator was overconfident except the oracle:\\n\\n| | 95% coverage, log range | Within \u03c7\xb2\u2084 95% | Median NEES ratio |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| E | 0.60 | 0.33 | 6.7 |\\n| C | 0.61 | 0.36 | 7.3 |\\n| Oracle (true segmentation) | 0.875 | 0.75 | 1.32 |\\n| IMM | 0.41 (22% no estimate) | 0.37 | 10.6 |\\n\\nA calibration head rescales E\'s covariance without touching its points. It reads E\'s encoder state\\nat the row, the raw standard deviations and C\'s code, and outputs four log-scales, for the standard\\ndeviations of log range, bearing, speed and course, keeping the stated correlations. It was trained\\non E\'s frozen features over the training stream, three seeds and a shuffled-label control. The\\nclassical control is a temperature: the same four scales fitted per cell of (time since the zig \xd7\\ntransition open at the row).\\n\\nThe [decision record](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261108-g3-decision)\\nfixed the criteria before training. Two changes came after the baseline and before any training.\\nThe owner replaced the original consistency check (median NEES near its \u03c7\xb2\u2084 value) with a tail\\ncheck, because E\'s errors are heavy-tailed and the two original checks could not both pass. And\\nthe head\'s form moved from one scale to four local ones, because covering course needed its\\nstandard deviation widened about 30 times, range and speed only about 5. The decision forecast an\\noverall pass at about 0.1, with the tail check the likely failure.\\n\\n## The result (validation, 3,934 rows)\\n\\n| Check | Carried head | Band | Outcome |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| G3-a 95% coverage, log range / course / speed | 0.986 / 0.923 / 0.984 | 0.92\u20130.98 | inconclusive |\\n| G3-b share within the \u03c7\xb2\u2084 95% point | 0.835 [0.819, 0.849] | 0.92\u20130.98 | **fail** |\\n| G3-c head \u2212 temperature, interval score in log range | +1.07 [+0.98, +1.17] | below 0 | **fail** |\\n| G3-d points unchanged | exact | \u2014 | pass |\\n\\n## Why, according to the panel\\n\\nFive reviewers (statistics, numerics, TMA geometry, reproducibility, red team) approved the stop and\\ncorrected the account:\\n\\n- **Course coverage is largely vacuous.** For more than half the carried head\'s rows the 95% course\\n  interval covers the whole circle (median stated course sd 101\xb0). Course coverage is reached by\\n  giving up on course, not by calibrating it. Course binds because it is the least observable state\\n  on short or low-bearing-rate legs and just after a zig.\\n- **The kept correlations sink the joint check.** E\'s local covariance is near-singular (median\\n  correlation 0.81 between log range and speed). With the same scales and the correlations\\n  dropped, 0.957 of rows would fall within the \u03c7\xb2\u2084 point. A scale-only family cannot pass.\\n- **The loss is not the score.** The head wins on its training loss and loses on the interval\\n  score: it widens log range everywhere to absorb tails that course drives. It trained on\\n  linearised errors, which cannot see a course flip, while the gate scores exact errors.\\n- **No per-row learning is shown.** The head is close to a constant; moved by its best single\\n  factor, it is no better than its shuffled control.\\n- **Much of the failure is physical.** On rows during or just after a manoeuvre even the oracle,\\n  with the true segmentation, falls well short (0.64 within the \u03c7\xb2\u2084 point): the bearings have not\\n  yet determined the new leg.\\n\\n## What follows\\n\\nRecommended follow-on (adopted if the owner has not objected by the next spec):\\n\\n1. **Report course as undetermined when it is.** A circular distribution for course, or an explicit\\n   \\"course not yet determined\\" state, instead of a Gaussian sd above 90\xb0.\\n2. **Gate the uncertainty on observability.** Calibrate and report on rows where the leg is\\n   determined, using a causal proxy (time since the last detected change, C\'s open-transition\\n   code), with a \\"manoeuvre suspected\\" flag otherwise.\\n3. **Shrink the stated correlations** and **train on the exact errors** the gate scores.\\n4. A heavy-tailed predictive only for the manoeuvre component, weighted by E\'s own change evidence.\\n\\nThe carried estimator stays E; its point estimates are unchanged. Its raw covariance should not be\\nshown to an operator as an uncertainty. The validation record, the decision and the panel\'s\\nreports are in\\n[experiments/20261108-g3-val](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261108-g3-val)."},{"id":"/2026/11/08/reporting-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/reporting-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-reporting-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: reporting plans and progress, two posts per task","description":"The owner asked for progress to be visible before and after each task, not only at gate verdicts:","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"process","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/process"}],"readingTime":2.37,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: reporting plans and progress, two posts per task","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["kickoff","process"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Gate 3: E\'s stated uncertainty can be widened, but not made honest, by a scale","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3"},"nextItem":{"title":"Slides: how progress is now reported, shown on a demo split","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/reporting-slides"}},"content":"The owner asked for progress to be visible before and after each task, not only at gate verdicts:\\na post that says where we are and where we are going when a task starts, and an illustrated deck\\nwith charts and track plots when it ends. This task sets that up and uses it on itself.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n- **Gate 2 passed** on the owner\'s manoeuvring scenarios, decided by the panel in round 2\\n  ([write-up](/blog/2026/11/08/gate-2)). E, the learned model, beats the classical segmenter C by\\n  +0.025 of the one-leg-to-oracle gap; most of that is two rules a designer could write, and the\\n  learning beyond them is +0.007 [+0.001, +0.012].\\n- **Carried**: E, with C\u2033 (zig veto and fixed fade, nothing learned) as a standing control.\\n- **In progress**: spec 008, uncertainty calibration (Gate 3). Test seed 320 is reserved for it.\\n- **Reporting so far**: five gate write-ups, critical in substance, no figures. P0 had no post\\n  until today\'s [backfill](/blog/2026/11/01/gate-p0). Nothing describes direction between gates.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\n- **Constitution 1.3.0**: two posts per task (a Spec Kit feature or a gate run), a kickoff before\\n  implementation and a slides post before the PR. Owner-merged, as every constitution amendment.\\n- **Practice**: `AGENTS.md` (Reporting), a [process page](/process/reporting), two post templates,\\n  a reveal.js deck template, and Spec Kit after-hooks so `/speckit-specify` writes the kickoff and\\n  `/speckit-implement` builds the slides.\\n- **Tooling**: `tma2-plot`, a CLI that draws track plots (ownship blue, target red, each\\n  estimator\'s belief with 1-\u03c3 ellipses at named times) and error curves from any truth split and\\n  prediction files, with a provenance sidecar per figure.\\n- **Plan**: plotter and tests; documents and templates; hooks; a P0 backfill post; this kickoff;\\n  the slides post for this task from a seeded demo split; PR.\\n- **Risks**: decks for small changes become boilerplate (so the rule is scoped to specs and gates,\\n  with dated paragraphs for the rest); a second narrative per gate (so a gate\'s slides post is the\\n  write-up\'s illustrated companion and links to it); figures that cannot be remade (so each has a\\n  sidecar naming data digests, command and commit). We would stop if the site build or the repo\\n  grew beyond reason: figures are SVG and kept under about 200 KB.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- A track plot of a near engagement (demo split from seed 316, 12 scenarios, the MAN2 ranges;\\n  scenario 8, 7.7 km mean range) with batch least squares, the frozen MAN2 IMM and the oracle,\\n  marked 5 and 10 minutes after the zig.\\n- A track plot of a far one (scenario 0, 28 km) where the IMM drifts.\\n- Error curves against time for scenario 8.\\n- All three made by `tma2-plot` at the PR\'s commit, with sidecars."},{"id":"/2026/11/08/reporting-slides","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/reporting-slides","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-reporting-slides.md","title":"Slides: how progress is now reported, shown on a demo split","description":"The kickoff promised a reporting practice and a plotting","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"slides","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/slides"},{"inline":true,"label":"process","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/process"}],"readingTime":3.24,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Slides: how progress is now reported, shown on a demo split","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["slides","process"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: reporting plans and progress, two posts per task","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/reporting-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kickoff: usable uncertainty, Gate 4","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/usable-uncertainty-kickoff"}},"content":"import Slides from \\"@site/src/components/Slides\\";\\n\\nThe [kickoff](/blog/2026/11/08/reporting-kickoff) promised a reporting practice and a plotting\\ntool. Both exist. The deck below is the first slides post, made with the templates and the tool it\\ndescribes, on a seeded demo split with the three estimators every result must carry.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<Slides slug=\\"2026-11-08-reporting\\" />\\n\\n## What the figures show\\n\\n**Scenario 8**, a 7.7 km engagement, 56 minutes, \u03c3 = 1\xb0. The ownship (blue) runs a long leg, zigs\\nat 2,140 s, and turns back. The target (red) turns once, early. Batch least squares (green) and the\\nIMM (orange) both place the target 2 to 3 km north of where it is at 5 and 10 minutes after the\\nzig, because the single-leg model has no way to know the target turned. The oracle (purple), which\\nis given the manoeuvre times, sits on the track. That is the gap the segmenters and E are meant\\nto close.\\n\\n**Scenario 0**, a 28 km engagement, 112 minutes. The IMM\'s relative range error grows from 10%\\nto 30% over the hour after the zig while batch least squares settles near 1%. On long, quiet\\nlegs the IMM\'s manoeuvre model is a cost, not a help.\\n\\n**Errors against time** for scenario 8: log-scaled relative range, course and speed error on\\neach estimator\'s plausible rows from the zig onward. Range error for the two single-model\\nestimators sits near 30% throughout; the oracle\'s falls below 1% by the fourth G5 point.\\n\\nPre-zig rows are hidden: the physics layer returns finite states there whose range sd is far\\nlarger than the range, and plotting them misleads.\\n\\n## Reproducing the figures\\n\\nData: generator 0.6.0 (MAN2), seed 316, 12 scenarios, `configs/man2-ranges.json` and\\n`configs/man2-heldout.json`; the train split has 9. Estimators: batch least squares per timestep,\\nthe frozen MAN2 IMM, the oracle.\\n\\n```bash\\nuv run tma2-generate --seed 316 --count 12 --out demo --ranges configs/man2-ranges.json --heldout-ranges configs/man2-heldout.json --format f32pack\\nuv run tma2-solve --in demo/train.f32pack --out demo/lsq.ndjson --per-timestep\\nuv run tma2-track --in demo/train.f32pack --out demo/imm.ndjson --filter imm --sigma-cv 0.03 --sigma-ct 1 --sigma-omega 0.01 --switch 0.0003 --p0-scale 4 --r-scale 2\\nuv run tma2-oracle --truth demo/train.f32pack --out demo/oracle.ndjson\\nP=\\"--predictions demo/lsq.ndjson demo/imm.ndjson demo/oracle.ndjson --labels batch LS,IMM,oracle\\"\\nuv run tma2-plot track  --truth demo/train.f32pack $P --scenario 8 --at 2440,2740 --title \\"Scenario 8: a 7.7 km engagement\\" --out site/static/img/2026-11-08-reporting/track-8.svg\\nuv run tma2-plot errors --truth demo/train.f32pack $P --scenario 8 --out site/static/img/2026-11-08-reporting/errors-8.svg\\nuv run tma2-plot track  --truth demo/train.f32pack $P --scenario 0 --at 3540,5340 --title \\"Scenario 0: a 28 km engagement, where the IMM drifts\\" --out site/static/img/2026-11-08-reporting/track-0.svg\\n```\\n\\nEach SVG has a `.json` sidecar next to it with the truth and prediction digests, the scenario,\\nthe command line and the code commit (fe0f089, clean tree; the demo directory is written as `demo`).\\n\\n## Not achieved\\n\\n- The deck is a static page: the figures do not animate the estimate through time. A per-timestep\\n  animation is a natural next step for `tma2-plot` and would show convergence better than two\\n  marks.\\n- No metric chart from a committed scoring run yet; the first gate slides post (Gate 3) will need\\n  a small script over its `errors.npz`.\\n- Figures are 70 to 85 KB each as SVG text; a long deck will want them optimised.\\n\\n## Next\\n\\n- The constitution amendment is owner-merged; until then the practice is adopted on the agent\\n  side as the documents state.\\n- Spec 008 continues; its kickoff post is written when its spec is next touched, and Gate 3\'s\\n  slides post will be the first for a gate."},{"id":"/2026/11/08/usable-uncertainty-kickoff","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/usable-uncertainty-kickoff","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-usable-uncertainty-kickoff.md","title":"Kickoff: usable uncertainty, Gate 4","description":"Spec 009 is the second attempt at stating how far each estimate can be trusted. Gate 3 showed that","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"kickoff","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/kickoff"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-009","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/spec-009"},{"inline":true,"label":"gate-4","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-4"}],"readingTime":2.58,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kickoff: usable uncertainty, Gate 4","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["kickoff","spec-009","gate-4"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Slides: how progress is now reported, shown on a demo split","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/reporting-slides"},"nextItem":{"title":"Gate S: the classical segmenter carries, learning is not shown","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/05/gate-s"}},"content":"Spec 009 is the second attempt at stating how far each estimate can be trusted. Gate 3 showed that\\nwidening E\'s covariance cannot make it honest: half the rows cannot carry a meaningful course\\ninterval. A third of E\'s courses are badly wrong, and nothing E knows at the time tells which.\\nSo this spec states course with the probability that it holds: \\"within \xb1X\xb0 with probability p,\\notherwise unknown\\", with X and p calibrated, and calibrated intervals for range and speed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Where we are\\n\\n- **Gate 3 stopped at validation** ([write-up](/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3),\\n  [slides](/blog/2026/11/08/gate-3-slides)). A learned scale reached 0.92\u20130.99 marginal coverage\\n  only by stating course intervals that cover the whole circle on half the rows; 0.835 of rows\\n  fell within the \u03c7\xb2\u2084 95% point against 0.92 required; the head was no better than a per-slice\\n  temperature. Even the oracle falls short just after manoeuvres.\\n- **Carried**: Gate 2\'s E (e-s3) and its point estimates, C as the segmenter and control, C\u2033 as a\\n  standing classical control; the time-late check showed the causal E-w leaves nothing usable in\\n  the future bearings.\\n- **Open**: E\'s raw covariance (95% coverage 0.60) must not be shown to an operator as an\\n  uncertainty. Test seed 320 is unused.\\n\\n## Where this task moves to\\n\\n- **SRD**: \xa77 step 5, first item (uncertainty calibration, second attempt); a new \xa75 row, Gate 4,\\n  proposed as an amendment the owner merges. Spec: `specs/009-usable-uncertainty/`.\\n- **Why a probability**: on the validation tuning rows, 35% of E\'s course errors exceed 30\xb0, and\\n  the best causal signal (E\'s encoder state, its stated sds, C\'s code) separates them only with\\n  AUC 0.62. The owner chose (8 November) to state course probabilistically rather than flag\\n  \\"undetermined\\" rows.\\n- **Proposed criteria**: range and speed 95% coverage in [0.92, 0.98] (G4-a); stated p reliable,\\n  overall within \xb10.03 and per bin within \xb10.05 (G4-b); the course statement more informative than\\n  \\"unknown\\", with mean p at least 0.5 (G4-c); learning beats the per-slice temperature, void if the\\n  shuffled control does too (G4-d); points unchanged (G4-e).\\n- **Plan**:\\n  1. Choose the statement\'s form and settings on validation tuning ordinals 0\u2013499 only.\\n  2. Train on exact errors (wrapped course, range ratio, speed): three seeds, the shuffled control\\n     and the temperature.\\n  3. The amendment and the decision record before any training.\\n  4. Validation; seed 320 only if the validation rule is met; the panel decides.\\n- **Risks**: the learned p may add nothing over the temperature (expected from the scoping AUC);\\n  then G4-d fails and the classical statement is carried. We stop at validation if the rule is not\\n  met.\\n\\n## How progress will be shown\\n\\n- Track plots of validation seed 314 scenarios (a settled leg and a manoeuvring target, as in Gate\\n  3\'s slides) with each scored row\'s course arc and stated probability.\\n- A reliability diagram (hit rate against stated p) and coverage charts for range and speed, for E\\n  raw, the temperature and the head, from the validation scoring run\'s stored arrays."},{"id":"/2026/11/05/gate-s","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/05/gate-s","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-05-gate-s.md","title":"Gate S: the classical segmenter carries, learning is not shown","description":"Gate S asked one learning question. Does a learned segmenter, L, find the target\'s steady legs","date":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"gate-s","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-s"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"}],"readingTime":7.06,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Gate S: the classical segmenter carries, learning is not shown","date":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["gate-s","results"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kickoff: usable uncertainty, Gate 4","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/08/usable-uncertainty-kickoff"},"nextItem":{"title":"Generator 0.6.0: the owner\'s manoeuvre model, and its pipeline gate","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/04/generator-man2"}},"content":"Gate S asked one learning question. Does a learned segmenter, L, find the target\'s steady legs\\nbetter than the classical segmenter, C, that it is built on? On the fresh test seed the answer is\\nno measurable difference: L minus C in gap recovered is +0.0012, with a 97.5% interval of\\n[\u22120.0031, +0.0049]. The pre-registered rule counts an inconclusive S4 as not passed. So C carries\\ninto Gate 2, and learning is recorded as not shown.\\n\\nThe classical result is strong. C recovers 0.90 of the range-error gap between a one-leg fit and\\nan oracle that knows the true change times. The GLR + confirm interim segmenter recovers 0.14.\\n\\nThe agent review panel\'s first round rejected the first record: C read later bearings on a few\\npercent of prefixes. The bug was fixed and everything the gate depends on was re-made; the\\nverdicts did not change, and the panel approved the corrected record in its second round.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\n[SRD Annex A \xa7A.9](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/blob/main/docs/SRD-annex-A.md) defines three\\ncomponents on the owner\'s manoeuvre model (generator 0.6.0, MAN2):\\n\\n- **O**, the ownship segmenter, from ownship\'s own course and speed;\\n- **C**, the classical target segmenter: GLR alarms and an additive leg search nominate\\n  transitions, and the coupled physics-layer fit on each nomination\'s own prefix confirms them\\n  (see [steady-leg segmentation](/algorithms/segmentation));\\n- **L**, which is C plus learned per-bearing evidence. Untrained, L is C, byte for byte.\\n\\nL\'s evidence network is a small causal convolution. It was trained on 10,000 scenarios from\\nstream seed 317 by the semi-Markov likelihood of the true segmentation, with three seeds and a\\nshuffled-label control. On validation no seed beat C. The median seed (seed 3) was frozen by\\nSHA-256 before the test split was opened.\\n\\nThe [decision record](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261104-gs-decision)\\nfixed the four criteria, the limits, the carried-seed rule, the shuffled rule, the follow-on and a\\nforecast before any L training.\\n\\n## The result\\n\\nSeed 315: 5,059 scenarios at \u03c3 = 1\xb0, 19,921 G5 rows, 97.5% scenario-clustered paired intervals.\\n\\n| Check | Statistic | Result |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| S1 | O\'s contamination P90 on ownship legs, limit 0.02 | 0.000 [0.000, 0.000]: **pass** |\\n| S2 | C \u2212 GLR + confirm, gap recovered in range | +0.755 [+0.711, +0.798]: **pass** |\\n| S3 | L \u2212 C, contamination P90 (limit +0.01); false splits (limit +0.05) | \u22120.0012 [\u22120.0056, +0.0036]; 0 [0, 0]: **pass** (the false-split part is underpowered; see caveats) |\\n| S4 | L \u2212 C, gap recovered in range | +0.0012 [\u22120.0031, +0.0049]: **inconclusive** |\\n\\nMedian relative range error on all G5 rows, with the share of rows each estimator fails on (no\\nplausible estimate) and its efficiency against the known-manoeuvres Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound:\\n\\n| | Range | Course \xb0 | Speed kt | No estimate | Efficiency | Gap recovered |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| One leg | 0.589 | 68.5 | 6.39 | 24.5% | 15.6 | 0 |\\n| Frozen IMM | 0.577 | 56.0 | 6.07 | 23.7% | 14.4 | \u2014 |\\n| Naive (training means) | 0.397 | 90.8 | 3.70 | 0% | 8.0 | \u2014 |\\n| GLR + confirm | 0.514 | 47.9 | 4.65 | 21.4% | 12.4 | 0.143 |\\n| C | 0.118 | 14.7 | 1.58 | 0.4% | 2.7 | 0.898 [0.889, 0.907] |\\n| L | 0.118 | 14.6 | 1.59 | 0.4% | 2.7 | 0.899 [0.890, 0.908] |\\n| Oracle | 0.065 | 7.4 | 0.96 | 0% | 1.6 | 1 |\\n\\nThe forecast was L \u2212 C of +0.01 [\u22120.01, +0.03], most likely inconclusive. The result is nearer\\nzero and tighter than forecast. Constitution IV allows one re-score of an inconclusive result on\\na larger fresh seed; it is not taken. The decision record fixed before any result that\\ninconclusive counts as not passed, and S4 has no minimum effect size, so a seed ten times larger\\ncould turn +0.001 into a \\"pass\\" that would change nothing in practice.\\n\\n## Reading it\\n\\n- **C\'s lead over GLR + confirm is in its segmentation.** Handing GLR + confirm\'s knots to the\\n  physics layer as C\'s are handed over (gap mode) recovers 0.108 against 0.143 with its own\\n  hand-over; on the rows every estimator answers it helps a little (0.276 against 0.263). Either\\n  way the hand-over explains at most about 0.02 of a margin near 0.6.\\n  What C adds is the second nominator, transition modelling and the coupled confirmation, and\\n  above all it rarely fails: 0.4% of rows have no plausible estimate, against a fifth for GLR +\\n  confirm.\\n- **Read the 0.90 with care.** It is measured from one leg\'s median, and one leg, like the IMM,\\n  fails outright on a quarter of the rows; even the naive baseline has a lower median. On the\\n  14,142 rows every estimator answers, C recovers about 0.84 of the gap (0.838; 0.837 measured\\n  from the naive baseline instead of one leg). On MAN2\'s long manoeuvring windows the references\\n  are weak, and C is still 2.7 times the bound.\\n- **Why L adds nothing.** Two explanations fit, and they are not exclusive.\\n  - *Little headroom.* C leaves 0.10 of the gap. By detectability band, C\'s gap recovered runs\\n    from 0.76 on the weakest changes to 0.96 on the strongest; much of what is left is in changes\\n    that bearings at \u03c3 = 1\xb0 barely reveal.\\n  - *The design and its training limit L.* L\'s loss teaches the additive search\'s lattice, but C\\n    also takes GLR nominations (the share from each was not counted), where the evidence can only\\n    shift a capped threshold. And one validation prefix with a failed fit in the search\'s cost\\n    table dominated the validation loss, so early stopping ended two of the three seeds too soon.\\n    The best-trained seed is no better than C on validation either, and the evidence barely\\n    separates steady from transition rows.\\n\\nWhere L and C differ at all is on rows that end inside an open transition: on the 18,481 rows\\nwhere neither is open their gap recovered is identical (0.9066 against 0.9065). Those open rows\\nare C\'s weakest (0.66 against 0.91 elsewhere), because an open transition is handed over with a\\nknot at the scored row, and they are the main lever for Gate 2.\\n\\nSo the result says that this evidence network, trained this way, added nothing measurable. It does\\nnot say that learning cannot help segmentation.\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive reviewers (geometry, numerics, statistics, reproducibility, red team) reviewed the first\\nrecord. The red team rejected it:\\n\\n- **A causality bug (blocker).** When C closed an open transition it polished the start with\\n  bearings up to about six minutes later, and prefixes before the close reported that start: 5.8%\\n  of C\'s test rows and 4.7% of L\'s were exposed. Fixed; every segmentation the gate depends on was\\n  re-made on CI from a clean tree, validation\'s rules were applied again (same mode, same carried\\n  seed, same shuffled-rule outcome), and the test split was re-scored. The verdicts are unchanged.\\n- **Missing reporting.** The naive baseline, gross-error rates, efficiency and the required strata\\n  were not in the first record; they are now.\\n- **S2\'s margin could have been the hand-over.** It is not (above).\\n- **L\'s training was cut short by an outlier**, and **the search\'s cost table has failed fits**:\\n  both are recorded; the second is fixed before Gate 2.\\n\\nThe full findings and responses are in\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261105-gs-r2/review.md).\\n\\n## Caveats\\n\\n- S3\'s false-split check rests on 67 change-free test prefixes from 49 scenarios; both segmenters\\n  split none, and an exact bound on that count would be about 0.085, above the 0.05 limit.\\n- The frozen trackers were tuned on validation rows that include their scoring rows. The gate\'s\\n  criteria are differences between segmentations and do not depend on them.\\n- An open transition is handed over with a second knot at the scored row; the oracle uses only its\\n  start knot. Gate 2 must fix one convention.\\n- L\'s training jobs recorded `git_dirty` because the workflow put downloaded tables and\\n  checkpoints inside the checkout; the code was clean, and the workflow is fixed.\\n\\n## What follows\\n\\nFixed in advance (FR-072): C is Gate 2\'s segmenter, with the search\'s cost-table fix. Gate 2\\nreports its \\"half the gap to the oracle\\" for C as well, so a Gate 2 gain is credited to the right\\ncomponent. Because C already recovers 0.90 of the one-leg-to-oracle gap, Gate 2\'s decision record\\nmust say which gap it gates before any result exists.\\n\\n**Owner decision (5 November 2026):** a learned nominator, or evidence trained through the\\nconfirmation step, is tested within Gate 2, not as a separate gate.\\n\\nRecord: [`experiments/20261105-gs-r2/`](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261105-gs-r2)\\n(the first record, `experiments/20261105-gs/`, is kept as reviewed)."},{"id":"/2026/11/04/generator-man2","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/04/generator-man2","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-04-generator-man2.md","title":"Generator 0.6.0: the owner\'s manoeuvre model, and its pipeline gate","description":"Steady-leg segmentation (spec 005, Gate S) uses the owner\'s thresholds: a leg is at least 5 min,","date":"2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"generator","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/generator"},{"inline":true,"label":"pipeline-gate","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/pipeline-gate"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"}],"readingTime":3.87,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Generator 0.6.0: the owner\'s manoeuvre model, and its pipeline gate","date":"2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["generator","pipeline-gate","results"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Gate S: the classical segmenter carries, learning is not shown","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/05/gate-s"},"nextItem":{"title":"Gate 1: the physics layer survives training, and the network learns nothing","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/02/gate-1"}},"content":"Steady-leg segmentation (spec 005, Gate S) uses the owner\'s thresholds: a leg is at least 5 min,\\na transition at least 1 min. Generator 0.5.0 did not fit them. Its target legs lasted 4 min at the\\nmedian and its turns took 50 s. The owner chose to change the simulation to the stated manoeuvre\\nmodel rather than the thresholds to the simulation. Generator 0.6.0 is that model. Its pipeline\\ngate, G0-MAN2, passed after two rounds of the agent review panel.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The model\\n\\nCalled MAN2 (SRD Annex B), the model works as follows:\\n\\n- **Target legs** last N(25, 9.1) min with a 5-min floor, so 5% are 10 min or less and 5% are\\n  40 min or more. This is the owner\'s \\"typically 10 to 40 minutes with a bell curve, only turning\\n  at 10 minutes on 5% of occasions\\".\\n- **Leg ends** are a course change (80%), a speed change (10%) or both (10%). They come at slower\\n  rates than 0.5.0\'s, so a transition takes about a minute or more. The median is 116 s.\\n- **Windows** last 40\u2013120 min, up to 720 rows at 10 s, against 0.5.0\'s 360.\\n- **Ownship** steers with helm wander (an autoregressive course error clipped at \xb1 4\xb0) or\\n  autopilot oscillation (1\u20133\xb0) on each steady leg. Its turns are unchanged.\\n- **Target changes** may now overlap the ownship\'s zig. That happens in 13% of engagements.\\n- **0.5.0 compatibility:** all of this sits behind new ranges fields, so 0.5.0 ranges reproduce\\n  0.5.0 byte for byte. Seed 304\'s 10,000 validation scenarios regenerate identically.\\n\\n## The gate\\n\\nThe checks were fixed before the first run (`specs/006-generator-man2/g0-checks.md`). On the\\nrecord of decision (`experiments/20261104-g0-man2-r2/`), all 17 pass:\\n\\n| Check | Result |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| gitma\'s fixtures, regenerated | byte-identical |\\n| two or more target changes per engagement | 93.5% (\u2265 60%) |\\n| change kinds | 0.798 / 0.101 / 0.101 (each within 2 points) |\\n| ownship course off its planned leg | 4.00\xb0 worst (\u2264 5\xb0) |\\n| noise-free zig leg, least squares and physics layer | 9.3e-6 relative range error (\u2264 1e-3) |\\n| straight-leg IMM NIS | 1.018 (0.75\u20131.3) |\\n| least-squares speed efficiency on the zig leg | 1.010 (0.8\u20131.3) |\\n| physics layer equals least squares, 720-row windows | 7.6e-5 (\u2264 1e-4) |\\n| 720-row export, Python / browser / Java | 3.1e-10 / 2.3e-10 / 3.1e-10 (\u2264 1e-5) |\\n| kernel throughput | 26,666 scenarios/s (\u2265 10,000) |\\n\\n### The zig leg\\n\\ngitma\'s noise-free check fits batch least squares up to the target\'s first manoeuvre. On MAN2\\ndata, that manoeuvre can come before the zig, where range is not yet observable. So the check now\\nfits the steady target leg that contains the zig, alone.\\n\\n## What the panel changed\\n\\nFive independent reviewers covered TMA geometry, numerics, statistics, reproducibility and a red\\nteam. In round 1 the red team rejected, and the others approved with findings. Three findings\\nmattered.\\n\\n- **The 720-row export check never used a row past 240.** Its golden batch had shrunk to two short\\n  scenarios. The fix masks bearings before the zig leg and takes only legs ending at row 480 or\\n  later.\\n- **The browser and Java results were bound only to the model\'s digest.** That digest does not\\n  change when the test inputs do, so stale results would have passed. Both legs now record a\\n  digest of the inputs too.\\n- **The pre-registration had dropped two checks the spec required.** These were the tracker NIS\\n  check and the efficiency check. Both were registered before their first run, and both pass.\\n\\nTwo criteria were changed after a result, both by the owner and both recorded:\\n\\n- **Check 4 coverage.** The noise-free check skips 42% of scenarios. The 20% limit set beforehand\\n  was wrong for the leg model: with 25-minute legs, about 40% of zigs fall too close to a target\\n  change. The limit became \\"at least 500 of 1,000 fitted\\". The accuracy limit is unchanged.\\n- **Throughput method.** Throughput is now timed on the path training uses: the numba kernel on\\n  four threads, with nothing else running. The one-thread Python loop gives about 7,600/s and is\\n  reported beside it.\\n\\n## What it leaves open\\n\\n- **The knot path at 720 rows is untested.** The physics layer\'s knot path has not been checked\\n  for equivalence or export conformance at 720 rows, and Gate S needs it.\\n- **The noise-free check is weak against off-by-one errors.** It would catch a one-row bookkeeping\\n  error at the leg\'s ends only through its worst case.\\n- **The IMM and EKF need re-tuning.** They are being re-tuned on the new validation split (seed\\n  314), and the GLR + confirm reference is re-measured on it, before spec 005\'s decision record."},{"id":"/2026/11/02/gate-1","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/02/gate-1","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-02-gate-1.md","title":"Gate 1: the physics layer survives training, and the network learns nothing","description":"Gate 1 passed on constant-velocity data, but not for the reason the project hoped. The learned","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"gate-1","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-1"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"}],"readingTime":4.46,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Gate 1: the physics layer survives training, and the network learns nothing","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["gate-1","results"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Generator 0.6.0: the owner\'s manoeuvre model, and its pipeline gate","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/04/generator-man2"},"nextItem":{"title":"Gate D: the steady-leg detector stops at validation","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/02/gate-d"}},"content":"Gate 1 passed on constant-velocity data, but not for the reason the project hoped. The learned\\nheads collapsed to constants during training, so the carried model *is* batch least squares\\ncomputed by the physics layer. What Gate 1 shows is narrower and still worth having: end-to-end\\ntraining through the declared physics layer is stable and does not degrade least squares at the\\ninformation limit. Whether the learned component can add anything is untested until Gate 2.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The gate\\n\\nOn a fresh test seed (303, 10,000 scenarios, generated once), \u03c3 = 1\xb0 rows at zig + 5 min (range,\\ncourse) and zig + 10 min (speed), the carried model had to be within 1.1 \xd7 batch least squares on\\nevery median with the whole interval below, have a gross-error rate at or below least squares\',\\nand have efficiency (median error \xf7 Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound \xf7 0.6745) at most 1.25. All of it was fixed in\\na decision record committed before training (`experiments/20261101-g1-decision/`).\\n\\n| Check | Result | 95% interval | Limit |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Range, ratio to least squares | 0.999 | 0.996\u20131.002 | < 1.1 |\\n| Course, ratio | 0.997 | 0.994\u20131.000 | < 1.1 |\\n| Speed, ratio | 1.000 | 1.000\u20131.000 (identity) | < 1.1 |\\n| Gross errors, model \u2212 least squares | \u22120.08 points (58 vs 62 of 4,884 rows) | \u22120.16 to \u22120.02 | \u2264 0 |\\n| Efficiency, range / course / speed | 0.99 / 0.96 / 0.96 | upper bounds 1.03 / 1.00 / 1.00 | \u2264 1.25 |\\n\\nBeside it, on the same rows: least squares 3.33% median range error (90th percentile 14.5%; the\\nmodel 14.4%), the tuned IMM 1.03 \xd7 least squares at the median and 1.17 \xd7 at the 90th\\npercentile, the EKF 1.04 \xd7 and 1.20 \xd7, naive about 12 \xd7. The \u03c3 = 2\xb0 rows, not gated, tell the same\\nstory (90th percentile 29.2% against 29.6%). On constant-velocity data least squares is at\\nthe bound and the trackers\' process noise costs them a little, as expected.\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive independent reviewers (geometry, numerics, statistics, reproducibility, red team) approved\\nwith findings, and every one of the substantive findings points the same way:\\n\\n- **The heads are dead.** In the carried model every head ends with 0 of 64 hidden units active;\\n  the per-bearing weights are one constant, the start correction and covariance scale are their\\n  biases. On validation the trained model\'s estimate equals the same model with its heads zeroed\\n  (that is, untrained) to a median 3.5e-11 relative. Its test numbers\\n  equal, to every digit, those of the physics layer with trivial inputs (the \\"oracle\\" row, which on\\n  constant-velocity data has nothing to know). An untrained model would have passed.\\n- **The gross-error edge is a convergence flag.** The 38 rows with a range error over 50% are the\\n  same for least squares, the model and the untrained layer. The whole margin is 4 rows where least\\n  squares\' solver reported non-convergence and the physics layer did not (24 against 20; exact sign\\n  test p = 0.06): a difference in how the two define convergence, not in accuracy, and nothing to\\n  do with learning. The gross result reads \\"no worse than least squares\\", not \\"better\\". The decision record\'s forecast\\n  said the learned start would avoid wrong minima; that was wrong.\\n- **The validation stop that was not.** The pre-registered shuffled-label control had to score\\n  \\"at naive level\\". Run through the physics layer it scores near least squares whatever it learns,\\n  so the registered rule would have stopped the phase at validation. The owner amended the rule\\n  (constitution 1.2.0) after training started and before the validation report; the amendment and\\n  this consequence are recorded.\\n\\nNone of this changes the verdict under the registered rules, and all of it changes what the\\nverdict means.\\n\\n## Why the heads died\\n\\nOn constant-velocity data with Gaussian noise, uniform weights and the least squares start are\\noptimal, so the loss gives the heads nothing to do; the untrained model\'s validation loss (3.27 per\\nrow, against 3.0 at the bound) is already at the floor, and it stayed there for every seed. The\\nheads\' last layers start at zero by design, so the model starts as least squares; with nothing to\\ngain, the hidden ReLU layers drifted dead and the encoder\'s output collapsed. That is a legitimate\\noptimum here and a problem for Gate 2: a dead ReLU never revives.\\n\\n## What changes before Gate 2\\n\\n- No warm start from these weights; head activations that cannot die; a test of alive units and of\\n  how much head outputs vary across rows.\\n- A learnability check before any Gate 2 plan, on data where the heads must matter (outlier or\\n  heteroscedastic bearings, or the manoeuvring data), so a dead network cannot pass silently again.\\n- Weights bound by digest in predictions and gate records; gross errors reported by cause; one\\n  median convention for efficiency; a minimum improvement in early stopping.\\n\\n## Caveats\\n\\n- Zig + 5 min falls inside the second ownship turn for about two thirds of the rows; these numbers\\n  are not comparable with gitma\'s single-zig results.\\n- Efficiency is median-based; least squares itself scores below 1 on course and speed, which\\n  reflects heavy tails rather than beating the bound, so the 1.25 limit separates little this close\\n  to it.\\n- The direct-regression control was under-trained (validation loss 50.5 \u2192 50.0); its 7\u201312 \xd7 is\\n  not a fair measure of direct regression.\\n\\nRecords: models `experiments/20261101-g1-a-models/`, validation report\\n`experiments/20261101-g1-a-val/`, test record, report and panel review `experiments/20261101-g1-a-g1/`."},{"id":"/2026/11/02/gate-d","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/02/gate-d","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-02-gate-d.md","title":"Gate D: the steady-leg detector stops at validation","description":"The learned steady-leg detector was meant to tell the physics layer where the target\'s legs begin","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"gate-d","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/gate-d"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"}],"readingTime":3.86,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Gate D: the steady-leg detector stops at validation","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["gate-d","results"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Gate 1: the physics layer survives training, and the network learns nothing","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/02/gate-1"},"nextItem":{"title":"P0: the pipeline reproduces gitma, after three panel rounds","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/01/gate-p0"}},"content":"The learned steady-leg detector was meant to tell the physics layer where the target\'s legs begin\\nand end. It did not earn a test. On validation, its manoeuvre probability never reached the\\npre-registered threshold. Its downstream estimate beat batch least squares only slightly and lost\\nto the tuned IMM. So the pre-registered rule stopped the phase before the test split was\\ngenerated.\\n\\nThe agent review panel confirmed the stop after two rounds. It found no pipeline error and\\ncorrected our first explanation of why.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\nSRD Annex A asks for a detector with three outputs, which the [physics layer](/algorithms/physics-layer)\\nconsumes as its knots and speed prior:\\n\\n- whether a manoeuvre is happening now;\\n- where the changepoints are;\\n- whether each change is in course or speed.\\n\\nThe [detector](/algorithms/detector) shares the Gate 1 encoder. It was trained on its labels\\nand on the downstream error of the solve it feeds, through the physics layer.\\n\\nBefore training, the [decision record](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261102-gd-decision)\\nfixed:\\n\\n- the threshold, 0.5;\\n- the tolerance, \xb160 s;\\n- the controls (CUSUM, a windowed GLR, the IMM\'s mode probability);\\n- the checks;\\n- a validation rule: a variant goes to test only if every gated check\'s point already meets its\\n  limit on validation.\\n\\nThe plan trained seven detectors on free runners: three seeds each of a causal and a time-late\\nvariant, and a classification-only control.\\n\\n## What happened\\n\\nOn validation seed 304, \u03c3 = 1\xb0 G5 rows:\\n\\n| Estimator | Median range error, all G5 rows | Settled rows |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| batch least squares | 12.1% | 6.8% |\\n| IMM (frozen) | 9.5% | 7.5% |\\n| causal detector \u2192 physics layer | 11.5% | 6.6% |\\n| time-late detector \u2192 physics layer | 26.8% | 16.0% |\\n| oracle (true manoeuvre times) | 5.2% | 4.0% |\\n\\nThe causal detector improved on least squares by 5% and recovered 9% of the gap to the oracle. It\\nwas 1.21\xd7 the IMM, with interval [1.15, 1.27], so D-downstream-all failed. It raised no alarm at\\nall: 0 of 8,943 manoeuvres detected and 0 false alarms. So D-delay failed too. On settled rows it\\nwas slightly better than least squares, which passed.\\n\\nThe controls were tuned to the detector\'s own 0% false-alarm rate. That silenced them as well, so\\nthe delay and miss comparisons set silent against silent.\\n\\nThe time-late variant reads the whole window. With almost no knots it fits one leg across the\\nlater manoeuvres, so it does much worse than least squares.\\n\\n## Why: a learning failure, not proof that manoeuvres are invisible\\n\\nOur first write-up said the detector \\"learned the base rate\\". The panel showed that this was\\nhalf right.\\n\\n- **Its \\"now\\" probability ranks rows by time alone.** It is near zero before the zig, where no\\n  manoeuvre can start, and flat afterwards. After the zig its AUC is about 0.5. It does not\\n  respond to how strongly a manoeuvre bends the bearings, at \u03c3 = 1\xb0 or 2\xb0.\\n- **The time-late variant does no better.** It can see the bearings after each turn, and it is\\n  still at chance.\\n- **The manoeuvres are identifiable in hindsight.** The oracle halves least squares\' error, and\\n  the IMM\'s own mode probability detects about half the manoeuvres at a 5% false-alarm rate.\\n\\nSo the encoder did not learn to tell target turns from ownship turns. Three design choices made\\nthat worse:\\n\\n- **The downstream loss swamped the classification losses.** It took the shared encoder\'s\\n  gradient about 1000:1.\\n- **The gate scored hard knots.** Training optimised soft changepoint weights, but the gate scored\\n  only knots exported at w \u2265 0.5, which threw away about half the learned gain.\\n- **The labels made 0.5 unreachable.** A 60-second \\"now\\" label, held legs weighted \xd73, and \xb160 s\\n  triangular targets meant the threshold could only be met with near-certain timing.\\n\\n## What follows\\n\\nAnnex A \xa7A.6 fixed the follow-on in advance. Gate D failed on more than false alarms, so the\\nresult is published here, and Gate 2 proceeds with the CUSUM/GLR segmenter.\\n\\nThe next spec declares that segmenter\'s operating point. At a 5% held-leg false-alarm rate the\\nGLR detected 57% of manoeuvres on validation, against 10% for the least-squares CUSUM.\\n\\nWe recommend the GLR at 5%, and a lesson for any future detector gate: a floor on the matched\\nfalse-alarm rate, so that a silent detector cannot make the comparisons degenerate. Gate 2\'s\\nend-to-end model may still learn segmentation through the estimation loss. This result says it\\nwill not come for free.\\n\\nRecord: [validation report](https://github.com/debrief/gitma2/tree/main/experiments/20261102-gd-a-val)\\n(`README.md`, `review.md`); models: `experiments/20261102-gd-a-models/`."},{"id":"/2026/11/01/gate-p0","metadata":{"permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/01/gate-p0","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-gate-p0.md","title":"P0: the pipeline reproduces gitma, after three panel rounds","description":"P0 was the pipeline gate (SRD \xa75, spec 002): before any training, the Python port had to","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"p0","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/p-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"pipeline-gate","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/pipeline-gate"},{"inline":true,"label":"results","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/tags/results"}],"readingTime":2.91,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"P0: the pipeline reproduces gitma, after three panel rounds","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["p0","pipeline-gate","results"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Gate D: the steady-leg detector stops at validation","permalink":"/gitma2/pr/37/blog/2026/11/02/gate-d"}},"content":"P0 was the pipeline gate (SRD \xa75, spec 002): before any training, the Python port had to\\nreproduce gitma\'s generator byte for byte, its batch least squares, EKF and IMM to float32, and\\nthe new physics layer with trivial inputs had to equal batch least squares and recover noise-free\\ntruth. It passed on the fourth record, decided by the agent review panel in its third round\\n(`experiments/20261101-p0-r4/`, commit 19c40c7).\\n\\nThis post is written on 8 November 2026, a week late. The constitution has required a critical\\npost per gate from the start; P0\'s was missed, and the new reporting practice\\n([process page](/process/reporting)) exists partly so that does not happen again.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What passed\\n\\nTwenty checks, all green on r4:\\n\\n- **Generator**: three gitma fixtures (`iter1-seed1`, `iter4-seed777`, `phase4-seed777`),\\n  8 files each, byte-identical.\\n- **References**: batch least squares, EKF and IMM on two fixtures, convergence flags identical,\\n  worst relative difference 1.6e-5 (least squares on `phase4-seed777`), typically 1e-7.\\n- **Physics layer**: equals batch least squares on 22,531 rows; recovers noise-free truth to a\\n  relative range error of 2.4e-6 on 160 of 160 scenarios.\\n- **Least-squares efficiency** on held-course rows: speed 0.949, range 1.007, course 0.988,\\n  inside the [0.8, 1.2] band.\\n- **Oracle ceiling** on a 10\u2075-scenario MAN validation split: median ratios 0.531 (speed prior,\\n  all rows) and 0.500 (settled), within the intervals measured in research note 05.\\n- **IMM at scale**: 1.2 million rows against gitma\'s output, no flag mismatches.\\n- **ONNX export**: Python, browser WASM and Java legs agree to 1.1e-11 in double.\\n\\n## What the panel found, and why it took four records\\n\\nThe first record passed its own machine checks 18 of 18. The panel rejected it anyway, on two\\nblockers:\\n\\n1. **NaN gradients on masked rows.** `sqrt(valid\xb7exp(\u2113)\xb7mask)` has an infinite derivative at\\n   zero, so masked and beyond-k rows produced NaN log-weight gradients. One backward pass would\\n   have poisoned every parameter of any model trained through the layer. The checks had never\\n   taken a gradient. The fix is `safe_sqrt`, and the tests now assert finite gradients everywhere.\\n2. **A conformance result that could not fail.** The toy model\'s ONNX digest does not depend on\\n   its golden inputs, so a stale result could pass. The legs now also record a digest over the\\n   inputs.\\n\\nRecord r2 then failed one of twenty checks after the fixes, r3 passed but recorded a dirty tree\\nin its manifest because provenance was read after the outputs were written, and r4 is the record\\nof decision. None of this was a numerical problem with the port. All of it was the verification\\nbeing weaker than it looked.\\n\\n## Reading it critically\\n\\n- P0 shows the pipeline reproduces gitma. It says nothing about whether learning will help; that\\n  question starts at Gate 1 (and, as it turned out, was not really answered until Gate 2).\\n- The float32 reporting path of the physics layer disagrees with the double path by up to 1e-2\\n  relative, because the damped solve\'s accept/reject decisions flip between runtimes. Double is\\n  the conformance artefact; float32 is reported only.\\n- The oracle ceiling check compares against an interval measured in a research note on a\\n  different implementation. Agreement is reassuring, not a proof that both are right.\\n\\n## Records\\n\\nRound 0 `experiments/20261031-p0/` (5adb8bf, superseded), r2 `experiments/20261101-p0-r2/`\\n(8e5dfe1), r3 `experiments/20261101-p0-r3/` (dd69fc8), r4 `experiments/20261101-p0-r4/` (19c40c7,\\nthe decision; `review.md` holds the three panel rounds)."}]}}')}}]);